10 | 1M HCl |  |  |', 'This table lists various common solutions along with their pH paper color, approximate pH values, and the nature of the substances (acidic, basic, or neutral). It includes biological fluids like saliva before and after meals, various juices, drinks, tap water, and strong acid and base solutions.', '["pH values", "solutions", "saliva", "lemon juice", "NaOH", "HCl", "acidic", "basic"]'::jsonb, NULL, 30, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'c87bf2374401e5fba375d74e9bba7873') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (42, 55, 6, 'concept', 'What is the pH of the soil in your backyard? Plants require a specific pH range for their healthy growth. To find out the pH required for the healthy growth of a plant, you can collect the soil from various places and check the pH in the manner described below.', 'Plants need soil within a certain pH range to grow healthily. Measuring the pH of soil samples collected from different locations helps determine the suitable pH range for plant growth in that area.', '["soil pH", "plant growth", "pH measurement", "soil samples"]'::jsonb, NULL, 31, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'b9d2f73a7292d36614c7f2e2fa8c2853') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (43, 57, NULL, 'concept', 'The atmosphere of Venus is made up of thick white and yellowish clouds of sulphuric acid. Do you think life can exist on this planet?', 'Venus''s atmosphere contains dense clouds composed of sulphuric acid, which creates an extremely harsh environment. The presence of such acidic clouds raises questions about the possibility of life existing on Venus due to the corrosive and toxic nature of sulphuric acid.', '["Venus", "atmosphere", "sulphuric acid", "acid clouds", "life existence"]'::jsonb, NULL, 32, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', NULL, NULL, '8b9d18c55034d1299814fb8b3d6a2aaf') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (44, 59, NULL, 'concept', 'It is very interesting to note that our stomach produces hydrochloric acid. It helps in the digestion of food without harming the stomach. During indigestion the stomach produces too much acid and this causes pain and irritation. To get rid of this pain, people use bases called antacids. One such remedy must have been suggested by you at the beginning of this Chapter. These antacids neutralise the excess acid. Magnesium hydroxide (Milk of magnesia), a mild base, is often used for this purpose.', 'The stomach produces hydrochloric acid which aids digestion without damaging the stomach lining. Excess acid production causes indigestion, leading to pain and irritation. Antacids, which are bases such as magnesium hydroxide (Milk of magnesia), neutralize this excess acid to relieve discomfort.', '["hydrochloric acid", "stomach", "digestion", "indigestion", "antacids", "magnesium hydroxide", "neutralisation"]'::jsonb, NULL, 34, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', NULL, NULL, '81a8f14f06b753a93dc2a75f83deb0c5') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (48, 64, NULL, 'concept', 'Nature provides neutralisation options. Nettle is a herbaceous plant which grows in the wild. Its leaves have stinging hair, which cause painful stings when touched accidentally. This is due to the methanoic acid secreted by them. A traditional remedy is rubbing the area with the leaf of the dock plant, which often grows beside the nettle in the wild. Can you guess the nature of the dock plant? So next time you know what to look out for if you accidentally touch a nettle plant while trekking. Are you aware of any other effective traditional remedies for such stings?', 'Nettle plants cause painful stings due to methanoic acid secreted by their stinging hairs. A traditional remedy involves rubbing the affected area with dock plant leaves, which grow nearby and presumably neutralize the acid. This illustrates natural neutralization methods for acid stings.', '["nettle", "methanoic acid", "dock plant", "neutralisation", "traditional remedy", "acid sting"]'::jsonb, NULL, 39, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', NULL, NULL, '64e9eef44de34ea77044f743a7ac3add') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (49, 65, NULL, 'table', 'Table 2.3 Some naturally occurring acids
Natural source Acid Natural source Acid
Vinegar Acetic acid Sour milk (Curd) Lactic acid
Orange Citric acid Lemon Citric acid
Tamarind Tartaric acid Ant sting Methanoic acid
Tomato Oxalic acid Nettle sting Methanoic acid', 'This table lists various naturally occurring acids along with their natural sources. Examples include acetic acid from vinegar, lactic acid from sour milk (curd), citric acid from orange and lemon, tartaric acid from tamarind, methanoic acid from ant and nettle stings, and oxalic acid from tomato.', '["naturally occurring acids", "acetic acid", "lactic acid", "citric acid", "tartaric acid", "methanoic acid", "oxalic acid", "natural sources"]'::jsonb, NULL, 40, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'af352a3f99299b43ba25b4a2100b8e51') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (50, 66, NULL, 'question', '1. You have two solutions, A and B. The pH of solution A is 6 and pH of solution B is 8. Which solution has more hydrogen ion concentration? Which of this is acidic and which one is basic?
2. What effect does the concentration of H+(aq) ions have on the nature of the solution?
3. Do basic solutions also have H+(aq) ions? If yes, then why are these basic?
4. Under what soil condition do you think a farmer would treat the soil of his fields with quick lime (calcium oxide) or slaked lime (calcium hydroxide) or chalk (calcium carbonate)?', 'These questions explore the relationship between pH values and hydrogen ion concentration, the nature of acidic and basic solutions, the presence of H+(aq) ions in basic solutions, and the use of lime or chalk to treat soil conditions. They test understanding of acidity, basicity, and soil treatment chemistry.', '["pH", "hydrogen ion concentration", "acidic solution", "basic solution", "H+(aq) ions", "quick lime", "slaked lime", "chalk", "soil treatment"]'::jsonb, NULL, 41, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', '["pH scale", "acid-base definitions"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'b27454a9edeb9c19c664532fb75f2fad') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (51, 67, NULL, 'concept', 'Identify the acids and bases from which the above salts may be obtained. Salts having the same positive or negative radicals are said to belong to a family. For example, NaCl and Na2SO4 belong to the family of sodium salts. Similarly, NaCl and KCl belong to the family of chloride salts. How many families can you identify among the salts given in this Activity?', 'Salts can be traced back to their parent acids and bases. Salts sharing the same positive or negative radicals form families, such as sodium salts or chloride salts. This concept helps classify salts based on their ionic components.', '["acids", "bases", "salts", "radicals", "salt families", "sodium salts", "chloride salts"]'::jsonb, NULL, 42, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', '["acid-base reactions", "salt formation"]'::jsonb, NULL, '5fe785dcc05c982adcb9d3b78be18826') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (52, 67, 7, 'concept', 'By now you have learnt that the salt formed by the combination of hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide solution is called sodium chloride. This is the salt that you use in food. You must have observed in the above Activity that it is a neutral salt. Seawater contains many salts dissolved in it. Sodium chloride is separated from these salts. Deposits of solid salt are also found in several parts of the world. These large crystals are often brown due to impurities. This is called rock salt. Beds of rock salt were formed when seas of bygone ages dried up. Rock salt is mined like coal. You must have heard about Mahatma Gandhi’s Dandi March. Did you know that sodium chloride was such an important symbol in our struggle for freedom?', 'Sodium chloride is formed by the reaction of hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide and is a neutral salt commonly used in food. It is found dissolved in seawater and as solid deposits called rock salt, which often appears brown due to impurities. Rock salt deposits were formed from ancient dried-up seas and are mined similarly to coal. Sodium chloride also held symbolic importance during India''s freedom struggle, notably in the Dandi March.', '["sodium chloride", "hydrochloric acid", "sodium hydroxide", "neutral salt", "seawater", "rock salt", "Dandi March"]'::jsonb, NULL, 44, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', NULL, NULL, '2fd1a9f7e5ad632c4d10945467c69627') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (53, 67, 7, 'concept', 'The common salt thus obtained is an important raw material for various materials of daily use, such as sodium hydroxide, baking soda, washing soda, bleaching powder and many more. Let us see how one substance is used for making all these different substances.', 'Common salt (sodium chloride) serves as a key raw material for producing several important chemical substances used daily, including sodium hydroxide, baking soda, washing soda, and bleaching powder. This section introduces the diverse applications derived from a single compound.', '["common salt", "raw material", "sodium hydroxide", "baking soda", "washing soda", "bleaching powder"]'::jsonb, NULL, 45, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', '["sodium chloride"]'::jsonb, NULL, '5b5865c8930a3119e70bf4fed2ab9f2a') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (54, 67, 7, 'concept', 'Sodium hydroxide
When electricity is passed through an aqueous solution of sodium chloride (called brine), it decomposes to form sodium hydroxide. The process is called the chlor-alkali process because of the products formed – chlor for chlorine and alkali for sodium hydroxide.
2NaCl(aq) + 2H2O(l) ® 2NaOH(aq) + Cl2(g) + H2(g)
Chlorine gas is given off at the anode, and hydrogen gas at the cathode. Sodium hydroxide solution is formed near the cathode. The three products produced in this process are all useful. Figure 2.8 shows the different uses of these products.', 'Passing electricity through aqueous sodium chloride (brine) decomposes it into sodium hydroxide, chlorine gas, and hydrogen gas in a process called the chlor-alkali process. Chlorine is released at the anode, hydrogen at the cathode, and sodium hydroxide forms near the cathode. All three products have significant industrial uses.', '["sodium hydroxide", "chlor-alkali process", "electrolysis", "sodium chloride", "chlorine gas", "hydrogen gas", "brine"]'::jsonb, NULL, 46, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', '["sodium chloride", "electrolysis"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'a9ed310b7e2abfe442a63d3e2c707dce') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (55, 67, 7, 'concept', 'Bleaching powder
You have already come to know that chlorine is produced during the electrolysis of aqueous sodium chloride (brine). This chlorine gas is used for the manufacture of bleaching powder. Bleaching powder is produced by the action of chlorine on dry slaked lime [Ca(OH)2]. Bleaching powder is represented as Ca(ClO)2, though the actual composition is quite complex.

2Ca(OH)2 + 2Cl2 ® Ca(ClO)2 + CaCl2 + 2H2O

Bleaching powder is used –
(i) for bleaching cotton and linen in the textile industry, for bleaching wood pulp in paper factories and for bleaching washed clothes in laundry;
(ii) as an oxidising agent in many chemical industries; and
(iii) to make drinking water free from germs.', 'Chlorine gas produced from the electrolysis of brine is used to manufacture bleaching powder by reacting with dry slaked lime (Ca(OH)2). The chemical reaction produces bleaching powder (Ca(ClO)2), calcium chloride, and water. Bleaching powder is widely used for bleaching textiles, wood pulp, and clothes, as an oxidizing agent in industries, and for disinfecting drinking water.', '["bleaching powder", "chlorine gas", "electrolysis", "slaked lime", "Ca(OH)2", "Ca(ClO)2", "oxidising agent"]'::jsonb, NULL, 47, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', '["chlor-alkali process", "chlorine gas"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'bec3a303e239a04146f181d1012e5132') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (56, 67, 7, 'concept', 'Baking soda
The baking soda is commonly used in the kitchen for making tasty crispy pakoras, etc. Sometimes it is added for faster cooking. The chemical name of the compound is sodium hydrogencarbonate (NaHCO3). It is produced using sodium chloride as one of the raw materials.
NaCl + H2O + CO2 + NH3 → NH4Cl + NaHCO3
(Ammonium chloride) (Sodium hydrogencarbonate)
Did you check the pH of sodium hydrogencarbonate in Activity 2.14? Can you correlate why it can be used to neutralise an acid? It is a mild non-corrosive basic salt. The following reaction takes place when it is heated during cooking –
2NaHCO3 Heat →  Na2CO3 + H2O + CO2
(Sodium hydrogencarbonate) (Sodium carbonate)
Sodium hydrogencarbonate has got various uses in the household.
Uses of Baking soda
(i) Baking powder is a mixture of baking soda (sodium hydrogen carbonate) and tartaric acid which is a weak edible acid. When baking powder is heated or mixed in water, the following reaction takes place –
NaHCO3 + H+ ® CO2 + H2O + Sodium salt of acid
(From any acid)
Carbon dioxide produced during the reaction can cause bread or cake to rise making them soft and spongy.
(ii) Sodium hydrogencarbonate is also an ingredient in antacids. Being alkaline, it neutralises excess acid in the stomach and provides relief.
(iii) It is also used in soda-acid fire extinguishers.', 'Baking soda, or sodium hydrogencarbonate (NaHCO3), is produced using sodium chloride and is commonly used in cooking to make foods crispy and to speed cooking. It is a mild basic salt that neutralizes acids. When heated, it decomposes into sodium carbonate, water, and carbon dioxide. Baking soda is used in baking powder to release CO2 for making bread rise, as an antacid to relieve stomach acidity, and in soda-acid fire extinguishers.', '["baking soda", "sodium hydrogencarbonate", "NaHCO3", "neutralisation", "baking powder", "antacid", "fire extinguisher"]'::jsonb, NULL, 48, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', '["sodium chloride"]'::jsonb, NULL, '6e2edadb305e8666de4b26525df4cea0') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (57, 69, NULL, 'concept', 'Heat a few crystals of copper sulphate in a dry boiling tube. What is the colour of the copper sulphate after heating? Do you notice water droplets in the boiling tube? Where have these come from? Add 2-3 drops of water on the sample of copper sulphate obtained after heating. What do you observe? Is the blue colour of copper sulphate restored? Removing water of crystallisation: Copper sulphate crystals which seem to be dry contain water of crystallisation. When we heat the crystals, this water is removed and the salt turns white. If you moisten the crystals again with water, you will find that blue colour of the crystals reappears. Water of crystallisation is the fixed number of water molecules present in one formula unit of a salt. Five water molecules are present in one formula unit of copper sulphate. Chemical formula for hydrated copper sulphate is CuSO4.5H2O. Now you would be able to answer the question whether the molecule of Na2CO3.10H2O is wet.', 'Copper sulphate crystals contain water of crystallisation, which is a fixed number of water molecules in one formula unit of a salt. Heating copper sulphate removes this water, turning the salt white, but adding water restores its blue colour. The chemical formula for hydrated copper sulphate is CuSO4.5H2O, indicating five water molecules per formula unit.', '["copper sulphate", "water of crystallisation", "hydrated salt", "CuSO4.5H2O", "heating", "blue colour"]'::jsonb, NULL, 49, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', NULL, NULL, '4eafd24b4265a984099e54405988aeeb') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (58, 70, NULL, 'concept', 'One other salt, which possesses water of crystallisation is gypsum. It has two water molecules as water of crystallisation. It has the chemical formula CaSO4.2H2O. It has the chemical formula CaSO4.2H2O. Let us look into the use of this salt. Plaster of Paris: On heating gypsum at 373 K, it loses water molecules and becomes calcium sulphate hemihydrate (CaSO4.1/2H2O). This is called Plaster of Paris, the substance which doctors use as plaster for supporting fractured bones in the right position. Plaster of Paris is a white powder and on mixing with water, it changes to gypsum once again giving a hard solid mass. CaSO4.1/2H2O + 1 1/2 H2O → CaSO4.2H2O (Plaster of Paris → Gypsum). Note that only half a water molecule is shown to be attached as water of crystallisation. It is written in this form because two formula units of CaSO4 share one molecule of water. Plaster of Paris is used for making toys, materials for decoration and for making surfaces smooth. Try to find out why calcium sulphate hemihydrate is called ‘Plaster of Paris’?', 'Gypsum contains two water molecules of crystallisation with the formula CaSO4.2H2O. Heating gypsum at 373 K removes water to form calcium sulphate hemihydrate (Plaster of Paris), CaSO4.1/2H2O. Plaster of Paris is a white powder that hardens back to gypsum when mixed with water. The half water molecule notation arises because two formula units share one water molecule. It is used medically and for making toys and decorations.', '["gypsum", "water of crystallisation", "CaSO4.2H2O", "Plaster of Paris", "calcium sulphate hemihydrate", "heating", "hardening"]'::jsonb, NULL, 50, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', '["water of crystallisation"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'd70020ecea42ce958d88db19e671f036') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (59, 71, NULL, 'equation_block', 'CaSO4.1/2H2O + 1 1/2 H2O → CaSO4.2H2O (Plaster of Paris → Gypsum)', 'Plaster of Paris (calcium sulphate hemihydrate) reacts with water to form gypsum by reabsorbing water molecules, converting back to calcium sulphate dihydrate.', '["Plaster of Paris", "gypsum", "calcium sulphate hemihydrate", "hydration reaction", "CaSO4.1/2H2O", "CaSO4.2H2O"]'::jsonb, NULL, 51, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', '["water of crystallisation", "gypsum", "Plaster of Paris"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'e5b866fca7e9b0d103ca8d2c1b2943bd') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (60, 72, NULL, 'concept', 'Plaster of Paris is used for making toys, materials for decoration and for making surfaces smooth. It is also used by doctors as plaster for supporting fractured bones in the right position.', 'Plaster of Paris is a versatile material used in medical applications to support fractured bones, as well as in making toys, decorative materials, and smoothing surfaces.', '["Plaster of Paris", "uses", "medical application", "toys", "decoration", "surface smoothing"]'::jsonb, NULL, 52, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', '["Plaster of Paris"]'::jsonb, NULL, '357f12483c2d242339ae53d954776253') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (61, 73, NULL, 'question', '1. What is the common name of the compound Ca(ClO)2?
2. Name the substance which on treatment with chlorine yields bleaching powder.
3. Name the sodium compound which is used for softening hard water.
4. What will happen if a solution of sodium hydrocarbonate is heated? Give the equation of the reaction involved.
5. Write an equation to show the reaction between Plaster of Paris and water.', 'Questions focus on identifying common names and substances related to calcium hypochlorite, bleaching powder, sodium compounds for water softening, thermal decomposition of sodium hydrocarbonate, and the reaction of Plaster of Paris with water.', '["Ca(ClO)2", "bleaching powder", "sodium compound", "hard water", "sodium hydrocarbonate", "Plaster of Paris", "chemical reactions"]'::jsonb, NULL, 53, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', '["Plaster of Paris", "chemical reactions of salts", "water of crystallisation"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'fb4e5058c51ce2013275f6e9d0208e75') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (62, 74, NULL, 'concept', 'Acid-base indicators are dyes or mixtures of dyes which are used to indicate the presence of acids and bases. Acidic nature of a substance is due to the formation of H+(aq) ions in solution. Formation of OH–(aq) ions in solution is responsible for the basic nature of a substance. When an acid reacts with a metal, hydrogen gas is evolved and a corresponding salt is formed. When a base reacts with a metal, along with the evolution of hydrogen gas a salt is formed which has a negative ion composed of the metal and oxygen. When an acid reacts with a metal carbonate or metal hydrogencarbonate, it gives the corresponding salt, carbon dioxide gas and water. Acidic and basic solutions in water conduct electricity because they produce hydrogen and hydroxide ions respectively.', 'Acid-base indicators detect acids and bases by color changes. Acids produce H+(aq) ions, bases produce OH–(aq) ions. Acids react with metals to release hydrogen gas and form salts. Bases also react with metals producing hydrogen gas and salts containing metal-oxygen ions. Acids react with metal carbonates or hydrogencarbonates to form salts, carbon dioxide, and water. Acidic and basic solutions conduct electricity due to ion production.', '["acid-base indicators", "H+(aq) ions", "OH\u2013(aq) ions", "acid-metal reaction", "base-metal reaction", "metal carbonate", "electrolytes"]'::jsonb, NULL, 54, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'd9a1cf6ee03731a796c856ef3097c22b') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (63, 75, NULL, 'concept', 'The strength of an acid or an alkali can be tested by using a scale called the pH scale (0-14) which gives the measure of hydrogen ion concentration in a solution. A neutral solution has a pH of exactly 7, while an acidic solution has a pH less than 7 and a basic solution a pH more than 7. Living beings carry out their metabolic activities within an optimal pH range. Mixing concentrated acids or bases with water is a highly exothermic process. Acids and bases neutralise each other to form corresponding salts and water.', 'The pH scale measures hydrogen ion concentration from 0 to 14, with 7 being neutral. Solutions with pH less than 7 are acidic, and those with pH greater than 7 are basic. Metabolic activities occur within an optimal pH range. Mixing concentrated acids or bases with water releases heat. Acids and bases neutralise each other producing salts and water.', '["pH scale", "acid strength", "alkali strength", "neutral solution", "exothermic mixing", "neutralisation", "metabolic pH range"]'::jsonb, NULL, 55, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', '["acid-base indicators", "acid and base properties"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'a7bd61303fb5a90bcee43b262d2ed79d') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (64, 76, NULL, 'question', 'Given solution of HCl. If we take 20 mL of the same solution of NaOH, the amount of HCl solution (the same solution as before) required to neutralise it will be (a) 4 mL (b) 8 mL (c) 12 mL (d) 16 mL
4. Which one of the following types of medicines is used for treating indigestion? (a) Antibiotic (b) Analgesic (c) Antacid (d) Antiseptic
5. Write word equations and then balanced equations for the reaction taking place when –
(a) dilute sulphuric acid reacts with zinc granules.
(b) dilute hydrochloric acid reacts with magnesium ribbon.
(c) dilute sulphuric acid reacts with aluminium powder.
(d) dilute hydrochloric acid reacts with iron filings.
6. Compounds such as alcohols and glucose also contain hydrogen but are not categorised as acids. Describe an activity to prove it.
7. Why does distilled water not conduct electricity, whereas rain water does?
8. Why does dry HCl gas not show acidic behaviour in the absence of water?
9. Five solutions A, B, C, D and E when tested with universal indicator showed pH as 4, 1, 11, 7 and 9, respectively. Which solution is
(a) neutral?
(b) strongly alkaline?
(c) strongly acidic?
(d) weakly acidic?
(e) weakly alkaline?
Arrange the pH in increasing order of hydrogen-ion concentration.
10. Equal lengths of magnesium ribbons are taken in test tubes A and B. Hydrochloric acid (HCl) is added to test tube A, while acetic acid (CH3COOH) is added to test tube B. Amount and concentration taken for both the acids are same. In which test tube will the fizzing occur more vigorously and why?
11. Fresh milk has a pH of 6. How do you think the pH will change as it turns into curd? Explain your answer.
12. A milkman adds a very small amount of baking soda to fresh milk.
(a) Why does he shift the pH of the fresh milk from 6 to slightly alkaline?
(b) Why does this milk take a long time to set as curd?
13. Plaster of Paris should be stored in a moisture-proof container. Explain why?
14. What is a neutralisation reaction? Give two examples.
15. Give two important uses of washing soda and baking soda.', 'This section contains multiple choice and short answer questions related to acids, bases, salts, and their chemical reactions. It covers topics such as neutralisation, pH values, acidic behaviour of gases, and practical applications of acids and bases in everyday life. The questions also include writing chemical equations and understanding the properties of substances like milk and plaster of Paris.', '["HCl", "NaOH", "neutralisation", "acidic behaviour", "pH", "universal indicator", "baking soda", "plaster of Paris"]'::jsonb, NULL, 56, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'df6d0bc1b29faf43538a08d11ae44388') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (65, 79, NULL, 'concept', 'Metals and Non-metals
In Class IX you have learnt about various elements. You have seen that elements can be classified as metals or non-metals on the basis of their properties.

Think of some uses of metals and non-metals in your daily life.

What properties did you think of while categorising elements as metals or non-metals?

How are these properties related to the uses of these elements?
Let us look at some of these properties in detail.', 'Elements are classified as metals or non-metals based on their properties. The classification relates to their different uses in daily life. Understanding the properties of metals and non-metals helps explain their practical applications.', '["metals", "non-metals", "elements", "properties", "classification", "uses"]'::jsonb, NULL, 0, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'b8c86028d775e9d8e764b068b1060958') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (66, 83, NULL, 'concept', 'List the metals whose wires you have seen in daily life. The ability of metals to be drawn into thin wires is called ductility. Gold is the most ductile metal. You will be surprised to know that a wire of about 2 km length can be drawn from one gram of gold. It is because of their malleability and ductility that metals can be given different shapes according to our needs. Can you name some metals that are used for making cooking vessels? Do you know why these metals are used for making vessels? Let us do the following Activity to find out the answer.', 'Ductility is the property of metals that allows them to be drawn into thin wires. Gold is the most ductile metal, capable of being drawn into a wire about 2 km long from just one gram. Metals can be shaped into various forms due to their malleability and ductility. Some metals are commonly used for making cooking vessels because of these properties.', '["ductility", "malleability", "gold", "metal wires", "cooking vessels"]'::jsonb, NULL, 4, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '966b356e2202b4ce1095e3618f33c7aa') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (67, 85, NULL, 'concept', 'You must have seen that the wires that carry current in your homes have a coating of polyvinylchloride (PVC) or a rubber-like material. Why are electric wires coated with such substances? What happens when metals strike a hard surface? Do they produce a sound? The metals that produce a sound on striking a hard surface are said to be sonorous. Can you now say why school bells are made of metals?', 'Electric wires are coated with insulating materials like PVC or rubber to prevent the flow of electricity outside the wire, ensuring safety. Metals produce a sound when struck against a hard surface, a property called sonority. This explains why metals are used to make school bells, as their sonorous nature produces clear ringing sounds.', '["electric wires", "polyvinylchloride", "PVC", "rubber", "insulation", "sonorous", "metal sound", "school bells"]'::jsonb, NULL, 6, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '5967f9281455eb6d48962ea3c99a1692') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (68, 85, 8, 'concept', 'In the previous Class you have learnt that there are very few non-metals as compared to metals. Some of the examples of non-metals are carbon, sulphur, iodine, oxygen, hydrogen, etc. The non-metals are either solids or gases except bromine which is a liquid.', 'Non-metals are fewer in number compared to metals. Examples of non-metals include carbon, sulphur, iodine, oxygen, and hydrogen. Most non-metals exist as solids or gases, with bromine being the only non-metal that is liquid at room temperature.', '["non-metals", "carbon", "sulphur", "iodine", "oxygen", "hydrogen", "bromine", "physical states"]'::jsonb, NULL, 7, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'cb5b903f439ea02208f5bbeb5df4d090') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (69, 86, NULL, 'concept', 'Collect samples of carbon (coal or graphite), sulphur and iodine. Carry out the Activities 3.1 to 3.4 and 3.6 with these non-metals and record your observations. Compile your observations regarding metals and non-metals in Table 3.1.

Table 3.1
Element Symbol Type of surface Hardness Malleability Ductility Conducts Electricity Sonority

On the basis of the observations recorded in Table 3.1, discuss the general physical properties of metals and non-metals in the class. You must have concluded that we cannot group elements according to their physical properties alone, as there are many exceptions. For example –
(i) All metals except mercury exist as solids at room temperature. In Activity 3.5, you have observed that metals have high melting points but gallium and caesium have very low melting points. These two metals will melt if you keep them on your palm.
(ii) Iodine is a non-metal but it is lustrous.
(iii) Carbon is a non-metal that can exist in different forms. Each form is called an allotrope. Diamond, an allotrope of carbon, is the hardest natural substance known and has a very high melting and boiling point. Graphite, another allotrope of carbon, is a conductor of electricity.
(iv) Alkali metals (lithium, sodium, potassium) are so soft that they can be cut with a knife. They have low densities and low melting points.
Elements can be more clearly classified as metals and non-metals on the basis of their chemical properties.', 'Metals and non-metals exhibit distinct physical properties, but there are exceptions that prevent classification based solely on these properties. Most metals are solid at room temperature except mercury, though gallium and caesium have low melting points. Iodine, a non-metal, is lustrous, and carbon exists in allotropes such as diamond and graphite with differing properties. Alkali metals are soft with low densities and melting points. Chemical properties provide a clearer basis for classifying elements as metals or non-metals.', '["carbon", "sulphur", "iodine", "metals", "non-metals", "allotrope", "melting point", "physical properties"]'::jsonb, NULL, 9, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '86967ef23544869270a3ebf8738738d7') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (70, 88, NULL, 'question', '1. Give an example of a metal which
(i) is a liquid at room temperature.
(ii) can be easily cut with a knife.
(iii) is the best conductor of heat.
(iv) is a poor conductor of heat.
2. Explain the meanings of malleable and ductile.', 'Questions ask for examples of metals with specific properties such as being liquid at room temperature, ease of cutting, and thermal conductivity. Additionally, the meanings of the terms malleable and ductile are requested.', '["metal", "liquid at room temperature", "cut with knife", "conductor of heat", "poor conductor", "malleable", "ductile"]'::jsonb, NULL, 11, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'e397081cafb72131102023c20d0f51d9') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (71, 89, NULL, 'concept', 'We will learn about the chemical properties of metals in the following Sections 3.2.1 to 3.2.4. For this, collect the samples of following metals – aluminium, copper, iron, lead, magnesium, zinc and sodium. Most non-metals produce acidic oxides when dissolved in water. On the other hand, most metals give rise to basic oxides. You will be learning more about these metal oxides in the next section.', 'This section introduces the chemical properties of metals, focusing on their oxides. It lists metals such as aluminium, copper, iron, lead, magnesium, zinc, and sodium for study. It contrasts the acidic oxides produced by most non-metals with the basic oxides formed by most metals.', '["chemical properties", "metals", "non-metals", "acidic oxides", "basic oxides", "metal oxides", "aluminium", "magnesium"]'::jsonb, NULL, 12, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'f8142e6307f93ff9bc74fb3440933c91') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (72, 91, NULL, 'concept', 'Almost all metals combine with oxygen to form metal oxides.
Metal + Oxygen → Metal oxide
For example, when copper is heated in air, it combines with oxygen to form copper(II) oxide, a black oxide.
2Cu + O2 → 2CuO
(Copper) (Copper(II) oxide)
Similarly, aluminium forms aluminium oxide.
4Al + 3O2 → 2Al2O3
(Aluminium) (Aluminium oxide)
Recall from Chapter 2, how copper oxide reacts with hydrochloric acid.
We have learnt that metal oxides are basic in nature. But some metal oxides, such as aluminium oxide, zinc oxide show both acidic as well as basic behaviour. Such metal oxides which react with both acids as well as bases to produce salts and water are known as amphoteric oxides.
Aluminium oxide reacts in the following manner with acids and bases –
Al2O3 + 6HCl → 2AlCl3 + 3H2O
Al2O3 + 2NaOH → 2NaAlO2 + H2O
(Sodium aluminate)
Most metal oxides are insoluble in water but some of these dissolve in water to form alkalis. Sodium oxide and potassium oxide dissolve in water to produce alkalis as follows –
Na2O(s) + H2O(l) → 2NaOH(aq)
K2O(s) + H2O(l) → 2KOH(aq)', 'Most metals react with oxygen to form metal oxides. Copper forms black copper(II) oxide, and aluminium forms aluminium oxide. Metal oxides are generally basic, but some like aluminium oxide and zinc oxide are amphoteric, reacting with both acids and bases to form salts and water. While most metal oxides are insoluble in water, oxides of sodium and potassium dissolve to form alkalis.', '["metal oxides", "copper(II) oxide", "aluminium oxide", "amphoteric oxides", "sodium oxide", "potassium oxide", "alkalis"]'::jsonb, NULL, 14, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '9c05e50e2584f0a3ca3169263c9cc215') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (73, 92, NULL, 'concept', 'We have observed in Activity 3.9 that all metals do not react with oxygen at the same rate. Different metals show different reactivities towards oxygen. Metals such as potassium and sodium react so vigorously that they catch fire if kept in the open. Hence, to protect them and to prevent accidental fires, they are kept immersed in kerosene oil.
At ordinary temperature, the surfaces of metals such as magnesium, aluminium, zinc, lead, etc., are covered with a thin layer of oxide. The protective oxide layer prevents the metal from further oxidation. Iron does not burn on heating but iron filings burn vigorously when sprinkled in the flame of the burner. Copper does not burn, but the hot metal is coated with a black coloured layer of copper(II) oxide. Silver and gold do not react with oxygen even at high temperatures.', 'Metals differ in their reactivity with oxygen; potassium and sodium react so vigorously that they ignite in air and are stored under kerosene. Some metals like magnesium, aluminium, zinc, and lead develop a thin oxide layer that protects them from further oxidation. Iron filings burn in flame, but bulk iron does not, while copper forms a black oxide layer on heating. Silver and gold are unreactive with oxygen even at high temperatures.', '["reactivity", "oxygen", "potassium", "sodium", "oxide layer", "iron filings", "copper(II) oxide", "silver", "gold"]'::jsonb, NULL, 15, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["metal oxides", "reaction with oxygen"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'e0f6f82d494b3b32629d31693e65dd92') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (74, 93, NULL, 'concept', 'Anodising is a process of forming a thick oxide layer of aluminium. Aluminium develops a thin oxide layer when exposed to air. This aluminium oxide coat makes it resistant to further corrosion. The resistance can be improved further by making the oxide layer thicker. During anodising, a clean aluminium article is made the anode and is electrolysed with dilute sulphuric acid. The oxygen gas evolved at the anode reacts with aluminium to make a thicker protective oxide layer. This oxide layer can be dyed easily to give aluminium articles an attractive finish.', 'Aluminium naturally forms a thin oxide layer that protects it from corrosion. The anodising process thickens this oxide layer by electrolysing aluminium as an anode in dilute sulphuric acid, where oxygen evolved reacts with aluminium to form a thicker protective coating. This thicker oxide layer enhances corrosion resistance and can be dyed for decorative purposes.', '["anodising", "aluminium oxide", "corrosion resistance", "electrolysis", "sulphuric acid", "protective oxide layer"]'::jsonb, NULL, 16, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["aluminium oxide", "oxide layer"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'b77c4a1828b88e09770c4bb6d6cdf4d9') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (75, 96, NULL, 'concept', 'Metals react with water and produce a metal oxide and hydrogen gas. Metal oxides that are soluble in water dissolve in it to further form metal hydroxide. But all metals do not react with water.
Metal + Water → Metal oxide + Hydrogen
Metal oxide + Water → Metal hydroxide
Metals like potassium and sodium react violently with cold water. In case of sodium and potassium, the reaction is so violent and exothermic that the evolved hydrogen immediately catches fire.
2K(s) + 2H2O(l) → 2KOH(aq) + H2(g) + heat energy
2Na(s) + 2H2O(l) → 2NaOH(aq) + H2(g) + heat energy
The reaction of calcium with water is less violent. The heat evolved is not sufficient for the hydrogen to catch fire.
Ca(s) + 2H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(aq) + H2(g)
Calcium starts floating because the bubbles of hydrogen gas formed stick to the surface of the metal.
Magnesium does not react with cold water. It reacts with hot water to form magnesium hydroxide and hydrogen. It also starts floating due to the bubbles of hydrogen gas sticking to its surface.
Metals like aluminium, iron and zinc do not react either with cold or hot water. But they react with steam to form the metal oxide and hydrogen.
2Al(s) + 3H2O(g) → Al2O3(s) + 3H2(g)
3Fe(s) + 4H2O(g) → Fe3O4(s) + 4H2(g)
Metals such as lead, copper, silver and gold do not react with water at all.', 'Metals react with water to produce metal oxides and hydrogen gas, with some metal oxides dissolving further to form metal hydroxides. Highly reactive metals like potassium and sodium react violently with cold water, producing hydrogen that catches fire. Calcium reacts less violently and floats due to hydrogen bubbles. Magnesium reacts only with hot water, while aluminium, iron, and zinc react with steam but not with cold or hot water. Lead, copper, silver, and gold do not react with water.', '["metal oxide", "hydrogen gas", "metal hydroxide", "potassium", "sodium", "calcium", "magnesium", "aluminium", "iron", "zinc", "steam", "reactivity"]'::jsonb, NULL, 19, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'fdff66ff5345736ac02bf839f9236541') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (76, 96, 9, 'concept', 'You have already learnt that metals react with acids to give a salt and hydrogen gas.

Metal + Dilute acid → Salt + Hydrogen

But do all metals react in the same manner? Let us find out.', 'Metals react with dilute acids to produce a salt and hydrogen gas. This reaction is a general property of metals, but the manner in which different metals react with acids can vary. The text introduces the concept of metal reactivity with acids and poses a question about the uniformity of this behavior among all metals.', '["metals", "acids", "salt", "hydrogen gas", "metal reactivity", "dilute acid"]'::jsonb, NULL, 20, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'e84c545b9bc39c090f48a800d21f5016') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (77, 98, NULL, 'concept', 'Hydrogen gas is not evolved when a metal reacts with nitric acid. It is because HNO3 is a strong oxidising agent. It oxidises the H2 produced to water and itself gets reduced to any of the nitrogen oxides (N2O, NO, NO2). But magnesium (Mg) and manganese (Mn) react with very dilute HNO3 to evolve H2 gas.', 'Nitric acid does not typically produce hydrogen gas when reacting with metals because it acts as a strong oxidising agent, converting the hydrogen gas into water while reducing itself to nitrogen oxides. However, magnesium and manganese can react with very dilute nitric acid to release hydrogen gas.', '["nitric acid", "hydrogen gas", "oxidising agent", "nitrogen oxides", "magnesium", "manganese", "dilute HNO3"]'::jsonb, NULL, 22, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["Reactivity of metals with acids"]'::jsonb, NULL, '300b6c50ac5197bc5c7916ab257b9d83') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (78, 99, NULL, 'concept', 'You must have observed in Activity 3.11, that the rate of formation of bubbles was the fastest in the case of magnesium. The reaction was also the most exothermic in this case. The reactivity decreases in the order Mg > Al > Zn > Fe. In the case of copper, no bubbles were seen and the temperature also remained unchanged. This shows that copper does not react with dilute HCl.', 'The reactivity of metals with dilute hydrochloric acid decreases in the order magnesium, aluminium, zinc, and iron. Magnesium reacts the fastest and releases the most heat. Copper does not react with dilute hydrochloric acid, as indicated by the absence of bubbles and temperature change.', '["reactivity order", "magnesium", "aluminium", "zinc", "iron", "copper", "dilute hydrochloric acid", "exothermic reaction"]'::jsonb, NULL, 23, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["Reactivity of Metals with Dilute Hydrochloric Acid"]'::jsonb, NULL, '225bcb66e89537a7a666ec327dd5d9a0') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (79, 100, NULL, 'concept', 'Do You Know? Aqua regia, (Latin for ‘royal water’) is a freshly prepared mixture of concentrated hydrochloric acid and concentrated nitric acid in the ratio of 3:1. It can dissolve gold, even though neither of these acids can do so alone. Aqua regia is a highly corrosive, fuming liquid. It is one of the few reagents that is able to dissolve gold and platinum.', 'Aqua regia is a corrosive mixture of concentrated hydrochloric acid and nitric acid in a 3:1 ratio. It can dissolve noble metals like gold and platinum, which neither acid can dissolve individually. It is a fuming liquid known for its strong corrosive properties.', '["aqua regia", "concentrated hydrochloric acid", "concentrated nitric acid", "gold", "platinum", "corrosive", "fuming liquid"]'::jsonb, NULL, 24, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["Properties of acids"]'::jsonb, NULL, '1a604cf764bf354318bf213a09748adf') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (80, 100, 10, 'concept', 'The reactivity series is a list of metals arranged in the order of their decreasing activities. After performing displacement experiments (Activities 1.9 and 3.12), the following series, (Table 3.2) known as the reactivity or activity series has been developed.

Table 3.2 Activity series : Relative reactivities of metals
K Potassium Most reactive
Na Sodium
Ca Calcium
Mg Magnesium
Al Aluminium
Zn Zinc Reactivity decreases
Fe Iron
Pb Lead
[H] [Hydrogen]
Cu Copper
Hg Mercury
Ag Silver
Au Gold Least reactive', 'The reactivity series arranges metals in order of decreasing chemical activity based on displacement experiments. Potassium is the most reactive metal in the series, followed by sodium, calcium, magnesium, aluminium, zinc, iron, lead, hydrogen, copper, mercury, silver, and gold as the least reactive. This series helps predict the outcome of reactions involving metals and their salts.', '["reactivity series", "metals", "displacement experiments", "potassium", "sodium", "calcium", "hydrogen", "gold"]'::jsonb, NULL, 26, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '59e3b6ed389fd062b4124afd2440befb') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (81, 100, 10, 'question', '1. Why is sodium kept immersed in kerosene oil?
2. Write equations for the reactions of
(i) iron with steam
(ii) calcium and potassium with water
3. Samples of four metals A, B, C and D were taken and added to the following solution one by one. The results obtained have been tabulated as follows.', 'The questions test understanding of metal reactivity, specifically why sodium is stored in kerosene oil to prevent unwanted reactions. They also require writing chemical equations for iron reacting with steam and calcium and potassium reacting with water. Additionally, a comparative analysis of reactions of four metal samples with a solution is introduced.', '["sodium", "kerosene oil", "iron", "steam", "calcium", "potassium", "chemical equations", "metal reactivity"]'::jsonb, NULL, 27, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["reactivity series"]'::jsonb, NULL, '6872f4f1d8d2b19b99893b6d8ebbd68f') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (82, 102, NULL, 'concept', 'In the above activities, you saw the reactions of metals with a number of reagents. Why do metals react in this manner? Let us recall what we learnt about the electronic configuration of elements in Class IX. We learnt that noble gases, which have a completely filled valence shell, show little chemical activity. We, therefore, explain the reactivity of elements as a tendency to attain a completely filled valence shell.', 'The reactivity of metals is explained by their tendency to attain a completely filled valence shell. Noble gases have completely filled valence shells and show little chemical activity. Metals react by losing or gaining electrons to achieve stable electronic configurations similar to noble gases.', '["reactivity", "metals", "electronic configuration", "noble gases", "valence shell"]'::jsonb, NULL, 28, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '7b9bff807d45023f7784c91034a7649e') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (83, 103, NULL, 'table', 'Let us have a look at the electronic configuration of noble gases and some metals and non-metals. We can see from Table 3.3 that a sodium atom has one electron in its outermost shell. If it loses the electron from its M shell then its L shell now becomes the outermost shell and that has a stable octet. The nucleus of this atom still has 11 protons but the number of electrons has become 10, so there is a net positive charge giving us a sodium cation Na+. On the other hand chlorine has seven electrons in its outermost shell and it requires one more electron to complete its octet. If sodium and chlorine were to react, the electron lost by sodium could be taken up by chlorine. After gaining an electron, the chlorine atom gets a unit negative charge, because its nucleus has 17 protons and there are 18 electrons in its K, L and M shells. This gives us a chloride anion Cl–. So both these elements can have a give-and-take relation between them as follows (Fig. 3.5).

Na 2,8,1 → Na+ 2,8 + e– (Sodium cation)
Cl 2,8,7 + e– → Cl– 2,8,8 (Chloride anion)

Sodium and chloride ions, being oppositely charged, attract each other and are held by strong electrostatic forces of attraction to exist as sodium chloride (NaCl). It should be noted that sodium chloride does not exist as molecules but aggregates of oppositely charged ions.

Table 3.3 Electronic configurations of some elements

Type of Element | Element | Atomic Number | Number of electrons in shells
K | L | M | N
Noble gases
Helium (He) | 2 | 2
Neon (Ne) | 10 | 2 | 8
Argon (Ar) | 18 | 2 | 8 | 8
Metals
Sodium (Na) | 11 | 2 | 8 | 1
Magnesium (Mg) | 12 | 2 | 8 | 2
Aluminium (Al) | 13 | 2 | 8 | 3
Potassium (K) | 19 | 2 | 8 | 8 | 1
Calcium (Ca) | 20 | 2 | 8 | 8 | 2
Non-metals
Nitrogen (N) | 7 | 2 | 5
Oxygen (O) | 8 | 2 | 6
Fluorine (F) | 9 | 2 | 7
Phosphorus (P) | 15 | 2 | 8 | 5
Sulphur (S) | 16 | 2 | 8 | 6
Chlorine (Cl) | 17 | 2 | 8 | 7', 'The electronic configurations of noble gases, metals, and non-metals show the distribution of electrons in their shells. Sodium has one electron in its outermost shell and loses it to form a sodium cation (Na+), achieving a stable octet. Chlorine has seven electrons in its outermost shell and gains one electron to form a chloride anion (Cl–). Sodium chloride forms due to electrostatic attraction between these ions. The table lists the atomic numbers and electron shell distributions of various elements.', '["electronic configuration", "sodium", "chlorine", "valence shell", "cation", "anion", "noble gases", "ionic bond"]'::jsonb, NULL, 29, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["electronic configuration", "valence shell", "octet rule"]'::jsonb, NULL, '43d77d908dd16d8577754c6418dda7c6') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (84, 104, NULL, 'concept', 'Na 2,8,1 → Na+ 2,8 + e– (Sodium cation)
Cl 2,8,7 + e– → Cl– 2,8,8 (Chloride anion)

Sodium and chloride ions, being oppositely charged, attract each other and are held by strong electrostatic forces of attraction to exist as sodium chloride (NaCl). It should be noted that sodium chloride does not exist as molecules but aggregates of oppositely charged ions.', 'Sodium loses one electron to form a sodium cation (Na+), and chlorine gains one electron to form a chloride anion (Cl–). These oppositely charged ions attract each other by strong electrostatic forces to form sodium chloride (NaCl), which exists as a lattice of ions rather than discrete molecules.', '["sodium chloride", "electron transfer", "sodium cation", "chloride anion", "electrostatic attraction", "ionic compound"]'::jsonb, NULL, 30, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["electronic configuration", "cation", "anion"]'::jsonb, NULL, '816fdd4afa3019661739ebea0d68dd23') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (85, 105, NULL, 'concept', 'Mg 2,8,2 → Mg2+ 2,8 + 2e– (Magnesium cation)
Cl 2,8,7 + e– → Cl– 2,8,8 (Chloride anion)

The compounds formed in this manner by the transfer of electrons from a metal to a non-metal are known as ionic compounds or electrovalent compounds. Can you name the cation and anion present in MgCl2?', 'Magnesium loses two electrons to form a magnesium cation (Mg2+), and chlorine gains electrons to form chloride anions (Cl–). The transfer of electrons from metal to non-metal forms ionic or electrovalent compounds such as magnesium chloride (MgCl2).', '["magnesium chloride", "electron transfer", "magnesium cation", "chloride anion", "ionic compounds", "electrovalent compounds"]'::jsonb, NULL, 31, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["electron transfer", "cation", "anion", "ionic compounds"]'::jsonb, NULL, '9acfe20bf1def3105b91d39047a6acde') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (86, 106, NULL, 'question', 'Use the Table above to answer the following questions about metals A, B, C and D.
(i) Which is the most reactive metal?
(ii) What would you observe if B is added to a solution of Copper(II) sulphate?
(iii) Arrange the metals A, B, C and D in the order of decreasing reactivity.
4. Which gas is produced when dilute hydrochloric acid is added to a reactive metal? Write the chemical reaction when iron reacts with dilute H2SO4.
5. What would you observe when zinc is added to a solution of iron(II) sulphate? Write the chemical reaction that takes place.

Metal | Iron(II) sulphate | Copper(II) sulphate | Zinc sulphate | Silver nitrate
A | No reaction | Displacement
B | Displacement | No reaction
C | No reaction | No reaction | No reaction | Displacement
D | No reaction | No reaction | No reaction | No reaction', 'The questions test understanding of metal reactivity and displacement reactions. Metal B displaces iron from iron(II) sulphate but not copper from copper(II) sulphate, indicating its relative reactivity. The gas produced when dilute hydrochloric acid reacts with reactive metals is hydrogen. Zinc displaces iron from iron(II) sulphate solution, demonstrating a displacement reaction.', '["metal reactivity", "displacement reaction", "hydrogen gas", "iron(II) sulphate", "copper(II) sulphate", "zinc", "silver nitrate"]'::jsonb, NULL, 32, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["metal reactivity", "displacement reactions", "chemical reactions with acids"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'afd16f81c0439f34a3dfd9f3c2d21644') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (87, 108, NULL, 'table', 'Table 3.4  Melting and boiling points of some ionic compounds
Ionic compound  Melting point (K)  Boiling point (K)
NaCl  1074  1686
LiCl  887  1600
CaCl2  1045  1900
CaO  2850  3120
MgCl2  981  1685', 'This table lists the melting and boiling points of several ionic compounds including sodium chloride, lithium chloride, calcium chloride, calcium oxide, and magnesium chloride. The melting points range from 887 K to 2850 K, and boiling points range from 1600 K to 3120 K, indicating the generally high thermal stability of ionic compounds.', '["ionic compounds", "melting point", "boiling point", "NaCl", "LiCl", "CaCl2", "CaO", "MgCl2"]'::jsonb, NULL, 35, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '1523b8d0b4a997eb017e863dd75971a1') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (88, 109, NULL, 'concept', 'You may have observed the following general properties for ionic compounds—
(i) Physical nature: Ionic compounds are solids and are somewhat hard because of the strong force of attraction between the positive and negative ions. These compounds are generally brittle and break into pieces when pressure is applied.
(ii) Melting and Boiling points: Ionic compounds have high melting and boiling points (see Table 3.4). This is because a considerable amount of energy is required to break the strong inter-ionic attraction.
(iii) Solubility: Electrovalent compounds are generally soluble in water and insoluble in solvents such as kerosene, petrol, etc.
(iv) Conduction of Electricity: The conduction of electricity through a solution involves the movement of charged particles. A solution of an ionic compound in water contains ions, which move to the opposite electrodes when electricity is passed through the solution. Ionic compounds in the solid state do not conduct electricity because movement of ions in the solid is not possible due to their rigid structure. But ionic compounds conduct electricity in the molten state. This is possible in the molten state since the electrostatic forces of attraction between the oppositely charged ions are overcome due to the heat. Thus, the ions move freely and conduct electricity.', 'Ionic compounds are solid, hard, and brittle due to strong electrostatic forces between ions. They have high melting and boiling points because breaking ionic bonds requires significant energy. These compounds are generally soluble in water but insoluble in organic solvents like kerosene and petrol. Ionic compounds conduct electricity when molten or dissolved in water because ions are free to move, but they do not conduct electricity in solid form due to the fixed positions of ions.', '["ionic compounds", "physical nature", "melting point", "boiling point", "solubility", "electric conductivity", "electrostatic forces"]'::jsonb, NULL, 36, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '71b5ef436578c9562a36864444240bb5') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (89, 110, NULL, 'question', '1. (i) Write the electron-dot structures for sodium, oxygen and magnesium.
(ii) Show the formation of Na2O and MgO by the transfer of electrons.
(iii) What are the ions present in these compounds?
2. Why do ionic compounds have high melting points?', 'Questions ask for the electron-dot structures of sodium, oxygen, and magnesium, the electron transfer process forming Na2O and MgO, identification of ions in these compounds, and the reason for high melting points of ionic compounds.', '["electron-dot structures", "Na2O", "MgO", "ions", "melting points", "ionic compounds"]'::jsonb, NULL, 37, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["electron-dot structures", "ionic bond formation"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'fd8fae7ab09d5c4c406a369925219e55') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (90, 111, NULL, 'concept', 'The earth’s crust is the major source of metals. Seawater also contains some soluble salts such as sodium chloride, magnesium chloride, etc. The elements or compounds, which occur naturally in the earth’s crust, are known as minerals. At some places, minerals contain a very high percentage of a particular metal and the metal can be profitably extracted from it. These minerals are called ores.', 'The earth''s crust is the primary source of metals, while seawater contains soluble salts like sodium chloride and magnesium chloride. Naturally occurring elements or compounds in the earth''s crust are called minerals. When minerals have a high concentration of a particular metal that can be economically extracted, they are termed ores.', '["earth\u2019s crust", "metals", "seawater", "soluble salts", "sodium chloride", "magnesium chloride", "minerals", "ores"]'::jsonb, NULL, 38, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '9efc20b2b74f54e107ae07c98d804d86') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (91, 111, 12, 'concept', 'You have learnt about the reactivity series of metals. Having this knowledge, you can easily understand how a metal is extracted from its ore. Some metals are found in the earth’s crust in the free state. Some are found in the form of their compounds. The metals at the bottom of the activity series are the least reactive. They are often found in a free state. For example, gold, silver, platinum and copper are found in the free state. Copper and silver are also found in the combined state as their sulphide or oxide ores. The metals at the top of the activity series (K, Na, Ca, Mg and Al) are so reactive that they are never found in nature as free elements. The metals in the middle of the activity series (Zn, Fe, Pb, etc.) are moderately reactive. They are found in the earth’s crust mainly as oxides, sulphides or carbonates. You will find that the ores of many metals are oxides. This is because oxygen is a very reactive element and is very abundant on the earth.', 'Metals vary in their reactivity and occurrence in nature. Least reactive metals like gold, silver, platinum, and copper are found in the free state. Highly reactive metals such as potassium, sodium, calcium, magnesium, and aluminium are never found free in nature but only in compound forms. Moderately reactive metals like zinc, iron, and lead occur mainly as oxides, sulphides, or carbonates. Oxygen''s high reactivity and abundance make metal oxides common ores.', '["reactivity series", "free state", "metal ores", "oxides", "sulphides", "carbonates", "reactive metals", "earth''s crust"]'::jsonb, NULL, 39, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["reactivity series of metals"]'::jsonb, NULL, '2e4b091919f0662ce1167f32dca8f89d') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (92, 111, 12, 'concept', 'Thus on the basis of reactivity, we can group the metals into the following three categories (Fig. 3.9) – (i) Metals of low reactivity; (ii) Metals of medium reactivity; (iii) Metals of high reactivity. Different techniques are to be used for obtaining the metals falling in each category.

K Na Ca Mg Al Zn Fe Pb Cu Ag Au

Reduction using carbon
Found in native state
Electrolysis

Figure 3.9

Activity series and related metallurgy', 'Metals are classified into three categories based on their reactivity: low, medium, and high reactivity metals. Each category requires different extraction techniques. Low reactivity metals are often found in the native state and can be extracted by simple methods. Medium reactivity metals can be extracted by reduction using carbon. Highly reactive metals require electrolysis for extraction. The activity series illustrates these classifications and corresponding metallurgical methods.', '["metal classification", "low reactivity metals", "medium reactivity metals", "high reactivity metals", "extraction techniques", "reduction using carbon", "electrolysis", "activity series"]'::jsonb, NULL, 40, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["reactivity series of metals", "metal occurrence in nature"]'::jsonb, NULL, '0b1d1a4dad2a1bd29d575450591e5838') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (93, 111, 12, 'concept', 'Figure 3.10

Steps involved in the extraction of metals from ores', 'The extraction of metals from their ores involves a series of steps. These steps vary depending on the metal''s reactivity and the nature of its ore. The process typically includes concentration of the ore, extraction of the metal, and purification. Figure 3.10 illustrates these steps.', '["metal extraction", "ore concentration", "metal purification", "extraction steps"]'::jsonb, NULL, 41, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["metal occurrence in nature", "metal classification based on reactivity"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'b59632654f567d37b32b047e45dada7f') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (94, 111, 13, 'concept', 'Ores mined from the earth are usually contaminated with large amounts of impurities such as soil, sand, etc., called gangue. The impurities must be removed from the ore prior to the extraction of the metal. The processes used for removing the gangue from the ore are based on the differences between the physical or chemical properties of the gangue and the ore. Different separation techniques are accordingly employed. Several steps are involved in the extraction of pure metal from ores. A summary of these steps is given in Fig.3.10. Each step is explained in detail in the following sections.', 'Ores contain impurities known as gangue, which include soil and sand. These impurities must be removed before metal extraction. The removal processes exploit differences in physical or chemical properties between gangue and ore. Metal extraction involves several steps, summarized in a figure and detailed in subsequent sections.', '["ores", "gangue", "impurities", "metal extraction", "separation techniques", "physical properties", "chemical properties"]'::jsonb, NULL, 42, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '67e934f4e258b2b3c7913ce43d4c3e09') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (95, 111, 14, 'concept', 'Metals low in the activity series are very unreactive. The oxides of these metals can be reduced to metals by heating alone. For example, cinnabar (HgS) is an ore of mercury. When it is heated in air, it is first converted into mercuric oxide (HgO). Mercuric oxide is then reduced to mercury on further heating.
2HgS(s) + 3O2(g) Heat →    2HgO(s) + 2SO2(g)
2HgO(s) Heat →    2Hg(l) + O2(g)
Similarly, copper which is found as Cu2S in nature can be obtained from its ore by just heating in air.
2Cu2S + 3O2(g) Heat →    2Cu2O(s) + 2SO2(g)
2Cu2O + Cu2S Heat →    6Cu(s) + SO2(g)', 'Metals that are low in the activity series are very unreactive and their oxides can be reduced to metals by heating alone. Cinnabar (HgS), an ore of mercury, converts first to mercuric oxide (HgO) when heated in air, which further decomposes to mercury metal upon continued heating. Similarly, copper found as Cu2S can be extracted by heating its ore in air, producing copper metal and sulfur dioxide gas.', '["activity series", "unreactive metals", "cinnabar", "mercuric oxide", "reduction by heating", "copper ore", "Cu2S", "metal extraction"]'::jsonb, NULL, 43, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'bdf67081081115ba004db422eb821d77') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (96, 111, 15, 'concept', 'The metals in the middle of the activity series such as iron, zinc, lead, copper, are moderately reactive. These are usually present as sulphides or carbonates in nature. It is easier to obtain a metal from its oxide, as compared to its sulphides and carbonates. Therefore, prior to reduction, the metal sulphides and carbonates must be converted into metal oxides. The sulphide ores are converted into oxides by heating strongly in the presence of excess air. This process is known as roasting. The carbonate ores are changed into oxides by heating strongly in limited air. This process is known as calcination. The chemical reaction that takes place during roasting and calcination of zinc ores can be shown as follows –
Roasting
2ZnS(s) + 3O2(g) → 2ZnO(s) + 2SO2(g)  Heat
Calcination
ZnCO3(s) → ZnO(s) + CO2(g)  Heat', 'Metals like iron, zinc, lead, and copper are moderately reactive and commonly found as sulphides or carbonates. To extract these metals, their ores are first converted into oxides because it is easier to reduce oxides to metals. Sulphide ores undergo roasting, which involves heating in excess air to form oxides, while carbonate ores undergo calcination, heating in limited air to form oxides. The chemical reactions for roasting and calcination of zinc ores illustrate these processes.', '["activity series", "metal sulphides", "metal carbonates", "roasting", "calcination", "metal oxides", "zinc ores"]'::jsonb, NULL, 44, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '957b7025034d386be6175a4984a11f4c') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (97, 111, 15, 'concept', 'The metal oxides are then reduced to the corresponding metals by using suitable reducing agents such as carbon. For example, when zinc oxide is heated with carbon, it is reduced to metallic zinc.
ZnO(s) + C(s) → Zn(s) + CO(g)
You are already familiar with the process of oxidation and reduction explained in the first Chapter. Obtaining metals from their compounds is also a reduction process.
Besides using carbon (coke) to reduce metal oxides to metals, sometimes displacement reactions can also be used. The highly reactive metals such as sodium, calcium, aluminium, etc., are used as reducing agents because they can displace metals of lower reactivity from their compounds. For example, when manganese dioxide is heated with aluminium powder, the following reaction takes place –
3MnO2(s) + 4Al(s) → 3Mn(l) + 2Al2O3(s) + Heat
Can you identify the substances that are getting oxidised and reduced?
These displacement reactions are highly exothermic. The amount of heat evolved is so large that the metals are produced in the molten state. In fact, the reaction of iron(III) oxide (Fe2O3) with aluminium is used to join railway tracks or cracked machine parts. This reaction is known as the thermit reaction.
Fe2O3(s) + 2Al(s) → 2Fe(l) + Al2O3(s) + Heat', 'Metal oxides can be reduced to metals using carbon as a reducing agent, as shown by the reduction of zinc oxide to zinc. Highly reactive metals like aluminium can also reduce metal oxides through displacement reactions, which are highly exothermic and produce molten metals. The thermit reaction, involving iron(III) oxide and aluminium, is an example used in practical applications such as welding railway tracks.', '["metal oxides", "reduction", "carbon", "displacement reactions", "aluminium", "manganese dioxide", "thermit reaction", "exothermic reactions"]'::jsonb, NULL, 45, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["oxidation and reduction"]'::jsonb, NULL, '563b5c3d6f4d0c4ef478d32f66958648') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (98, 111, 16, 'concept', 'The metals high up in the reactivity series are very reactive. They cannot be obtained from their compounds by heating with carbon. For example, carbon cannot reduce the oxides of sodium, magnesium, calcium, aluminium, etc., to the respective metals. This is because these metals have more affinity for oxygen than carbon. These metals are obtained by electrolytic reduction. For example, sodium, magnesium and calcium are obtained by the electrolysis of their molten chlorides. The metals are deposited at the cathode (the negatively charged electrode), whereas, chlorine is liberated at the anode (the positively charged electrode). The reactions are –
At cathode Na+ + e–→Na
At anode       2Cl –→Cl2 + 2e–
Similarly, aluminium is obtained by the electrolytic reduction of aluminium oxide.', 'Highly reactive metals such as sodium, magnesium, calcium, and aluminium cannot be extracted by heating their compounds with carbon because they have a greater affinity for oxygen than carbon. These metals are instead obtained by electrolytic reduction, where molten chlorides are electrolyzed. During electrolysis, metals are deposited at the cathode, and chlorine gas is released at the anode. Aluminium is extracted by the electrolytic reduction of aluminium oxide.', '["reactivity series", "electrolytic reduction", "sodium", "magnesium", "calcium", "aluminium", "electrolysis", "cathode", "anode"]'::jsonb, NULL, 46, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '2de1cdb21ecc573a1617ae1cbde33a0a') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (99, 111, 17, 'concept', 'The metals produced by various reduction processes described above are not very pure. They contain impurities, which must be removed to obtain pure metals. The most widely used method for refining impure metals is electrolytic refining. Many metals, such as copper, zinc, tin, nickel, silver, gold, etc., are refined electrolytically. In this process, the impure metal is made the anode and a thin strip of pure metal is made the cathode. A solution of the metal salt is used as an electrolyte. The apparatus is set up as shown in Fig. 3.12. On passing the current through the electrolyte, the pure metal from the anode dissolves into the electrolyte. An equivalent amount of pure metal from the electrolyte is deposited on the cathode. The soluble impurities go into the solution, whereas the insoluble impurities settle down at the bottom of the anode and are known as anode mud.', 'Impure metals obtained from reduction processes contain impurities that must be removed to obtain pure metals. Electrolytic refining is a common method used for this purpose, applicable to metals like copper, zinc, tin, nickel, silver, and gold. In this process, the impure metal acts as the anode and a pure metal strip as the cathode, with a metal salt solution as the electrolyte. When electric current passes, pure metal dissolves from the anode and deposits on the cathode, while soluble impurities remain in solution and insoluble impurities form anode mud.', '["electrolytic refining", "impure metals", "anode", "cathode", "electrolyte", "anode mud", "metal salt solution"]'::jsonb, NULL, 47, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '90c4420b3400bb59c9c142039b7fa3d2') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (100, 111, 17, 'question', '1. Define the following terms.
(i) Mineral (ii) Ore (iii) Gangue
2. Name two metals which are found in nature in the free state.
3. What chemical process is used for obtaining a metal from its oxide?', 'This section asks for definitions of mineral, ore, and gangue, identification of two metals found in nature in the free state, and the chemical process used to obtain a metal from its oxide.', '["mineral", "ore", "gangue", "free state metals", "chemical process", "metal oxide"]'::jsonb, NULL, 48, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, '16befb6b0682805ee806388d7c928a28') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (101, 113, NULL, 'concept', 'You have learnt the following about corrosion in Chapter 1 –
	6 Silver articles become black after some time when exposed to air. This is because it reacts with sulphur in the air to form a coating of silver sulphide.
	6 Copper reacts with moist carbon dioxide in the air and slowly loses its shiny brown surface and gains a green coat. This green substance is basic copper carbonate.
	6 Iron when exposed to moist air for a long time acquires a coating of a brown flaky substance called rust.
Let us find out the conditions under which iron rusts.', 'Silver articles turn black due to the formation of silver sulphide when they react with sulphur in the air. Copper reacts with moist carbon dioxide, losing its shiny brown surface and forming a green coating of basic copper carbonate. Iron exposed to moist air over time develops a brown flaky coating known as rust.', '["corrosion", "silver sulphide", "basic copper carbonate", "rust", "silver", "copper", "iron", "moist air"]'::jsonb, NULL, 50, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'aa90f6d8325c718839f6f6438e1b8eee') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (102, 114, NULL, 'concept', 'Pure gold, known as 24 carat gold, is very soft. It is, therefore, not suitable for making jewellery. It is alloyed with either silver or copper to make it hard. Generally, in India, 22 carat gold is used for making ornaments. It means that 22 parts of pure gold is alloyed with 2 parts of either copper or silver.
If one of the metals is mercury, then the alloy is known as an amalgam. The electrical conductivity and melting point of an alloy is less than that of pure metals. For example, brass, an alloy of copper and zinc (Cu and Zn), and bronze, an alloy of copper and tin (Cu and Sn), are not good conductors of electricity whereas copper is used for making electrical circuits. Solder, an alloy of lead and tin (Pb and Sn), has a low melting point and is used for welding electrical wires together.', 'Pure 24 carat gold is very soft and unsuitable for jewellery, so it is alloyed with silver or copper to increase hardness, commonly as 22 carat gold in India. An alloy containing mercury is called an amalgam. Alloys generally have lower electrical conductivity and melting points than pure metals. Brass (copper and zinc) and bronze (copper and tin) are poor conductors, unlike pure copper. Solder (lead and tin) has a low melting point and is used for welding electrical wires.', '["gold alloys", "24 carat gold", "22 carat gold", "amalgam", "brass", "bronze", "solder", "electrical conductivity", "melting point"]'::jsonb, NULL, 51, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'f1706a7a9e1835edb2ef75ff7db883ee') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (103, 114, 18, 'concept', 'The rusting of iron can be prevented by painting, oiling, greasing, galvanising, chrome plating, anodising or making alloys. Galvanisation is a method of protecting steel and iron from rusting by coating them with a thin layer of zinc. The galvanised article is protected against rusting even if the zinc coating is broken. Can you reason this out? Alloying is a very good method of improving the properties of a metal. We can get the desired properties by this method. For example, iron is the most widely used metal. But it is never used in its pure state. This is because pure iron is very soft and stretches easily when hot. But, if it is mixed with a small amount of carbon (about 0.05 %), it becomes hard and strong. When iron is mixed with nickel and chromium, we get stainless steel, which is hard and does not rust. Thus, if iron is mixed with some other substance, its properties change. In fact, the properties of any metal can be changed if it is mixed with some other substance. The substance added may be a metal or a non-metal. An alloy is a homogeneous mixture of two or more metals, or a metal and a non-metal. It is prepared by first melting the primary metal, and then, dissolving the other elements in it in definite proportions. It is then cooled to room temperature.', 'Rusting of iron can be prevented by various methods including painting, oiling, greasing, galvanising, chrome plating, anodising, and alloying. Galvanisation protects iron and steel by coating them with zinc, which prevents rust even if the coating is damaged. Alloying improves metal properties; for example, adding carbon to iron makes it hard and strong, and mixing iron with nickel and chromium produces stainless steel that resists rust. An alloy is a homogeneous mixture of metals or metals with non-metals, prepared by melting and mixing in definite proportions.', '["rusting", "galvanisation", "alloying", "iron", "carbon", "stainless steel", "zinc coating", "homogeneous mixture"]'::jsonb, NULL, 52, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'fb928a0d7bb5c3fe01afa310af132a77') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (104, 114, 18, 'concept', 'Iron pillar at Delhi The wonder of ancient Indian metallurgy The iron pillar near the Qutub Minar in Delhi was built more than 1600 years ago by the iron workers of India. They had developed a process which prevented iron from rusting. For its quality of rust resistance it has been examined by scientists from all parts of the world. The iron pillar is 8 m high and weighs 6 tonnes (6000 kg).', 'The iron pillar near the Qutub Minar in Delhi, built over 1600 years ago, demonstrates ancient Indian metallurgy with its remarkable resistance to rust. Scientists worldwide have studied this 8-meter-high, 6-tonne iron pillar for its corrosion resistance, indicating advanced metalworking techniques in ancient India.', '["iron pillar", "Delhi", "rust resistance", "ancient metallurgy", "Qutub Minar", "corrosion"]'::jsonb, NULL, 53, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["rusting", "iron"]'::jsonb, NULL, '01e1a65939a6c072497d07c7f48356b3') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (105, 114, 18, 'concept', 'Elements can be classified as metals and non-metals. Metals are lustrous, malleable, ductile and are good conductors of heat and electricity. They are solids at room temperature, except mercury which is a liquid. Metals can form positive ions by losing electrons to non-metals. Metals combine with oxygen to form basic oxides. Aluminium oxide and zinc oxide show the properties of both basic as well as acidic oxides. These oxides are known as amphoteric oxides. Different metals have different reactivities with water and dilute acids. A list of common metals arranged in order of their decreasing reactivity is known as an activity series. Metals above hydrogen in the Activity series can displace hydrogen from dilute acids. A more reactive metal displaces a less reactive metal from its salt solution. Metals occur in nature as free elements or in the form of their compounds. The extraction of metals from their ores and then refining them for use is known as metallurgy. An alloy is a homogeneous mixture of two or more metals, or a metal and a non-metal. The surface of some metals, such as iron, is corroded when they are exposed to moist air for a long period of time. This phenomenon is known as corrosion. Non-metals have properties opposite to that of metals. They are neither malleable nor ductile. They are bad conductors of heat and electricity, except for graphite, which conducts electricity. Non-metals form negatively charged ions by gaining electrons when reacting with metals. Non-metals form oxides which are either acidic or neutral. Non-metals do not displace hydrogen from dilute acids. They react with hydrogen to form hydrides.', 'Elements are classified as metals and non-metals based on their properties. Metals are lustrous, malleable, ductile, good conductors, and mostly solid at room temperature except mercury. They form positive ions and basic oxides, with some oxides like aluminium and zinc oxide being amphoteric. Metals vary in reactivity, arranged in an activity series, and can displace hydrogen from acids or less reactive metals from their salts. Metallurgy involves extracting and refining metals. Corrosion is the deterioration of metals like iron due to moist air. Non-metals have opposite properties, are poor conductors except graphite, form negative ions, acidic or neutral oxides, and react with hydrogen to form hydrides but do not displace hydrogen from acids.', '["metals", "non-metals", "activity series", "basic oxides", "amphoteric oxides", "corrosion", "hydrides", "metallurgy"]'::jsonb, NULL, 54, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'c7f2d2539f3c98580804413b7922696a') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (106, 114, 18, 'question', 'In which cases will you find displacement reactions taking place? 2. Which metals do not corrode easily? 3. What are alloys? QUESTIONS 1. Metallic oxides of zinc, magnesium and copper were heated with the following metals. Metal Zinc Magnesium Copper Zinc oxide Magnesium oxide Copper oxide What you have learnt', 'Questions ask about the conditions under which displacement reactions occur, identification of metals resistant to corrosion, and the definition of alloys. An exercise involves heating metallic oxides of zinc, magnesium, and copper with different metals to observe reactions.', '["displacement reactions", "corrosion", "alloys", "metallic oxides", "zinc oxide", "magnesium oxide", "copper oxide"]'::jsonb, NULL, 55, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["displacement reactions", "corrosion", "alloys"]'::jsonb, NULL, '9bb027789e5ac4e5495fd983d387af4d') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (107, 114, 18, 'question', 'EXERCISES 1. Which of the following pairs will give displacement reactions? (a) NaCl solution and copper metal (b) MgCl2 solution and aluminium metal (c) FeSO4 s', 'Exercise asks to identify which pairs of metal solutions and metals will undergo displacement reactions, including pairs such as NaCl solution with copper metal, MgCl2 solution with aluminium metal, and FeSO4 with an unspecified metal.', '["displacement reactions", "NaCl", "copper metal", "MgCl2", "aluminium metal", "FeSO4"]'::jsonb, NULL, 56, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00', '["displacement reactions", "activity series"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'dcc6dfb6e601d9b5358cbd71307cbb69') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (108, 115, NULL, 'concept', 'You have studied in the previous chapter about refraction of light by lenses. You also studied the nature, position and relative size of images formed by lenses. How can these ideas help us in the study of the human eye? The human eye uses light and enables us to see objects around us. It has a lens in its structure. What is the function of the lens in a human eye? How do the lenses used in spectacles correct defects of vision? Let us consider these questions in this chapter. We have learnt in the previous chapter about light and some of its properties. In this chapter, we shall use these ideas to study some of the optical phenomena in nature. We shall also discuss about rainbow formation, splitting of white light and blue colour of the sky.', 'The chapter introduces the human eye as an optical instrument that uses light to enable vision. It connects prior knowledge of refraction and lenses to understanding the eye''s lens function and vision correction through spectacles. The chapter also aims to explore natural optical phenomena such as rainbow formation, white light splitting, and the blue colour of the sky.', '["human eye", "lens", "refraction", "images", "spectacles", "rainbow formation", "white light", "blue colour of the sky"]'::jsonb, NULL, 0, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', '["refraction of light", "properties of light", "lenses and image formation"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'bd7279df7ec3adbf5928ac7a87f60ec1') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (109, 116, NULL, 'concept', 'The human eye is one of the most valuable and sensitive sense organs. It enables us to see the wonderful world and the colours around us. On closing the eyes, we can identify objects to some extent by their smell, taste, sound they make or by touch. It is, however, impossible to identify colours while closing the eyes. Thus, of all the sense organs, the human eye is the most significant one as it enables us to see the beautiful, colourful world around us. The human eye is like a camera. Its lens system forms an image on a light-sensitive screen called the retina. Light enters the eye through a thin membrane called the cornea. It forms the transparent bulge on the front surface of the eyeball as shown in Fig. 10.1. The eyeball is approximately spherical in shape with a diameter of about 2.3 cm. Most of the refraction for the light rays entering the eye occurs at the outer surface of the cornea. The crystalline lens merely provides the finer adjustment of focal length required to focus objects at different distances on the retina. We find a structure called iris behind the cornea. Iris is a dark muscular diaphragm that controls the size of the pupil. The pupil regulates and controls the amount of light entering the eye. The eye lens forms an inverted real image of the object on the retina. The retina is a delicate membrane having enormous number of light-sensitive cells. The light-sensitive cells get activated upon illumination and generate electrical signals. These signals are sent to the brain via the optic nerves. The brain interprets these signals, and finally, processes the information so that we perceive objects as they are.', 'The human eye is a sensitive organ that enables vision and colour perception. It functions like a camera, with light entering through the cornea and being focused by the crystalline lens onto the retina. The iris controls the pupil size to regulate light entry. The retina contains light-sensitive cells that convert light into electrical signals sent to the brain via the optic nerves, where the signals are interpreted to form visual perception.', '["human eye", "cornea", "crystalline lens", "retina", "iris", "pupil", "optic nerves", "light-sensitive cells"]'::jsonb, NULL, 1, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', NULL, NULL, '650b0beacea25bd56354625eca0c9d47') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (110, 116, 19, 'concept', 'The eye lens is composed of a fibrous, jelly-like material. Its curvature can be modified to some extent by the ciliary muscles. The change in the curvature of the eye lens can thus change its focal length. When the muscles are relaxed, the lens becomes thin. Thus, its focal length increases. This enables us to see distant objects clearly. When you are looking at objects closer to the eye, the ciliary muscles contract. This increases the curvature of the eye lens. The eye lens then becomes thicker. Consequently, the focal length of the eye lens decreases. This enables us to see nearby objects clearly.
The ability of the eye lens to adjust its focal length is called accommodation. However, the focal length of the eye lens cannot be decreased below a certain minimum limit. Try to read a printed page by holding it very close to your eyes. You may see the image being blurred or feel strain in the eye. To see an object comfortably and distinctly, you must hold it at about 25 cm from the eyes. The minimum distance, at which objects can be seen most distinctly without strain, is called the least distance of distinct vision. It is also called the near point of the eye. For a young adult with normal vision, the near point is about 25 cm. The farthest point up to which the eye can see objects clearly is called the far point of the eye. It is infinity for a normal eye. You may note here a normal eye can see objects clearly that are between 25 cm and infinity.
Sometimes, the crystalline lens of people at old age becomes milky and cloudy. This condition is called cataract. This causes partial or complete loss of vision. It is possible to restore vision through a cataract surgery.', 'The eye lens is made of a fibrous, jelly-like material whose curvature is controlled by ciliary muscles to change its focal length. Relaxed muscles make the lens thin, increasing focal length for distant vision, while contracted muscles thicken the lens, decreasing focal length for near vision. This adjustment ability is called accommodation. The least distance of distinct vision (near point) is about 25 cm for a normal young adult, and the far point is at infinity. Cataract is a condition where the lens becomes cloudy with age, causing vision loss, which can be treated surgically.', '["eye lens", "ciliary muscles", "curvature", "focal length", "accommodation", "least distance of distinct vision", "near point", "far point", "cataract"]'::jsonb, NULL, 2, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', NULL, NULL, '1cce04643339373eecc1c26ece2a21d0') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (111, 117, NULL, 'concept', 'Sometimes, the eye may gradually lose its power of accommodation. In such conditions, the person cannot see the objects distinctly and comfortably. The vision becomes blurred due to the refractive defects of the eye. There are mainly three common refractive defects of vision. These are (i) myopia or near-sightedness, (ii) hypermetropia or far-sightedness, and (iii) presbyopia. These defects can be corrected by the use of suitable spherical lenses. We discuss below these defects and their correction.

(a) Myopia
Myopia is also known as near-sightedness. A person with myopia can see nearby objects clearly but cannot see distant objects distinctly. A person with this defect has the far point nearer than infinity. Such a person may see clearly up to a distance of a few metres. In a myopic eye, the image of a distant object is formed in front of the retina [Fig.', 'The eye can lose its accommodation power, causing blurred vision due to refractive defects. The three common refractive defects are myopia (near-sightedness), hypermetropia (far-sightedness), and presbyopia. These defects affect the clarity of vision at different distances and can be corrected using appropriate spherical lenses. Myopia causes distant objects to appear blurry because their images form in front of the retina.', '["refractive defects", "myopia", "near-sightedness", "accommodation", "spherical lenses", "vision", "far point", "retina"]'::jsonb, NULL, 3, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'c92902ea8ba424db126436c9cb0a123f') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (112, 118, NULL, 'concept', 'This defect may arise due to (i) excessive curvature of the eye lens, or (ii) elongation of the eyeball. This defect can be corrected by using a concave lens of suitable power. This is illustrated in Fig. 10.2 (c). A concave lens of suitable power will bring the image back on to the retina and thus the defect is corrected.', 'Myopia, or nearsightedness, occurs due to excessive curvature of the eye lens or elongation of the eyeball. This causes images to focus in front of the retina, making distant objects appear blurry. The defect is corrected by using a concave lens of appropriate power, which diverges light rays to focus the image on the retina.', '["myopia", "concave lens", "eye lens curvature", "eyeball elongation", "retina"]'::jsonb, NULL, 4, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', NULL, NULL, '56a32e93cee1d26e83a3f6b5a769ecb5') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (113, 119, NULL, 'concept', 'Hypermetropia is also known as far-sightedness. A person with hypermetropia can see distant objects clearly but cannot see nearby objects distinctly. The near point, for the person, is farther away from the normal near point (25 cm). Such a person has to keep a reading material much beyond 25 cm from the eye for comfortable reading. This is because the light rays from a closeby object are focussed at a point behind the retina as shown in Fig. 10.3 (b). This defect arises either because (i) the focal length of the eye lens is too long, or (ii) the eyeball has become too small. This defect can be corrected by using a convex lens of appropriate power. This is illustrated in Fig. 10.3 (c). Eye-glasses with converging lenses provide the additional focussing power required for forming the image on the retina.', 'Hypermetropia, or farsightedness, is a defect where distant objects are seen clearly but nearby objects appear blurry. This occurs because the eye lens has too long a focal length or the eyeball is too small, causing light rays from near objects to focus behind the retina. The defect is corrected by using convex lenses that converge light rays to focus images on the retina.', '["hypermetropia", "farsightedness", "convex lens", "focal length", "eyeball size", "near point"]'::jsonb, NULL, 5, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'bed18d1b6fcfc5fb20224cd0dc726cdb') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (114, 120, NULL, 'concept', 'The power of accommodation of the eye usually decreases with ageing. For most people, the near point gradually recedes away. They find it difficult to see nearby objects comfortably and distinctly without corrective eye-glasses. This defect is called Presbyopia. It arises due to the gradual weakening of the ciliary muscles and diminishing flexibility of the eye lens. Sometimes, a person may suffer from both myopia and hypermetropia. Such people often require bi-focal lenses. A common type of bi-focal lenses consists of both concave and convex lenses. The upper portion consists of a concave lens. It facilitates distant vision. The lower part is a convex lens. It facilitates near vision. These days, it is possible to correct the refractive defects with contact lenses or through surgical interventions.', 'Presbyopia is an age-related defect where the eye''s accommodation power decreases due to weakening ciliary muscles and less flexible eye lens, causing difficulty in seeing nearby objects. Some individuals have both myopia and hypermetropia and require bifocal lenses, which combine concave lenses for distant vision and convex lenses for near vision. Modern corrections include contact lenses and surgical methods.', '["presbyopia", "power of accommodation", "ciliary muscles", "bifocal lenses", "concave lens", "convex lens", "contact lenses", "surgical correction"]'::jsonb, NULL, 6, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', '["myopia", "hypermetropia"]'::jsonb, NULL, '514829555792a9616744e78ade223ab1') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (115, 121, NULL, 'concept', 'Do you know that our eyes can live even after our death? By donating our eyes after we die, we can light the life of a blind person. About 35 million people in the developing world are blind and most of them can be cured. About 4.5 million people with corneal blindness can be cured through corneal transplantation of donated eyes. Out of these 4.5 million, 60% are children below the age of 12. So, if we have got the gift of vision, why not pass it on to somebody who does not have it? What do we have to keep in mind when eyes have to be donated? Eye donors can belong to any age group or sex. People who use spectacles, or those operated for cataract, can still donate the eyes. People who are diabetic, have hypertension, asthma patients and those without communicable diseases can also donate eyes.', 'Eyes can be donated after death to help cure blindness through corneal transplantation. Approximately 35 million people in developing countries are blind, with 4.5 million suffering from corneal blindness that is curable by transplantation. Sixty percent of these patients are children under 12. Eye donors can be of any age or sex, including those who wear spectacles or have had cataract surgery, as long as they do not have communicable diseases.', '["eye donation", "corneal blindness", "corneal transplantation", "blindness cure", "donor eligibility", "spectacles", "cataract surgery"]'::jsonb, NULL, 7, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', NULL, NULL, '637ddcb56225c793edc4f7b47f1c7c26') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (116, 122, NULL, 'question', '1. What is meant by power of accommodation of the eye?
2. A person with a myopic eye cannot see objects beyond 1.2 m distinctly. What should be the type of the corrective lens used to restore proper vision?
3. What is the far point and near point of the human eye with normal vision?
4. A student has difficulty reading the blackboard while sitting in the last row. What could be the defect the child is suffering from? How can it be corrected?', 'These questions test understanding of the eye''s power of accommodation, corrective lenses for myopia, normal far and near points of the human eye, and identification and correction of common vision defects such as difficulty seeing distant objects.', '["power of accommodation", "myopia", "corrective lens", "far point", "near point", "vision defect", "reading difficulty"]'::jsonb, NULL, 8, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', '["myopia", "power of accommodation", "corrective lenses"]'::jsonb, NULL, '3f8f6c8e7619f345fd4e566ee8d435cb') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (117, 125, NULL, 'concept', 'You must have seen and appreciated the spectacular colours in a rainbow. How could the white light of the Sun give us various colours of the rainbow? Before we take up this question, we shall first go back to the refraction of light through a prism. The inclined refracting surfaces of a glass prism show exciting phenomenon. Let us find it out through an activity.
PE – Incident ray ∠i – Angle of incidence
EF – Refracted ray ∠r – Angle of refraction
FS – Emergent ray ∠e – Angle of emergence
∠A – Angle of the prism ∠D – Angle of deviation
Figure 10.4 Refraction of light through a triangular glass prism

The prism has probably split the incident white light into a band of colours. Note the colours that appear at the two ends of the colour band. What is the sequence of colours that you see on the screen? The various colours seen are Violet, Indigo, Blue, Green, Yellow, Orange and Red, as shown in Fig. 10.5. The acronym VIBGYOR will help you to remember the sequence of colours. The band of the coloured components of a light beam is called its spectrum. You might not be able to see all the colours separately. Yet something makes each colour distinct from the other. The splitting of light into its component colours is called dispersion.

You have seen that white light is dispersed into its seven-colour components by a prism. Why do we get these colours? Different colours of light bend through different angles with respect to the incident ray, as they pass through a prism. The red light bends the least while the violet the most. Thus the rays of each colour emerge along different paths and thus become distinct. It is the band of distinct colours that we see in a spectrum.', 'White light from the Sun can be split into a spectrum of seven colours—Violet, Indigo, Blue, Green, Yellow, Orange, and Red—by refraction through a glass prism. This splitting of light into its component colours is called dispersion. Different colours bend at different angles when passing through the prism, with red bending the least and violet the most, resulting in distinct coloured rays forming a spectrum.', '["white light", "prism", "refraction", "dispersion", "spectrum", "VIBGYOR", "colours", "angle of deviation"]'::jsonb, NULL, 11, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', NULL, NULL, '2145bbe4c416f3fcfa3aefefc32d3bf7') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (118, 126, NULL, 'concept', 'Isaac Newton was the first to use a glass prism to obtain the spectrum of sunlight. He tried to split the colours of the spectrum of white light further by using another similar prism. However, he could not get any more colours. He then placed a second identical prism in an inverted position with respect to the first prism, as shown in Fig. 10.6. This allowed all the colours of the spectrum to pass through the second prism. He found a beam of white light emerging from the other side of the second prism. This observation gave Newton the idea that the sunlight is made up of seven colours.

Any light that gives a spectrum similar to that of sunlight is often referred to as white light.', 'Newton demonstrated that white light is composed of seven colours by passing sunlight through a prism to create a spectrum and then recombining the spectrum with a second inverted prism to produce white light again. This experiment showed that the colours in sunlight are fundamental components of white light.', '["Isaac Newton", "prism", "spectrum", "white light", "recombination", "seven colours"]'::jsonb, NULL, 12, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', '["Dispersion of White Light through a Glass Prism"]'::jsonb, NULL, '05e334b90e5d5bd1044bb235eb2d1b80') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (119, 127, NULL, 'concept', 'A rainbow is a natural spectrum appearing in the sky after a rain shower (Fig. 10.7). It is caused by dispersion of sunlight by tiny water droplets, present in the atmosphere. A rainbow is always formed in a direction opposite to that of the Sun. The water droplets act like small prisms. They refract and disperse the incident sunlight, then reflect it internally, and finally refract it again when it comes out of the raindrop (Fig. 10.8). Due to the dispersion of light and internal reflection, different colours reach the observer’s eye.

You can also see a rainbow on a sunny day when you look at the sky through a waterfall or through a water fountain, with the Sun behind you.', 'Rainbows form when sunlight is dispersed by tiny water droplets in the atmosphere, which act like prisms. The droplets refract, internally reflect, and again refract the sunlight, causing the light to split into its component colours and appear as a spectrum opposite the Sun. Rainbows can also be seen near waterfalls or fountains with the Sun behind the observer.', '["rainbow", "dispersion", "sunlight", "water droplets", "internal reflection", "spectrum"]'::jsonb, NULL, 13, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', '["Dispersion of White Light through a Glass Prism"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'e80ca4c202ef6e1f47e467ada16b1074') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (120, 128, NULL, 'concept', 'You might have observed the apparent random wavering or flickering of objects seen through a turbulent stream of hot air rising above a fire or a radiator. The air just above the fire becomes hotter than the air further up. The hotter air is lighter (less dense) than the cooler air.', 'Objects viewed through hot, turbulent air appear to flicker or waver due to atmospheric refraction caused by temperature differences. Hot air above a fire is less dense than cooler air above it, causing light to bend and produce this visual effect.', '["atmospheric refraction", "hot air", "turbulence", "density", "light bending", "flickering"]'::jsonb, NULL, 14, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', NULL, NULL, '942ffee12a4dc600977ff226d84d6471') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (121, 129, NULL, 'concept', 'Sun with respect to the horizon. The time difference between actual sunset and the apparent sunset is about 2 minutes. The apparent flattening of the Sun’s disc at sunrise and sunset is also due to the same phenomenon.

Apparent star position due to atmospheric refraction.', 'Atmospheric refraction causes the apparent position of the Sun and stars to differ from their actual positions. This effect results in a time difference of about 2 minutes between the actual and apparent sunset. Additionally, the Sun appears flattened at sunrise and sunset due to atmospheric refraction.', '["atmospheric refraction", "apparent sunset", "actual sunset", "flattening of Sun''s disc", "apparent star position"]'::jsonb, NULL, 15, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'c1b93701c4d6b7c814a8f2447a8d6bfd') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (122, 130, NULL, 'concept', 'The interplay of light with objects around us gives rise to several spectacular phenomena in nature. The blue colour of the sky, colour of water in deep sea, the reddening of the sun at sunrise and the sunset are some of the wonderful phenomena we are familiar with. In the previous class, you have learnt about the scattering of light by colloidal particles. The path of a beam of light passing through a true solution is not visible. However, its path becomes visible through a colloidal solution where the size of the particles is relatively larger.', 'Scattering of light by particles in the atmosphere and other media causes various natural phenomena such as the blue colour of the sky, the colour of deep sea water, and the reddening of the sun at sunrise and sunset. Light passing through a true solution is invisible, but becomes visible when passing through a colloidal solution due to larger particle size causing scattering.', '["scattering of light", "blue colour of sky", "reddening of sun", "colloidal particles", "true solution", "colloidal solution"]'::jsonb, NULL, 16, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', NULL, NULL, '0bebfa4c760b13359eae7dc10fac4a96') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (123, 130, 20, 'concept', 'The earth’s atmosphere is a heterogeneous mixture of minute particles. These particles include smoke, tiny water droplets, suspended particles of dust and molecules of air. When a beam of light strikes such fine particles, the path of the beam becomes visible. The light reaches us, after being reflected diffusely by these particles. The phenomenon of scattering of light by the colloidal particles gives rise to Tyndall effect which you have studied in Class IX. This phenomenon is seen when a fine beam of sunlight enters a smoke-filled room through a small hole. Thus, scattering of light makes the particles visible. Tyndall effect can also be observed when sunlight passes through a canopy of a dense forest. Here, tiny water droplets in the mist scatter light. The colour of the scattered light depends on the size of the scattering particles. Very fine particles scatter mainly blue light while particles of larger size scatter light of longer wavelengths. If the size of the scattering particles is large enough, then, the scattered light may even appear white.', 'The earth''s atmosphere contains minute particles such as smoke, water droplets, dust, and air molecules that scatter light. This scattering makes the path of a light beam visible and causes the Tyndall effect, observable when sunlight passes through smoke or mist. The color of scattered light depends on particle size: very fine particles scatter blue light, larger particles scatter longer wavelengths, and very large particles can scatter white light.', '["earth\u2019s atmosphere", "minute particles", "scattering of light", "Tyndall effect", "colloidal particles", "blue light", "particle size", "white light"]'::jsonb, NULL, 17, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', NULL, NULL, '2e07f8c3569bba36f2b4cc26b76f932a') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (124, 130, 21, 'concept', 'The molecules of air and other fine particles in the atmosphere have size smaller than the wavelength of visible light. These are more effective in scattering light of shorter wavelengths at the blue end than light of longer wavelengths at the red end. The red light has a wavelength about 1.8 times greater than blue light. Thus, when sunlight passes through the atmosphere, the fine particles in air scatter the blue colour (shorter wavelengths) more strongly than red. The scattered blue light enters our eyes. If the earth had no atmosphere, there would not have been any scattering. Then, the sky would have looked dark. The sky appears dark to passengers flying at very high altitudes, as scattering is not prominent at such heights. You might have observed that ‘danger’ signal lights are red in colour. Do you know why? The red is least scattered by fog or smoke. Therefore, it can be seen in the same colour at a distance.', 'Air molecules and fine particles scatter shorter wavelengths of visible light (blue) more effectively than longer wavelengths (red). This scattering causes the sky to appear blue. Without an atmosphere, no scattering occurs and the sky appears dark. At high altitudes, scattering is minimal, so the sky looks dark. Red signal lights are used because red light is least scattered by fog or smoke, making them visible from a distance.', '["scattering", "wavelength", "blue light", "red light", "atmosphere", "sky colour", "signal lights"]'::jsonb, NULL, 18, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', NULL, NULL, '92b4998eced348001fca4164dcec3e4f') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (125, 130, 21, 'concept', 'The ability of the eye to focus on both near and distant objects, by adjusting its focal length, is called the accommodation of the eye. The smallest distance, at which the eye can see objects clearly without strain, is called the near point of the eye or the least distance of distinct vision. For a young adult with normal vision, it is about 25 cm. The common refractive defects of vision include myopia, hypermetropia and presbyopia. Myopia (short-sightedness – the image of distant objects is focussed before the retina) is corrected by using a concave lens of suitable power. Hypermetropia (far-sightedness – the image of nearby objects is focussed beyond the retina) is corrected by using a convex lens of suitable power. The eye loses its power of accommodation at old age. The splitting of white light into its component colours is called dispersion. Scattering of light causes the blue colour of sky.', 'Accommodation is the eye''s ability to adjust its focal length to focus on objects at different distances. The near point is the closest distance at which the eye can see clearly, about 25 cm for a young adult. Refractive defects include myopia, corrected by concave lenses, and hypermetropia, corrected by convex lenses. Presbyopia is the loss of accommodation with age. Dispersion splits white light into colours, and scattering causes the sky''s blue colour.', '["accommodation", "near point", "myopia", "hypermetropia", "presbyopia", "dispersion", "scattering"]'::jsonb, NULL, 19, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', NULL, NULL, '99a8546ba6070da26fa07b7ce9095793') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (126, 130, 21, 'question', '1. The human eye can focus on objects at different distances by adjusting the focal length of the eye lens. This is due to (a) presbyopia. (b) accommodation. (c) near-sightedness. (d) far-sightedness.
2. The human eye forms the image of an object at its (a) cornea. (b) iris. (c) pupil. (d) retina.
3. The least distance of distinct vision for a young adult with normal vision is about (a) 25 m. (b) 2.5 cm. (c) 25 cm. (d) 2.5 m.
4. The change in focal length of an eye lens is caused by the action of the (a) pupil. (b) retina. (c) ciliary muscles. (d) iris.
5. A person needs a lens of power –5.5 dioptres for correcting his distant vision. For correcting his near vision he needs a lens of power +1.5 dioptre. What is the focal length of the lens required for correcting (i) distant vision, and (ii) near vision?
6. The far point of a myopic person is 80 cm in front of the eye. What is the nature and power of the lens required to correct the problem?
7. Make a diagram to show how hypermetropia is corrected. The near point of a hypermetropic eye is 1 m. What is the power of the lens required to correct this defect? Assume that the near point of the normal eye is 25 cm.
8. Why is a normal eye not able to see clearly the objects placed closer than 25 cm?
9. What happens to the image distance in the eye when we increase the distance of an object from the eye?
10. Why do stars twinkle?
11. Explain why the planets do not twinkle.
12. Why does the sky appear dark instead of blue to an astronaut?', 'These questions test understanding of eye accommodation, image formation on the retina, near point of distinct vision, role of ciliary muscles, lens power calculations for vision correction, and phenomena such as twinkling of stars and sky colour at high altitudes. They also cover refractive defects like myopia and hypermetropia and their corrections.', '["accommodation", "retina", "near point", "ciliary muscles", "lens power", "myopia", "hypermetropia", "twinkling", "sky colour"]'::jsonb, NULL, 20, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00', '["accommodation", "near point", "myopia", "hypermetropia", "scattering"]'::jsonb, NULL, '0242f690ea510ec835891453b443727f') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (127, 131, NULL, 'concept', 'Electricity has an important place in modern society. It is a controllable and convenient form of energy for a variety of uses in homes, schools, hospitals, industries and so on. What constitutes electricity? How does it flow in an electric circuit? What are the factors that control or regulate the current through an electric circuit? In this Chapter, we shall attempt to answer such questions. We shall also discuss the heating effect of electric current and its applications.', 'Electricity is a controllable and convenient form of energy widely used in various sectors such as homes, schools, hospitals, and industries. The chapter introduces fundamental questions about the nature of electricity, its flow in circuits, and the factors regulating electric current. It also covers the heating effect of electric current and its practical applications.', '["electricity", "electric circuit", "electric current", "heating effect", "energy", "applications"]'::jsonb, NULL, 0, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', NULL, NULL, '1db2d4917e3cb96d705234d78da6d66b') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (128, 132, NULL, 'concept', 'We are familiar with air current and water current. We know that flowing water constitutes water current in rivers. Similarly, if the electric charge flows through a conductor (for example, through a metallic wire), we say that there is an electric current in the conductor. In a torch, we know that the cells (or a battery, when placed in proper order) provide flow of charges or an electric current through the torch bulb to glow. We have also seen that the torch gives light only when its switch is on. What does a switch do? A switch makes a conducting link between the cell and the bulb. A continuous and closed path of an electric current is called an electric circuit. Now, if the circuit is broken anywhere (or the switch of the torch is turned off), the current stops flowing and the bulb does not glow. How do we express electric current? Electric current is expressed by the amount of charge flowing through a particular area in unit time. In other words, it is the rate of flow of electric charges. In circuits using metallic wires, electrons constitute the flow of charges. However, electrons were not known at the time when the phenomenon of electricity was first observed. So, electric current was considered to be the flow of positive charges and the direction of flow of positive charges was taken to be the direction of electric current. Conventionally, in an electric circuit the direction of electric current is taken as opposite to the direction of the flow of electrons, which are negative charges.

If a net charge Q, flows across any cross-section of a conductor in time t, then the current I, through the cross-section is

I = Q / t  (11.1)

The SI unit of electric charge is coulomb (C), which is equivalent to the charge contained in nearly 6 × 10^18 electrons. (We know that an electron possesses a negative charge of 1.6 × 10–19 C.) The electric current is expressed by a unit called ampere (A), named after the French scientist, Andre-Marie Ampere (1775–1836). One ampere is constituted by the flow of one coulomb of charge per second, that is,

1 A = 1 C / 1 s.

Small quantities of current are expressed in milliampere (1 mA = 10–3 A) or in microampere (1 µA = 10–6 A).

An instrument called ammeter measures electric current in a circuit. It is always connected in series in a circuit through which the current is to be measured. Figure 11.1 shows the schematic diagram of a typical electric circuit comprising a cell, an electric bulb, an ammeter and a plug key. Note that the electric current flows in the circuit from the positive terminal of the cell to the negative terminal of the cell through the bulb and ammeter.', 'Electric current is the flow of electric charge through a conductor, typically carried by electrons in metallic wires. The direction of conventional current is opposite to the flow of electrons. Electric current is quantified as the amount of charge passing through a cross-section per unit time, measured in amperes (A). An ammeter is used to measure current and is connected in series in a circuit. A closed path allowing current flow is called an electric circuit, and breaking the circuit stops the current.', '["electric current", "electric charge", "conductor", "electric circuit", "electron", "ampere", "ammeter", "coulomb"]'::jsonb, NULL, 1, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', NULL, NULL, '212547edc190057d339fee95964a4c42') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (129, 133, NULL, 'example', 'Example 11.1
A current of 0.5 A is drawn by a filament of an electric bulb for 10 minutes. Find the amount of electric charge that flows through the circuit.

We are given, I = 0.5 A; t = 10 min = 600 s.
From Eq. (11.1), we have
Q = I t
= 0.5 A × 600 s
= 300 C', 'The example calculates the electric charge flowing through a circuit when a current of 0.5 amperes flows for 10 minutes. Using the formula Q = I × t, the charge is found to be 300 coulombs.', '["electric charge", "current", "time", "coulomb", "ampere"]'::jsonb, NULL, 2, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["electric current", "electric charge", "unit of current"]'::jsonb, NULL, '0bb2a36ef63d3fdb9d34732fa69a3b50') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (130, 134, NULL, 'question', '1. What does an electric circuit mean?
2. Define the unit of current.
3. Calculate the number of electrons constituting one coulomb of charge.', 'These questions test understanding of the definition of an electric circuit, the unit of electric current, and the calculation of the number of electrons in one coulomb of charge.', '["electric circuit", "unit of current", "electron", "coulomb"]'::jsonb, NULL, 3, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["electric current", "electric charge"]'::jsonb, NULL, '00973cfa9083e41e142d7e44d14ec7ea') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (131, 135, NULL, 'concept', 'What makes the electric charge to flow? Let us consider the analogy of flow of water. Charges do not flow in a copper wire by themselves, just as water in a perfectly horizontal tube does not flow. If one end of the tube is connected to a tank of water kept at a higher level, such that there is a pressure difference between the two ends of the tube, water flows out of the other end of the tube. For flow of charges in a conducting metallic wire, the gravity, of course, has no role to play; th', 'Electric charges do not flow spontaneously in a conductor; a potential difference is required to drive the flow, analogous to water flowing due to pressure difference. The analogy compares electric current flow to water flow in a tube connected to a water tank at a higher level.', '["electric charge flow", "analogy", "water flow", "pressure difference", "potential difference", "conductor"]'::jsonb, NULL, 4, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["electric current", "electric circuit"]'::jsonb, NULL, '4c880d301051cc9a53f06ea014598e08') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (132, 136, NULL, 'example', 'How much work is done in moving a charge of 2 C across two points having a potential difference 12 V? The amount of charge Q, that flows between two points at potential difference V (= 12 V) is 2 C. Thus, the amount of work W, done in moving the charge [from Eq. (11.2)] is

W = VQ

= 12 V × 2 C

= 24 J.', 'The work done in moving a charge of 2 coulombs across a potential difference of 12 volts is calculated using the formula W = VQ. Substituting the values, the work done is 24 joules.', '["work done", "charge", "potential difference", "W = VQ", "joules", "coulombs", "volts"]'::jsonb, NULL, 5, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["electric charge", "potential difference", "work done by electric force"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'b8ba8c6a5e1eb2819592ce7ff8ec07ff') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (133, 137, NULL, 'concept', 'We know that an electric circuit, as shown in Fig. 11.1, comprises a cell (or a battery), a plug key, electrical component(s), and connecting wires. It is often convenient to draw a schematic diagram, in which different components of the circuit are represented by the symbols conveniently used. Conventional symbols used to represent some of the most commonly used electrical components are given in Table 11.1.

Table 11.1 Symbols of some commonly used components in circuit diagrams
Sl. No.  Components                         Symbols
1        An electric cell
2        A battery or a combination of cells
3        Plug key or switch (open)
4        Plug key or switch (closed)
5        A wire joint
6        Wires crossing without joining', 'An electric circuit consists of a cell or battery, a plug key, electrical components, and connecting wires. Schematic diagrams use conventional symbols to represent these components for clarity. Table 11.1 lists the symbols for common components such as electric cells, batteries, switches (open and closed), wire joints, and crossing wires without joining.', '["electric circuit", "cell", "battery", "plug key", "electrical components", "schematic diagram", "circuit symbols"]'::jsonb, NULL, 6, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', NULL, NULL, '28040f074faa3221a35a6d4dde6bdee1') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (134, 138, NULL, 'question', '1. Name a device that helps to maintain a potential difference across a conductor.
2. What is meant by saying that the potential difference between two points is 1 V?
3. How much energy is given to each coulomb of charge passing through a 6 V battery?', 'These questions focus on understanding the concept of potential difference in electric circuits. They ask for the identification of a device that maintains potential difference, the meaning of a 1-volt potential difference, and the energy given to each coulomb of charge passing through a 6-volt battery.', '["potential difference", "device", "voltage", "energy", "coulomb", "battery"]'::jsonb, NULL, 7, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["electric circuit", "potential difference"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'a50d5e1ad69096a500ef20b4284e12b6') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (135, 141, NULL, 'concept', 'V–I graph for a nichrome wire. A straight line plot shows that as the current through a wire increases, the potential difference across the wire increases linearly – this is Ohm’s law.', 'The voltage-current (V–I) graph for a nichrome wire is a straight line, indicating a linear relationship between potential difference and current. This linearity confirms Ohm’s law, which states that the potential difference across a conductor is directly proportional to the current flowing through it.', '["V\u2013I graph", "nichrome wire", "linear relationship", "potential difference", "current", "Ohm\u2019s law"]'::jsonb, NULL, 10, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["Experimental Verification of Ohm\u2019s Law Using a Nichrome Wire"]'::jsonb, NULL, '23d9a71bae5e7129316fbeb94c785c82') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (136, 142, NULL, 'concept', 'Take a nichrome wire, a torch bulb, a 10 W bulb and an ammeter (0–5 A range), a plug key and some connecting wires. Set up the circuit by connecting four dry cells of 1.5 V each in series with the ammeter leaving a gap XY in the circuit, as shown in Fig. 11.4. In this Activity, you will find that approximately the same value for V/I is obtained in each case. Thus the V–I graph is a straight line that passes through the origin of the graph, as shown in Fig. 11.3. Thus, V/I is a constant ratio.

In 1827, a German physicist Georg Simon Ohm (1787–1854) found out the relationship between the current I, flowing in a metallic wire and the potential difference across its terminals. The potential difference, V, across the ends of a given metallic wire in an electric circuit is directly proportional to the current flowing through it, provided its temperature remains the same. This is called Ohm’s law. In other words –

V ∝ I (11.4)

or V/I = constant

= R

or V = IR (11.5)

In Eq. (11.4), R is a constant for the given metallic wire at a given temperature and is called its resistance. It is the property of a conductor to resist the flow of charges through it. Its SI unit is ohm, represented by the Greek letter Ω. According to Ohm’s law,

R = V/I (11.6)

If the potential difference across the two ends of a conductor is 1 V and the current through it is 1 A, then the resistance R, of the conductor is 1 Ω. That is, 1 ohm = 1 volt / 1 ampere.

Also from Eq. (11.5) we get

I = V/R (11.7)

It is obvious from Eq. (11.7) that the current through a resistor is inversely proportional to its resistance. If the resistance is doubled the current gets halved. In many practical cases it is necessary to increase or decrease the current in an electric circuit. A component used to regulate current without changing the voltage source is called variable resistance. In an electric circuit, a device called rheostat is often used to change the resistance in the circuit.', 'Ohm''s law states that the potential difference across a metallic wire is directly proportional to the current flowing through it, provided the temperature remains constant. The ratio V/I is constant for a given wire and is called resistance, measured in ohms (Ω). The current through a resistor is inversely proportional to its resistance. Variable resistance devices like rheostats are used to regulate current without changing the voltage.', '["Ohm''s law", "potential difference", "current", "resistance", "rheostat", "variable resistance", "V \u221d I", "\u2126"]'::jsonb, NULL, 11, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', NULL, NULL, '0e501117a2188b71e2a35d25ac4c4578') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (142, 150, NULL, 'example', 'Resistance of a metal wire of length 1 m is 26 Ω at 20°C. If the diameter of the wire is 0.3 mm, what will be the resistivity of the metal at that temperature? Using Table 11.2, predict the material of the wire.
We are given the resistance R of the wire = 26 Ω, the diameter d = 0.3 mm = 3 × 10-4 m, and the length l of the wire = 1 m.
Therefore, from Eq. (11.10), the resistivity of the given metallic wire is

ρ=  (RA/l)  =  (R πd2/4l)

Substitution of values in this gives

ρ=  1.84 × 10–6 Ω m

The resistivity of the metal at 20°C is 1.84 × 10–6 Ω m. From Table 11.2, we see that this is the resistivity of manganese.', 'The resistance of a 1 meter long metal wire with diameter 0.3 mm is given as 26 Ω at 20°C. Using the formula ρ = (RA/l) = (R πd²/4l), the resistivity is calculated as 1.84 × 10–6 Ω m. Comparing this value with standard resistivity values in Table 11.2 identifies the metal as manganese.', '["resistance", "resistivity", "metal wire", "diameter", "length", "manganese", "Table 11.2", "Eq. (11.10)"]'::jsonb, NULL, 19, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["resistance", "resistivity", "formula for resistivity"]'::jsonb, NULL, '8299c92e8d9ea6c8b0e36ce1aaf560bb') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (137, 143, NULL, 'concept', 'In this Activity we observe that the current is different for different components. Why do they differ? Certain components offer an easy path for the flow of electric current while the others resist the flow. We know that motion of electrons in an electric circuit constitutes an electric current. The electrons, however, are not completely free to move within a conductor. They are restrained by the attraction of the atoms among which they move. Thus, motion of electrons through a conductor is retarded by its resistance. A component of a given size that offers a low resistance is a good conductor. A conductor having some appreciable resistance is called a resistor. A component of identical size that offers a higher resistance is a poor conductor. An insulator of the same size offers even higher resistance.', 'Electric current varies among different components because of their differing resistance to electron flow. Electrons are partially restricted by atomic attraction within conductors, causing resistance. Components with low resistance are good conductors, those with appreciable resistance are called resistors, and those with higher resistance are poor conductors. Insulators have the highest resistance among these.', '["electric current", "electrons", "resistance", "conductor", "resistor", "insulator", "electron flow"]'::jsonb, NULL, 12, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["Ohm''s law", "resistance"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'e9918f89cc400bb02d23ee8878cb18b6') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (138, 145, NULL, 'concept', 'Complete an electric circuit consisting of a cell, an ammeter, a nichrome wire of length l [say, marked (1)] and a plug key, as shown in Fig. 11.5. It is observed that the ammeter reading decreases to one-half when the length of the wire is doubled. The ammeter reading is increased when a thicker wire of the same material and of the same length is used in the circuit. A change in ammeter reading is observed when a wire of different material of the same length and the same area of cross-section is used. On applying Ohm’s law [Eqs. (11.5) – (11.7)], we observe that the resistance of the conductor depends (i) on its length, (ii) on its area of cross-section, and (iii) on the nature of its material. Precise measurements have shown that resistance of a uniform metallic conductor is directly proportional to its length (l) and inversely proportional to the area of cross-section (A). That is, R ∝ l (11.8) and R ∝ 1/A (11.9) Combining Eqs. (11.8) and (11.9) we get R ∝ l/A or, R = ρl/A (11.10) where ρ (rho) is a constant of proportionality and is called the electrical resistivity of the material of the conductor. The SI unit of resistivity is Ω m. It is a characteristic property of the material.', 'The resistance of a conductor depends on its length, cross-sectional area, and the material it is made of. Resistance is directly proportional to the length and inversely proportional to the cross-sectional area. The relationship is expressed as R = ρl/A, where ρ is the resistivity, a material-specific constant with SI unit ohm meter (Ω m).', '["resistance", "length", "cross-sectional area", "material", "Ohm\u2019s law", "resistivity", "R = \u03c1l/A"]'::jsonb, NULL, 14, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["Ohm\u2019s law"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'ee985e8d5b34da7fd4e7f6d1daa3e517') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (139, 147, NULL, 'table', 'Electricity have very low resistivity in the range of 10–8 Ω m to 10–6 Ω m. They are good conductors of electricity. Insulators like rubber and glass have resistivity of the order of 1012 to 1017 Ω m. Both the resistance and resistivity of a material vary with temperature. Table 11.2 reveals that the resistivity of an alloy is generally higher than that of its constituent metals. Alloys do not oxidise (burn) readily at high temperatures. For this reason, they are commonly used in electrical heating devices, like electric iron, toasters etc. Tungsten is used almost exclusively for filaments of electric bulbs, whereas copper and aluminium are generally used for electrical transmission lines.

Table 11.2 Electrical resistivity* of some substances at 20°C
Material Resistivity (Ω m)
Conductors
Silver 1.60 × 10–8
Copper 1.62 × 10–8
Aluminium 2.63 × 10–8
Tungsten 5.20 × 10–8
Nickel 6.84 × 10–8
Iron 10.0 × 10–8
Chromium 12.9 × 10–8
Mercury 94.0 × 10–8
Manganese 1.84 × 10–6
Alloys
Constantan 49 × 10–6
(alloy of Cu and Ni)
Manganin 44 × 10–6
(alloy of Cu, Mn and Ni)
Nichrome 100 × 10–6
(alloy of Ni, Cr, Mn and Fe)
Insulators
Glass 1010 – 1014
Hard rubber 1013 – 1016
Ebonite 1015 – 1017
Diamond 1012 - 1013
Paper (dry) 1012
* You need not memorise these values. You can use these values for solving numerical problems.', 'The resistivity of conductors ranges from about 10–8 to 10–6 Ω m, making them good conductors of electricity. Insulators have much higher resistivity values, from 10^12 to 10^17 Ω m. Alloys generally have higher resistivity than their constituent metals and are used in heating devices due to their resistance to oxidation at high temperatures. Tungsten is commonly used for bulb filaments, while copper and aluminium are used for electrical transmission lines.', '["resistivity", "conductors", "alloys", "insulators", "temperature", "electrical heating devices", "tungsten", "copper", "aluminium"]'::jsonb, NULL, 16, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["resistivity"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'a4dbf57487860711cb3bb131020012a9') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (140, 148, NULL, 'example', '(a) How much current will an electric bulb draw from a 220 V source, if the resistance of the bulb filament is 1200 Ω? (b) How much current will an electric heater coil draw from a 220 V source, if the resistance of the heater coil is 100 Ω?

(a) We are given V = 220 V; R = 1200 Ω.

From Eq. (12.6), we have the current I = 220 V / 1200 Ω = 0.18 A.

(b) We are given, V = 220 V, R = 100 Ω.

From Eq. (11.6), we have the current I = 220 V / 100 Ω = 2.2 A.

Note the difference of current drawn by an electric bulb and electric heater from the same 220 V source!', 'The current drawn by an electric bulb with a filament resistance of 1200 Ω from a 220 V source is calculated as 0.18 A using Ohm''s law. Similarly, an electric heater coil with resistance 100 Ω draws a current of 2.2 A from the same 220 V source. This illustrates how different resistances affect the current drawn from the same voltage source.', '["electric bulb", "electric heater", "current", "resistance", "220 V source", "Ohm''s law"]'::jsonb, NULL, 17, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["Ohm''s law", "electric current", "resistance"]'::jsonb, NULL, '04d044303dd154e4f77b2c1f3bb2a485') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (141, 149, NULL, 'example', 'The potential difference between the terminals of an electric heater is 60 V when it draws a current of 4 A from the source. What current will the heater draw if the potential difference is increased to 120 V?

We are given, potential difference V = 60 V, current I = 4 A.

According to Ohm’s law, 60 V = I × R = 4 A × R, so R = 15 Ω.

When the potential difference is increased to 120 V the current is given by

current = 120 V / 15 Ω = 8 A.

The current through the heater becomes 8 A.', 'An electric heater drawing 4 A current at 60 V has a resistance of 15 Ω calculated using Ohm''s law. When the potential difference is increased to 120 V, the current drawn by the heater doubles to 8 A, demonstrating the direct proportionality between voltage and current for a fixed resistance.', '["electric heater", "potential difference", "current", "resistance", "Ohm''s law"]'::jsonb, NULL, 18, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["Ohm''s law", "electric current", "resistance"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'c42d4f962987fa88a881faa1fb707e74') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (143, 151, NULL, 'example', 'A wire of given material having length l and area of cross-section A has a resistance of 4 Ω. What would be the resistance of another wire of the same material having length l/2 and area of cross-section 2 A?

For first wire
R_1 = ρ l / A = 4 Ω

Now for second wire
R_2 = ρ (l/2) / (2A) = ρ l / (4A) = (1/4) R_1 = 1 Ω

The resistance of the new wire is 1 Ω.', 'The resistance of a wire depends on its length and cross-sectional area. For a wire of length l and area A with resistance 4 Ω, a wire of the same material with half the length and twice the cross-sectional area has a resistance of 1 Ω. This is calculated using the formula R = ρ l / A, showing resistance is directly proportional to length and inversely proportional to area.', '["resistance", "wire", "length", "cross-sectional area", "resistivity", "R = \u03c1 l / A"]'::jsonb, NULL, 20, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', NULL, NULL, '3ae724839e2949d31703d84f11121650') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (144, 152, NULL, 'question', '1. On what factors does the resistance of a conductor depend?
2. Will current flow more easily through a thick wire or a thin wire of the same material, when connected to the same source? Why?
3. Let the resistance of an electrical component remain constant while the potential difference across the two ends of the component decreases to half of its former value. What change will occur in the current through it?
4. Why are coils of electric toasters and electric irons made of an alloy rather than a pure metal?
5. Use the data in Table 11.2 to answer the following –
(a) Which among iron and mercury is a better conductor?
(b) Which material is the best conductor?', 'Resistance of a conductor depends on factors such as material, length, and cross-sectional area. Current flows more easily through thicker wires of the same material due to lower resistance. If resistance remains constant and potential difference halves, current also halves according to Ohm''s law. Coils in electric appliances are made of alloys to increase resistance and durability. Conductivity comparisons among materials like iron and mercury determine which is a better conductor.', '["resistance factors", "current flow", "thick wire", "thin wire", "potential difference", "electric toasters", "alloy coils", "conductivity comparison"]'::jsonb, NULL, 21, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["resistance", "current", "Ohm''s law"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'ef6d4b88c6abf2ab21b92e592fa10fe0') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (145, 153, NULL, 'concept', 'In preceding sections, we learnt about some simple electric circuits. We have noticed how the current through a conductor depends upon its resistance and the potential difference across its ends. In various electrical gadgets, we often use resistors in various combinations. We now therefore intend to see how Ohm’s law can be applied to combinations of resistors. There are two methods of joining the resistors together. Figure 11.6 shows an electric circuit in which three resistors having resistances R1, R2 and R3, respectively, are joined end to end. Here the resistors are said to be connected in series.

Figure 11.7 shows a combination of resistors in which three resistors are connected together between points X and Y. Here, the resistors are said to be connected in parallel.', 'Electric circuits often use resistors connected in different ways. Two common methods of joining resistors are series and parallel connections. In a series connection, resistors are joined end to end, and in a parallel connection, resistors are connected between two common points.', '["resistors", "series connection", "parallel connection", "resistance", "potential difference", "Ohm\u2019s law"]'::jsonb, NULL, 22, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["electric current", "resistance", "potential difference"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'cf80382b6ab62ac0a32746f1f494de90') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (146, 154, NULL, 'concept', 'You will observe that the value of the current in the ammeter is the same, independent of its position in the electric circuit. It means that in a series combination of resistors the current is the same in every part of the circuit or the same current through each resistor.', 'In a series combination of resistors, the current flowing through each resistor is the same regardless of the position of the ammeter in the circuit. This uniform current flow is a characteristic of series circuits.', '["current", "series combination", "ammeter", "electric circuit"]'::jsonb, NULL, 23, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["series connection", "electric current"]'::jsonb, NULL, '2d4da7ae4d3a43ad9a461270672d8cc7') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (147, 156, NULL, 'concept', 'In Activity 11.4, insert a voltmeter across the ends X and Y of the series combination of three resistors, as shown in Fig. 11.6. Plug the key in the circuit and note the voltmeter reading. It gives the potential difference across the series combination of resistors. Let it be V. Now measure the potential difference across the two terminals of the battery. Compare the two values. Take out the plug key and disconnect the voltmeter. Now insert the voltmeter across the ends X and P of the first resistor, as shown in Fig. 11.8. Plug the key and measure the potential difference across the first resistor. Let it be V1. Similarly, measure the potential difference across the other two resistors, separately. Let these values be V2 and V3, respectively. Deduce a relationship between V, V1, V2 and V3. You will observe that the potential difference V is equal to the sum of potential differences V1, V2, and V3. That is the total potential difference across a combination of resistors in series is equal to the sum of potential difference across the individual resistors. That is,

V = V1 + V2 + V3 (11.11)

In the electric circuit shown in Fig. 11.8, let I be the current through the circuit. The current through each resistor is also I. It is possible to replace the three resistors joined in series by an equivalent single resistor of resistance R, such that the potential difference V across it, and the current I through the circuit remains the same. Applying the Ohm’s law to the entire circuit, we have

V = I R (11.12)

On applying Ohm’s law to the three resistors separately, we further have

V1 = I R1 [11.13(a)]
V2 = I R2 [11.13(b)]
and V3 = I R3 [11.13(c)]

From Eq. (11.11),

I R = I R1 + I R2 + I R3

or

Rs = R1 + R2 + R3 (11.14)

We can conclude that the equivalent resistance Rs of resistors connected in series is the sum of their individual resistances.', 'The total potential difference across resistors connected in series is equal to the sum of the potential differences across each resistor. The current through each resistor in series is the same. Using Ohm’s law, the equivalent resistance of resistors in series is the sum of their individual resistances, expressed as Rs = R1 + R2 + R3.', '["potential difference", "series resistors", "equivalent resistance", "Ohm\u2019s law", "current", "voltmeter"]'::jsonb, NULL, 25, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["series connection", "Ohm\u2019s law", "electric current", "potential difference"]'::jsonb, NULL, '4a0b289c2536d4699d58aae9350360f9') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (148, 157, NULL, 'example', 'An electric lamp, whose resistance is 20 Ω, and a conductor of 4 Ω resistance are connected to a 6 V battery (Fig. 11.9). Calculate (a) the total resistance of the circuit, (b) the current through the circuit, and (c) the potential difference across the electric lamp and conductor.
The resistance of electric lamp, R1 = 20 Ω.
The resistance of the conductor connected in series, R2 = 4 Ω.
Then the total resistance in the circuit

R = R1 + R2
Rs = 20 Ω + 4 Ω = 24 Ω.

The total potential difference across the two terminals of the battery
V = 6 V.
Now by Ohm’s law, the current through the circuit is given by

I = V / Rs
= 6 V / 24 Ω
= 0.25 A.

Applying Ohm’s law to the electric lamp and conductor separately, we get potential difference across the electric lamp,

V1 = 20 Ω × 0.25 A
= 5 V;
and,
that across the conductor, V2 = 4 Ω × 0.25 A
= 1 V.

Suppose that we like to replace the series combination of electric lamp and conductor by a single and equivalent resistor. Its resistance must be such that a potential difference of 6 V across the battery terminals will cause a current of 0.25 A in the circuit. The resistance R of this equivalent resistor would be

R = V / I
= 6 V / 0.25 A
= 24 Ω.
This is the total resistance of the series circuit; it is equal to the sum of the two resistances.', 'An electric lamp with resistance 20 Ω and a conductor with resistance 4 Ω are connected in series to a 6 V battery. The total resistance of the circuit is the sum of the two resistances, 24 Ω. Using Ohm''s law, the current through the circuit is calculated as 0.25 A. The potential difference across the lamp is 5 V and across the conductor is 1 V. The series combination can be replaced by a single equivalent resistor of 24 Ω to maintain the same current and potential difference.', '["electric lamp", "resistance", "series circuit", "Ohm\u2019s law", "potential difference", "current", "equivalent resistor"]'::jsonb, NULL, 26, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', NULL, NULL, '2d7a8bf0e8253437c22e1e4fca1d55f9') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (149, 158, NULL, 'question', '1. Draw a schematic diagram of a circuit consisting of a battery of three cells of 2 V each, a 5 Ω resistor, an 8 Ω resistor, and a 12 Ω resistor, and a plug key, all connected in series.
2. Redraw the circuit of Question 1, putting in an ammeter to measure the current through the resistors and a voltmeter to measure the potential difference across the 12 Ω resistor. What would be the readings in the ammeter and the voltmeter?', 'The questions ask to draw a series circuit with a battery of three 2 V cells, three resistors of 5 Ω, 8 Ω, and 12 Ω, and a plug key. Then, the circuit is to be redrawn including an ammeter to measure current and a voltmeter to measure the potential difference across the 12 Ω resistor. The expected readings of these instruments are to be determined.', '["series circuit", "battery", "resistor", "ammeter", "voltmeter", "potential difference", "current measurement"]'::jsonb, NULL, 27, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["Calculation of Total Resistance, Current, and Potential Difference in a Series Circuit"]'::jsonb, NULL, '65d8dcc99d139049b15de3864133f2de') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (150, 158, 22, 'concept', 'Now, let us consider the arrangement of three resistors joined in parallel with a combination of cells (or a battery), as shown in Fig.11.7.', 'This section introduces the concept of connecting three resistors in parallel with a battery or combination of cells. It sets the stage for analyzing the electrical behavior of such a parallel resistor network.', '["resistors", "parallel connection", "battery", "combination of cells", "Fig.11.7"]'::jsonb, NULL, 28, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'e0b8f46ed858fede4fa6ad72c5b148ad') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (151, 160, NULL, 'equation_block', 'I = I1 + I2 + I3(11.15)

Let Rp be the equivalent resistance of the parallel combination of resistors. By applying Ohm’s law to the parallel combination of resistors, we have

I = V/Rp(11.16)

On applying Ohm’s law to each resistor, we have

I1 = V /R1;    I2 = V /R2; and I3 = V /R3(11.17)

From Eqs. (11.15) to (11.17), we have

V/Rp = V/R1 + V/R2 + V/R3

or

1/Rp = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3(11.18)

Thus, we may conclude that the reciprocal of the equivalent resistance of a group of resistances joined in parallel is equal to the sum of the reciprocals of the individual resistances.', 'The total current in a parallel resistor circuit is the sum of the currents through each resistor. Using Ohm''s law, the equivalent resistance Rp of the parallel combination satisfies the relation 1/Rp = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3. This means the reciprocal of the equivalent resistance is the sum of the reciprocals of the individual resistances.', '["equivalent resistance", "parallel resistors", "Ohm''s law", "current", "resistance formula"]'::jsonb, NULL, 30, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["Current and Voltage in Parallel Resistor Combinations"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'c9340b909809d5c783ef06d8e76383b0') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (152, 161, NULL, 'example', 'In the circuit diagram given in Fig. 11.10, suppose the resistors R1, R2 and R3 have the values 5 Ω, 10 Ω, 30 Ω, respectively, which have been connected to a battery of 12 V. Calculate (a) the current through each resistor, (b) the total current in the circuit, and (c) the total circuit resistance.
R1 = 5 Ω, R2 = 10 Ω, and R3 = 30 Ω.
Potential difference across the battery, V = 12 V.
This is also the potential difference across each of the individual resistor; therefore, to calculate the current in the resistors, we use Ohm’s law.

The current I1, through R1 = V/ R1
I1 = 12 V / 5 Ω = 2.4 A.

The current I2, through R2 = V / R2
I2 = 12 V / 10 Ω = 1.2 A.

The current I3, through R3 = V / R3
I3 = 12 V / 30 Ω = 0.4 A.

The total current in the circuit,
I = I1 + I2 + I3 = (2.4 + 1.2 + 0.4) A = 4 A.

The total resistance Rp, is given by [Eq. (11.18)]
1/Rp = 1/5 + 1/10 + 1/30

Thus, Rp = 3 Ω.', 'The example calculates the current through each resistor connected in parallel to a 12 V battery using Ohm''s law. The currents through resistors of 5 Ω, 10 Ω, and 30 Ω are found to be 2.4 A, 1.2 A, and 0.4 A respectively. The total current in the circuit is the sum of these currents, equal to 4 A. The total resistance of the parallel circuit is calculated using the reciprocal sum formula, resulting in 3 Ω.', '["resistors", "Ohm\u2019s law", "current", "potential difference", "parallel circuit", "total resistance", "battery", "circuit"]'::jsonb, NULL, 31, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["Ohm\u2019s law", "concept of resistance", "parallel circuits"]'::jsonb, NULL, '1f6135ff028ba4d2cb4ace88ef14f7fd') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (153, 162, NULL, 'example', 'If in Fig. 11.12, R1 = 10 Ω, R2 = 40 Ω, R3 = 30 Ω, R4 = 20 Ω, R5 = 60 Ω, and a 12 V battery is connected to the arrangement. Calculate (a) the total resistance in the circuit, and (b) the total current flowing in the circuit. Suppose we replace the parallel resistors R1 and R2 by an equivalent resistor of resistance, R′. Similarly we replace the parallel resistors R3, R4 and R5 by an equivalent single resistor of resistance R″. Then using Eq. (11.18), we have

1/ R′ = 1/10 + 1/40 = 5/40; that is R′ = 8 Ω.
Similarly, 1/ R″ = 1/30 + 1/20 + 1/60 = 6/60;

that is, R″ = 10 Ω.

Thus, the total resistance, R = R′ + R″ = 18 Ω.

To calculate the current, we use Ohm’s law, and get

I = V/R = 12 V/18 Ω = 0.67 A.', 'The total resistance of a circuit combining series and parallel resistors is calculated by first finding the equivalent resistance of parallel groups and then adding them in series. For the given resistors, the equivalent resistances are R′ = 8 Ω and R″ = 10 Ω, resulting in a total resistance of 18 Ω. Using Ohm''s law, the total current flowing through the circuit with a 12 V battery is 0.67 A.', '["total resistance", "parallel resistors", "series resistors", "Ohm\u2019s law", "equivalent resistance", "current calculation"]'::jsonb, NULL, 32, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["Ohm\u2019s law", "series and parallel resistor combinations"]'::jsonb, NULL, '8e91653be9013231859665770e3a3f43') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (154, 163, NULL, 'concept', 'We have seen that in a series circuit the current is constant throughout the electric circuit. Thus it is obviously impracticable to connect an electric bulb and an electric heater in series, because they need currents of widely different values to operate properly (see Example 11.3). Another major disadvantage of a series circuit is that when one component fails the circuit is broken and none of the components works. If you have used ‘fairy lights’ to decorate buildings on festivals, on marriage celebrations etc., you might have seen the electrician spending lot of time in trouble-locating and replacing the ‘dead’ bulb – each has to be tested to find which has fused or gone. On the other hand, a parallel circuit divides the current through the electrical gadgets. The total resistance in a parallel circuit is decreased as per Eq. (11.18). This is helpful particularly when each gadget has different resistance and requires different current to operate properly.', 'In a series circuit, the current is the same through all components, making it unsuitable for devices requiring different currents. A major drawback is that if one component fails, the entire circuit stops working. Parallel circuits divide the current among devices, lowering total resistance and allowing devices with different resistances to operate properly. This makes parallel circuits more practical for household electrical wiring.', '["series circuit", "parallel circuit", "current division", "total resistance", "component failure", "electrical gadgets"]'::jsonb, NULL, 33, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["current in series circuits", "resistance in parallel circuits"]'::jsonb, NULL, '8e522ebd7f77d16be23a4df64e2a3808') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (155, 164, NULL, 'concept', 'We know that a battery or a cell is a source of electrical energy. The chemical reaction within the cell generates the potential difference between its two terminals that sets the electrons in motion to flow the current through a resistor or a system of resistors connected to the battery. We have also seen, in Section 11.2, that to maintain the current, the source has to keep expending its energy. Where does this energy go? A part of the source energy in maintaining the current may be consumed into useful work (like in rotating the blades of an electric fan). Rest of the source energy may be expended in heat to raise the temperature of gadget. We often observe this in our everyday life. For example, an electric fan becomes warm if used continuously for longer time etc. On the other hand, if the electric circuit is purely resistive, that is, a configuration of resistors only connected to a battery; the source energy continually gets dissipated entirely in the form of heat. This is known as the heating effect of electric current. This effect is utilised in devices such as electric heater, electric iron etc.

Consider a current I flowing through a resistor of resistance R. Let the potential difference across it be V (Fig. 11.13). Let t be the time during which a charge Q flows across. The work done in moving the charge Q through a potential difference V is VQ. Therefore, the source must supply energy equal to VQ in time t. Hence the power input to the circuit by the source is

P = VQ/t = VI (11.19)

Or the energy supplied to the circuit by the source in time t is P × t, that is, VIt. What happens to this energy expended by the source? This energy gets dissipated in the resistor as heat. Thus for a steady current I, the amount of heat H produced in time t is

H = VIt (11.20)', 'A battery generates a potential difference through chemical reactions, causing current to flow in a circuit. The energy supplied by the source is partly used for useful work and partly dissipated as heat, especially in purely resistive circuits. The heating effect of electric current is the conversion of electrical energy into heat energy in resistors, which is utilized in devices like electric heaters and irons. The power supplied by the source is given by P = VI, and the heat produced in time t is H = VIt.', '["battery", "potential difference", "electric current", "heating effect", "power", "energy dissipation", "resistor"]'::jsonb, NULL, 34, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["electric current", "potential difference", "Ohm\u2019s law"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'c09628fbb742a4c4a6bf3904300f00d3') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (156, 165, NULL, 'question', '1. Judge the equivalent resistance when the following are connected in parallel – (a) 1 Ω and 10^6 Ω, (b) 1 Ω and 10^3 Ω, and 10^6 Ω.
2. An elect', 'The questions ask to determine the equivalent resistance of various resistors connected in parallel, specifically for combinations involving resistors of 1 Ω, 10^3 Ω, and 10^6 Ω. The second question is incomplete.', '["equivalent resistance", "parallel connection", "resistors", "resistance calculation"]'::jsonb, NULL, 35, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["parallel resistors", "equivalent resistance calculation"]'::jsonb, NULL, '97c9f9039d4b7a40b865b1aed483f6f6') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (157, 166, NULL, 'example', 'An electric iron consumes energy at a rate of 840 W when heating is at the maximum rate and 360 W when the heating is at the minimum. The voltage is 220 V. What are the current and the resistance in each case? From Eq. (11.19), we know that the power input is P = V I. Thus the current I = P/V.

(a) When heating is at the maximum rate,

I = 840 W/220 V = 3.82 A;

and the resistance of the electric iron is

R = V/I = 220 V/3.82 A = 57.60 Ω.

(b) When heating is at the minimum rate,

I = 360 W/220 V = 1.64 A;

and the resistance of the electric iron is

R = V/I = 220 V/1.64 A = 134.15 Ω.', 'The electric iron operates at two power levels: 840 W (maximum heating) and 360 W (minimum heating) with a constant voltage of 220 V. Using the formula P = V I, the current at maximum power is calculated as 3.82 A and the resistance as 57.60 Ω. At minimum power, the current is 1.64 A and the resistance is 134.15 Ω, showing that resistance varies with the heating rate.', '["electric iron", "power", "voltage", "current", "resistance", "maximum heating", "minimum heating", "P = V I"]'::jsonb, NULL, 36, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["electric power formula", "Ohm''s law"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'b446c0716f8c418d4ee1b27e4037bd34') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (158, 167, NULL, 'example', '100 J of heat is produced each second in a 4 Ω resistance. Find the potential difference across the resistor.
H = 100 J, R = 4 Ω, t = 1 s, V = ?
From Eq. (11.21) we have the current through the resistor as

I = √(H/Rt )
= √[100 J/(4 Ω × 1 s)]
= 5 A
Thus the potential difference across the resistor, V [from Eq. (11.5)] is
V = IR
= 5 A × 4 Ω
= 20 V.', 'When 100 joules of heat is produced each second in a 4 ohm resistor, the current through the resistor is calculated using the formula I = √(H/Rt), resulting in 5 amperes. Using Ohm''s law, the potential difference across the resistor is then found as V = IR = 20 volts.', '["heat produced", "resistor", "potential difference", "current", "Ohm''s law", "joule heating"]'::jsonb, NULL, 37, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["electric current", "Ohm''s law", "heat produced by current"]'::jsonb, NULL, '15aa3717e0c7069fce14f331e608393a') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (159, 168, NULL, 'concept', 'The generation of heat in a conductor is an inevitable consequence of electric current. In many cases, it is undesirable as it converts useful electrical energy into heat. In electric circuits, the unavoidable heating can increase the temperature of the components and alter their properties. However, heating effect of electric current has many useful applications. The electric laundry iron, electric toaster, electric oven, electric kettle and electric heater are some of the familiar devices based on Joule’s heating.
The electric heating is also used to produce light, as in an electric bulb. Here, the filament must retain as much of the heat generated as is possible, so that it gets very hot and emits light. It must not melt at such high temperature. A strong metal with high melting point such as tungsten (melting point 3380°C) is used for making bulb filaments. The filament should be thermally isolated as much as possible, using insulating support, etc. The bulbs are usually filled with chemically inactive nitrogen and argon gases to prolong the life of filament. Most of the power consumed by the filament appears as heat, but a small part of it is in the form of light radiated.
Another common application of Joule’s heating is the fuse used in electric circuits. It protects circuits and appliances by stopping the flow of any unduly high electric current. The fuse is placed in series with the device. It consists of a piece of wire made of a metal or an alloy of appropriate melting point, for example aluminium, copper, iron, lead etc. If a current larger than the specified value flows through the circuit, the temperature of the fuse wire increases. This melts the fuse wire and breaks the circuit. The fuse wire is usually encased in a cartridge of porcelain or similar material with metal ends. The fuses used for domestic purposes are rated as 1 A, 2 A, 3 A, 5 A, 10 A, etc. For an electric iron which consumes 1 kW electric power when operated at 220 V, a current of (1000/220) A, that is, 4.54 A will flow in the circuit. In this case, a 5 A fuse must be used.', 'Electric current inevitably produces heat in conductors, which can be undesirable but also has many practical uses. Devices such as electric irons, toasters, ovens, kettles, and heaters utilize Joule''s heating effect. Electric bulbs use heating of tungsten filaments to produce light, with filaments designed to withstand high temperatures and insulated to prolong life. Fuses protect circuits by melting when excessive current flows, breaking the circuit to prevent damage.', '["Joule''s heating", "electric iron", "electric bulb filament", "tungsten", "fuse", "electric current", "heat generation"]'::jsonb, NULL, 38, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["electric current", "heat produced by current"]'::jsonb, NULL, '71ee584ab502c402c43469e9091e635c') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (160, 169, NULL, 'question', '1. Why does the cord of an electric heater not glow while the heating element does?
2. Compute the heat generated while transferring 96000 coulomb of charge in one hour through a potential difference of 50 V.
3. An electric iron of resistance 20 Ω takes a current of 5 A. Calculate the heat developed in 30 s.', 'These questions test understanding of the heating effect of electric current, including why certain parts of an electric heater do not glow, calculation of heat generated by charge transfer through a potential difference, and heat developed in a resistor over time.', '["heating element", "electric heater", "heat calculation", "charge transfer", "resistance", "current"]'::jsonb, NULL, 39, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["heating effect of electric current", "Ohm''s law", "heat produced by current"]'::jsonb, NULL, '2b304093e794e1f114b46e65e37cda68') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (161, 170, NULL, 'concept', 'You have studied in your earlier Class that the rate of doing work is power. This is also the rate of consumption of energy.
Equation (11.21) gives the rate at which electric energy is dissipated or consumed in an electric circuit. This is also termed as electric power.
The power P is given by
P = VI
Or P = I2R = V2/R (11.22)
The SI unit of electric power is watt (W). It is the power consumed by a device that carries 1 A of current when operated at a potential difference of 1 V. Thus,

1 W = 1 volt × 1 ampere = 1 V A (11.23)

The unit ‘watt’ is very small. Therefore, in actual practice we use a much larger unit called ‘kilowatt’. It is equal to 1000 watts. Since electrical energy is the product of power and time, the unit of electric energy is, therefore, watt hour (Wh). One watt hour is the energy consumed when 1 watt of power is used for 1 hour.', 'Electric power is the rate of doing work or consuming energy in an electric circuit. It is given by P = VI, or equivalently P = I²R or P = V²/R. The SI unit of power is the watt, defined as one volt times one ampere. Larger units like kilowatt (1000 watts) are commonly used. Electric energy is measured in watt-hours, representing power consumption over time.', '["electric power", "power formula", "watt", "kilowatt", "electric energy", "watt hour"]'::jsonb, NULL, 40, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["electric current", "potential difference", "Ohm''s law"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'b14b9fc523df8d2be0ea27ffb82f7400') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (162, 171, NULL, 'example', 'An electric refrigerator rated 400 W operates 8 hour/day. What is the cost of the energy to operate it for 30 days at Rs 3.00 per kW h?

Solution

The total energy consumed by the refrigerator in 30 days would be

400 W × 8.0 hour/day × 30 days = 96000 W h = 96 kW h

Thus the cost of energy to operate the refrigerator for 30 days is

96 kW h × Rs 3.00 per kW h = Rs 288.00', 'A 400 W electric refrigerator operating 8 hours daily consumes 96 kWh of energy in 30 days. At a rate of Rs 3.00 per kWh, the total cost to operate the refrigerator for 30 days is Rs 288.00.', '["electric refrigerator", "power rating", "energy consumption", "kilowatt hour", "cost calculation"]'::jsonb, NULL, 41, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["electrical power", "energy consumption", "units of electrical energy"]'::jsonb, NULL, '545cc99b24ba0bb338b3825ec161c3f6') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (163, 172, NULL, 'concept', 'A stream of electrons moving through a conductor constitutes an electric current. Conventionally, the direction of current is taken opposite to the direction of flow of electrons. The SI unit of electric current is ampere. To set the electrons in motion in an electric circuit, we use a cell or a battery. A cell generates a potential difference across its terminals. It is measured in volts (V). Resistance is a property that resists the flow of electrons in a conductor. It controls the magnitude of the current. The SI unit of resistance is ohm (Ω). Ohm’s law: The potential difference across the ends of a resistor is directly proportional to the current through it, provided its temperature remains the same. The resistance of a conductor depends directly on its length, inversely on its area of cross-section, and also on the material of the conductor. The equivalent resistance of several resistors in series is equal to the sum of their individual resistances. A set of resistors connected in parallel has an equivalent resistance Rp given by

1/Rp = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3 + ...

The electrical energy dissipated in a resistor is given by

W = V × I × t

The unit of power is watt (W). One watt of power is consumed when 1 A of current flows at a potential difference of 1 V. The commercial unit of electrical energy is kilowatt hour (kWh).

1 kW h = 3,600,000 J = 3.6 × 10^6 J.', 'Electric current is the flow of electrons through a conductor, with current direction defined opposite to electron flow. Current is measured in amperes, and potential difference in volts. Resistance opposes electron flow and is measured in ohms. Ohm’s law states that voltage across a resistor is proportional to current if temperature is constant. Resistance depends on length, cross-sectional area, and material. Equivalent resistance in series is the sum of resistances; in parallel, it is given by the reciprocal sum formula. Electrical energy dissipated is calculated by W = V × I × t. Power is measured in watts, and electrical energy in kilowatt hours, where 1 kWh equals 3.6 million joules.', '["electric current", "ampere", "potential difference", "resistance", "Ohm\u2019s law", "series resistance", "parallel resistance", "electrical energy", "power", "kilowatt hour"]'::jsonb, NULL, 42, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', NULL, NULL, '6e2ecdffe553f44f6b320239aaeaec50') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (164, 173, NULL, 'question', '1. A piece of wire of resistance R is cut into five equal parts. These parts are then connected in parallel. If the equivalent resistance of this combination is R′, then the ratio R/R′ is –
(a) 1/25 (b) 1/5 (c) 5 (d) 25

2. Which of the following terms does not represent electrical power in a circuit?
(a) I^2R (b) IR^2 (c) VI (d) V^2/R

3. An electric bulb is rated 220 V and 100 W. When it is operated on 110 V, the power consumed will be –
(a) 100 W (b) 75 W (c) 50 W (d) 25 W

4. Two conducting wires of the same material and of equal lengths and equal diameters are first connected in series and then parallel in a circuit across the same potential difference. The ratio of heat produced in series and parallel combinations would be –
(a) 1:2 (b) 2:1 (c) 1:4 (d) 4:1

5. How is a voltmeter connected in the circuit to measure the potential difference between two points?

6. A copper wire has diameter 0.5 mm and resistivity of 1.6 × 10–8 Ω m. What will be the length of this wire to make its resistance 10 Ω? How much does the resistance change if the diameter is doubled?

7. The values of current I flowing in a given resistor for the corresponding values of potential difference V across the resistor are given below –
I (amperes): 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0
V (volts): 1.6, 3.4, 6.7, 10.2, 13.2
Plot a graph between V and I and calculate the resistance of that resistor.

8. When a 12 V battery is connected across an unknown resistor, there is a current of 2.5 mA in the circuit. Find the value of the resistance of the resistor.

9. A battery of 9 V is connected in series with resistors of 0.2 Ω, 0.3 Ω, 0.4 Ω, 0.5 Ω, and 12 Ω, respectively. How much current would flow through the 12 Ω resistor?

10. How many 176 Ω resistors (in parallel) are required to carry 5 A on a 220 V line?

11. Show how you would connect three resistors, each of resistance 6 Ω, so that the combination has a resistance', 'The exercises include problems on calculating equivalent resistance when a wire is cut and connected in parallel, identifying incorrect expressions for electrical power, determining power consumption of bulbs at different voltages, comparing heat produced in series and parallel wire connections, and understanding voltmeter connections. Additional problems involve calculating wire length from resistance and resistivity, plotting voltage-current graphs to find resistance, finding resistance from current and voltage, calculating current through resistors in series, determining the number of resistors needed in parallel for a given current, and configuring resistors to achieve a desired resistance.', '["resistance", "parallel connection", "electrical power", "voltmeter", "resistivity", "current", "voltage", "series connection", "heat produced"]'::jsonb, NULL, 43, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00', '["electric current", "resistance", "Ohm\u2019s law", "series and parallel circuits"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'd36abf6c86cee1534050df23aa1fb8b6') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (165, 174, NULL, 'concept', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current

In the previous Chapter on ‘Electricity’ we learnt about the heating effects of electric current. What could be the other effects of electric current? We know that an electric current-carrying wire behaves like a magnet. Let us perform the following Activity to reinforce it.', 'Electric current not only produces heating effects but also exhibits magnetic effects. A wire carrying electric current behaves like a magnet, demonstrating the magnetic influence of electric current.', '["electric current", "magnetic effects", "electric current-carrying wire", "magnet"]'::jsonb, NULL, 0, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["heating effects of electric current"]'::jsonb, NULL, '3ec020484b888b79e85f7fb59cac89fc') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (166, 176, NULL, 'concept', 'We see that the needle is deflected. What does it mean? It means that the electric current through the copper wire has produced a magnetic effect. Thus we can say that electricity and magnetism are linked to each other. Then, what about the reverse possibility of an electric effect of moving magnets? In this Chapter we will study magnetic fields and such electromagnetic effects. We shall also study about electromagnets which involve the magnetic effect of electric current.

Hans Christian Oersted (1777–1851)
Hans Christian Oersted, one of the leading scientists of the 19th century, played a crucial role in understanding electromagnetism. In 1820 he accidentally discovered that a compass needle got deflected when an electric current passed through a metallic wire placed nearby. Through this observation Oersted showed that electricity and magnetism were related phenomena. His research later created technologies such as the radio, television and fiber optics. The unit of magnetic field strength is named the oersted in his honor.', 'Electric current produces a magnetic effect, linking electricity and magnetism. Hans Christian Oersted discovered in 1820 that a compass needle deflects when placed near a current-carrying wire, proving the relationship between electricity and magnetism. His work laid the foundation for technologies like radio and television. The magnetic field strength unit ''oersted'' is named after him.', '["electric current", "magnetic effect", "Hans Christian Oersted", "electromagnetism", "compass needle", "magnetic field strength", "oersted"]'::jsonb, NULL, 2, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["Deflection of Compass Needle by Electric Current in a Copper Wire"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'bcc9dd76a084bac0dd7c97bef1c2003d') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (167, 177, NULL, 'concept', 'We are familiar with the fact that a compass needle gets deflected when brought near a bar magnet. A compass needle is, in fact, a small bar magnet. The ends of the compass needle point approximately towards north and south directions. The end pointing towards north is called north seeking or north pole. The other end that points towards south is called south seeking or south pole. Through various activities we have observed that like poles repel, while unlike poles of magnets attract each other.', 'A compass needle is a small bar magnet with a north-seeking pole and a south-seeking pole. It aligns itself along the Earth''s magnetic field, pointing approximately north and south. Like magnetic poles repel each other, whereas unlike poles attract each other.', '["compass needle", "bar magnet", "north pole", "south pole", "magnetic poles", "attraction", "repulsion"]'::jsonb, NULL, 3, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', NULL, NULL, '8d58a6ab598c472b11f310bc98ee7553') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (168, 178, NULL, 'question', 'Why does a compass needle get deflected when brought near a bar magnet?', 'A compass needle deflects near a bar magnet because the magnetic field of the bar magnet exerts a force on the magnetic needle, causing it to align with the magnet''s field.', '["compass needle", "deflection", "bar magnet", "magnetic field"]'::jsonb, NULL, 4, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["Magnetic Poles of a Compass Needle and Their Interaction"]'::jsonb, NULL, '61ca663ff44cb55495c8d46a36e349f9') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (169, 181, NULL, 'concept', 'Magnetic field is a quantity that has both direction and magnitude. The direction of the magnetic field is taken to be the direction in which a north pole of the compass needle moves inside it. Therefore it is taken by convention that the field lines emerge from north pole and merge at the south pole (note the arrows marked on the field lines in Fig. 12.4). Inside the magnet, the direction of field lines is from its south pole to its north pole. Thus the magnetic field lines are closed curves. The relative strength of the magnetic field is shown by the degree of closeness of the field lines. The field is stronger, that is, the force acting on the pole of another magnet placed is greater where the field lines are crowded (see Fig. 12.4). No two field-lines are found to cross each other. If they did, it would mean that at the point of intersection, the compass needle would point towards two directions, which is not possible.', 'Magnetic fields have both direction and magnitude, with field lines conventionally drawn from the north pole to the south pole outside a magnet and from south to north inside it, forming closed loops. The strength of the magnetic field is indicated by the density of these lines, with closer lines representing stronger fields. Magnetic field lines never cross each other because that would imply conflicting directions of the magnetic field at a point.', '["magnetic field", "field lines", "north pole", "south pole", "magnetic strength", "closed curves"]'::jsonb, NULL, 7, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["compass", "bar magnet", "magnetic poles"]'::jsonb, NULL, '435eb45823098bc1540bd1d94fe7095c') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (170, 182, NULL, 'concept', 'In Activity 12.1, we have seen that an electric current through a metallic conductor produces a magnetic field around it. In order to find the direction of the field produced let us repeat the activity in the following way – Figure 12.3

Drawing a magnetic field line with the help of a compass needle:
- Mark the position of two ends of the needle.
- Now move the needle to a new position such that its south pole occupies the position previously occupied by its north pole.
- In this way, proceed step by step till you reach the south pole of the magnet as shown in Fig. 12.3.
- Join the points marked on the paper by a smooth curve. This curve represents a field line.
- Repeat the above procedure and draw as many lines as you can. You will get a pattern shown in Fig. 12.4. These lines represent the magnetic field around the magnet. These are known as magnetic field lines.
- Observe the deflection in the compass needle as you move it along a field line. The deflection increases as the needle is moved towards the poles.

Figure 12.4
Field lines around a bar magnet', 'An electric current through a metallic conductor produces a magnetic field around it. The direction of this magnetic field can be traced using a compass needle by marking successive positions of the needle as it aligns with the field. Connecting these points forms magnetic field lines, which represent the pattern of the magnetic field around the conductor or magnet. The deflection of the compass needle increases as it approaches the poles of the magnet.', '["electric current", "metallic conductor", "magnetic field", "compass needle", "magnetic field lines", "deflection", "bar magnet"]'::jsonb, NULL, 8, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', NULL, NULL, '86b3907b57c935c5205017f28d52f5d0') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (171, 182, 23, 'concept', 'Imagine that you are holding a current-carrying straight conductor in your right hand such that the thumb points towards the direction of current. Then your fingers will wrap around the conductor in the direction of the field lines of the magnetic field, as shown in Fig. 12.7. This is known as the right-hand thumb rule *.', 'The right-hand thumb rule states that if a current-carrying straight conductor is held in the right hand with the thumb pointing in the direction of current, the curled fingers indicate the direction of the magnetic field lines around the conductor. This rule helps determine the orientation of magnetic fields generated by electric currents.', '["right-hand thumb rule", "magnetic field direction", "current-carrying conductor", "magnetic field lines"]'::jsonb, NULL, 12, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["magnetic field lines", "current direction"]'::jsonb, NULL, '36db10788061bb4832f381f336703d4f') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (172, 185, NULL, 'example', 'A current through a horizontal power line flows in east to west direction. What is the direction of magnetic field at a point directly below it and at a point directly above it?
Solution
The current is in the east-west direction. Applying the right-hand thumb rule, we get that the magnetic field (at any point below or above the wire) turns clockwise in a plane perpendicular to the wire, when viewed from the east end, and anti-clockwise, when viewed from the west end.
Right-hand thumb rule
*This rule is also called Maxwell’s corkscrew rule. If we consider ourselves driving a corkscrew in the direction of the current, then the direction of the rotation of corkscrew is the direction of the magnetic field.', 'The magnetic field around a current-carrying horizontal wire flowing from east to west can be determined using the right-hand thumb rule. The magnetic field lines form concentric circles around the wire, turning clockwise below or above the wire when viewed from the east end, and anti-clockwise when viewed from the west end. This rule is also known as Maxwell’s corkscrew rule, where the direction of rotation of a corkscrew driven in the current''s direction indicates the magnetic field direction.', '["current", "horizontal wire", "east-west direction", "magnetic field", "right-hand thumb rule", "Maxwell\u2019s corkscrew rule"]'::jsonb, NULL, 13, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', NULL, NULL, '8259dc35e55eeed6a63351f5eb695a79') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (173, 186, NULL, 'concept', 'We have so far observed the pattern of the magnetic field lines produced around a current-carrying straight wire. Suppose this straight wire is bent in the form of a circular loop and a current is passed through it. How would the magnetic field lines look like? We know that the magnetic field produced by a current-carrying straight wire depends inversely on the distance from it. Similarly at every point of a current-carrying circular loop, the concentric circles representing the magnetic field around it would become larger and larger as we move away from the wire (Fig. 12.8). By the time we reach at the centre of the circular loop, the arcs of these big circles would appear as straight lines. Every point on the wire carrying current would give rise to the magnetic field appearing as straight lines at the center of the loop. By applying the right hand rule, it is easy to check that every section of the wire contributes to the magnetic field lines in the same direction within the loop.', 'When a straight current-carrying wire is bent into a circular loop, the magnetic field lines around the loop form concentric circles that increase in size with distance from the wire. At the center of the loop, these arcs appear as straight lines, and the magnetic field contributions from all parts of the loop add up in the same direction. The magnetic field strength depends inversely on the distance from the wire, and the right-hand rule helps determine the direction of the magnetic field within the loop.', '["current-carrying wire", "circular loop", "magnetic field lines", "concentric circles", "right-hand rule", "magnetic field direction"]'::jsonb, NULL, 14, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["right-hand thumb rule", "magnetic field around straight wire"]'::jsonb, NULL, '0cd6cd055a1710a0156f82efab5c86cd') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (174, 187, NULL, 'concept', 'We know that the magnetic field produced by a current-carrying wire at a given point depends directly on the current passing through it. Therefore, if there is a circular coil having n turns, the field produced is n times as large as that produced by a single turn. This is because the current in each circular turn has the same direction, and the field due to each turn then just adds up.', 'The magnetic field produced by a current-carrying wire is directly proportional to the current. In a circular coil with n turns, the magnetic field strength at a point is n times the field produced by a single turn because the fields from each turn add up due to the current flowing in the same direction in all turns.', '["magnetic field", "current-carrying wire", "circular coil", "multiple turns", "field strength", "current"]'::jsonb, NULL, 15, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["magnetic field around circular loop", "current"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'a0e881b325440b8b8a121fc74f8442b9') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (175, 188, NULL, 'question', 'QUESTIONS
1. Draw magnetic field lines around a bar magnet.
2. List the properties of magnetic field lines.
3. Why don’t two magnetic field lines intersect each other?', 'The questions ask to draw magnetic field lines around a bar magnet, list the properties of magnetic field lines, and explain why two magnetic field lines do not intersect. These questions focus on understanding the nature and behavior of magnetic field lines.', '["magnetic field lines", "bar magnet", "properties", "intersection"]'::jsonb, NULL, 16, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["magnetic field lines"]'::jsonb, NULL, '4b57d3429f2aae9b0b4cb306b462008f') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (176, 188, 24, 'concept', 'A coil of many circular turns of insulated copper wire wrapped closely in the shape of a cylinder is called a solenoid. The pattern of the magnetic field lines around a current-carrying solenoid is shown in Fig. 12.10. Compare the pattern of the field with the magnetic field around a bar magnet (Fig. 12.4). Do they look similar? Yes, they are similar. In fact, one end of the solenoid behaves as a magnetic north pole, while the other behaves as the south pole. The field lines inside the solenoid are in the form of parallel straight lines. This indicates that the magnetic field is the same at all points inside the solenoid. That is, the field is uniform inside the solenoid.
A strong magnetic field produced inside a solenoid can be used to magnetise a piece of magnetic material, like soft iron, when placed inside the coil (Fig. 12.11). The magnet so formed is called an electromagnet.', 'A solenoid is a cylindrical coil of insulated copper wire with many circular turns. The magnetic field pattern around a current-carrying solenoid resembles that of a bar magnet, with one end acting as a north pole and the other as a south pole. Inside the solenoid, the magnetic field lines are parallel and straight, indicating a uniform magnetic field. This strong magnetic field can magnetise soft iron placed inside the coil, creating an electromagnet.', '["solenoid", "magnetic field lines", "current-carrying solenoid", "magnetic north pole", "magnetic south pole", "uniform magnetic field", "electromagnet", "soft iron"]'::jsonb, NULL, 18, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', NULL, NULL, '4a8f4feb74100ca134c41a15eae0f71a') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (177, 188, 24, 'question', '1. Consider a circular loop of wire lying in the plane of the table. Let the current pass through the loop clockwise. Apply the right-hand rule to find out the direction of the magnetic field inside and outside the loop.
2. The magnetic field in a given region is uniform. Draw a diagram to represent it.
3. Choose the correct option.
The magnetic field inside a long straight solenoid-carrying current
(a) is zero.
(b) decreases as we move towards its end.
(c) increases as we move towards its end.
(d) is the same at all points.', 'The questions ask to determine the direction of the magnetic field inside and outside a circular current-carrying loop using the right-hand rule, to represent a uniform magnetic field diagrammatically, and to identify the correct description of the magnetic field inside a long straight solenoid carrying current.', '["circular loop", "current direction", "right-hand rule", "magnetic field direction", "uniform magnetic field", "solenoid", "magnetic field variation"]'::jsonb, NULL, 19, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["magnetic field", "right-hand rule", "solenoid magnetic field"]'::jsonb, NULL, '4fc60bfc5cd284346a414ba5648f28f5') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (178, 188, 24, 'concept', 'We have learnt that an electric current flowing through a conductor produces a magnetic field. The field so produced exerts a force on a magnet placed in the vicinity of the conductor. French scientist Andre Marie Ampere (1775–1836) suggested that the magnet must also exert an equal and opposite force on the current-carrying conductor. The force due to a magnetic field acting on a current-carrying conductor can be demonstrated through the following activity.', 'An electric current flowing through a conductor produces a magnetic field that exerts a force on nearby magnets. Andre Marie Ampere proposed that the magnet also exerts an equal and opposite force on the current-carrying conductor. This mutual force interaction can be demonstrated experimentally.', '["electric current", "conductor", "magnetic field", "force", "Andre Marie Ampere", "current-carrying conductor", "magnet"]'::jsonb, NULL, 20, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["magnetic field", "electric current"]'::jsonb, NULL, '6bae03b22d8636bca2662acee2126902') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (179, 190, NULL, 'concept', 'In Activity 12.7, we considered the direction of the current and that of the magnetic field perpendicular to each other and found that the force is perpendicular to both of them. The three directions can be illustrated through a simple rule, called Fleming’s left-hand rule. According to this rule, stretch the thumb, forefinger and middle finger of your left hand such that they are mutually perpendicular (Fig. 12.13). If the first finger points in the direction of magnetic field and the second finger in the direction of current, then the thumb will point in the direction of motion or the force acting on the conductor.

Devices that use current-carrying conductors and magnetic fields include electric motor, electric generator, loudspeakers, microphones and measuring instruments.', 'Fleming’s left-hand rule states that if the forefinger points in the direction of the magnetic field and the second finger points in the direction of current, then the thumb points in the direction of the force or motion on the conductor. This rule helps determine the direction of force on a current-carrying conductor placed in a magnetic field. Various devices such as electric motors, generators, loudspeakers, microphones, and measuring instruments operate based on this principle.', '["Fleming\u2019s left-hand rule", "magnetic field", "current", "force direction", "electric motor", "electric generator", "loudspeakers", "microphones"]'::jsonb, NULL, 21, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["force on current-carrying conductor", "magnetic field"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'db77a61c2ca0b662601d31e984ec705f') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (180, 191, NULL, 'concept', 'An electron enters a magnetic field at right angles to it, as shown in Fig. 12.14. The direction of force acting on the electron will be (a) to the right. (b) to the left. (c) out of the page. (d) into the page.
Solution
Answer is option (d). The direction of force is perpendicular to the direction of magnetic field and current as given by Fleming’s left hand rule. Recall that the direction of current is taken opposite to the direction of motion of electrons. The force is therefore directed into the page.', 'When an electron moves perpendicular to a magnetic field, the force acting on it is perpendicular to both the magnetic field and the electron''s velocity. Using Fleming’s left hand rule, the force direction is determined to be into the page, considering that current direction is opposite to electron motion.', '["electron", "magnetic field", "force direction", "Fleming\u2019s left hand rule", "current direction"]'::jsonb, NULL, 22, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', NULL, NULL, '1149e4686c8f41119da229db8afa5400') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (181, 192, NULL, 'question', '1. Which of the following property of a proton can change while it moves freely in a magnetic field? (There may be more than one correct answer.) (a) mass (b) speed (c) velocity (d) momentum', 'This question asks which properties of a proton can change as it moves freely in a magnetic field, listing mass, speed, velocity, and momentum as options.', '["proton", "magnetic field", "mass", "speed", "velocity", "momentum"]'::jsonb, NULL, 23, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["force on charged particle in magnetic field"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'c6cf51d891d2be303648d38697407eaf') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (182, 193, NULL, 'concept', 'In our homes, we receive supply of electric power through a main supply (also called mains), either supported through overhead electric poles or by underground cables. One of the wires in this supply, usually with red insulation cover, is called live wire (or positive). Another wire, with black insulation, is called neutral wire (or negative). In our country, the potential difference between the two is 220 V.
At the meter-board in the house, these wires pass into an electricity meter through a main fuse. Through the main switch they are connected to the line wires in the house. These wires supply electricity to separate circuits within the house. Often, two separate circuits are used, one of 15 A current rating for appliances with higher power ratings such as geysers, air coolers, etc. The other circuit is of 5 A current rating for bulbs, fans, etc. The earth wire, which has insulation of green colour, is usually connected to a metal plate deep in the earth near the house. This is used as a safety measure, especially for those appliances that have a metallic body, for example, electric press, toaster, table fan, refrigerator, etc. The metallic body is connected to the earth wire, which provides a low-resistance conducting path for the current. Thus, it ensures that any leakage of current to the metallic body of the appliance keeps its potential to that of the earth, and the user may not get a severe electric shock.', 'Domestic electric circuits receive power through mains consisting of live (red insulated) and neutral (black insulated) wires with a potential difference of 220 V. The supply passes through a meter and fuse before distributing to separate circuits rated at 15 A and 5 A for different appliances. The earth wire (green insulated) is connected to a metal plate in the ground to provide safety by preventing electric shocks from leakage currents through metallic appliance bodies.', '["domestic electric circuits", "live wire", "neutral wire", "earth wire", "main fuse", "current rating", "electric shock safety"]'::jsonb, NULL, 24, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', NULL, NULL, '189508232efc7b0f23a78a6360444930') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (183, 194, NULL, 'question', '2. In Activity 12.7, how do we think the displacement of rod AB will be affected if (i) current in rod AB is increased; (ii) a stronger horse-shoe magnet is used; and (iii) length of the rod AB is increased?', 'This question asks how the displacement of a current-carrying rod AB in a magnetic field changes with variations in current magnitude, magnetic field strength, and rod length.', '["current", "magnetic field strength", "rod length", "displacement", "magnetic force"]'::jsonb, NULL, 25, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["force on current-carrying conductor in magnetic field"]'::jsonb, NULL, '3ff1f4b8f7bf2ea364235f051aabb978') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (184, 195, NULL, 'question', '3. A positively-charged particle (alpha-particle) projected towards west is deflected towards north by a magnetic field. The direction of magnetic field is (a) towards south (b) towards east (c) downward (d) upward', 'This question asks to determine the direction of a magnetic field based on the deflection of a positively charged alpha particle moving west and deflected north.', '["alpha particle", "magnetic field direction", "deflection", "charged particle"]'::jsonb, NULL, 26, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["force on charged particle in magnetic field", "right hand rule"]'::jsonb, NULL, '765c555e9ff9cdf28e337e8165b83f8a') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (185, 196, NULL, 'concept', 'An electric current always produces a magnetic field. Even weak ion currents that travel along the nerve cells in our body produce magnetic fields. When we touch something, our nerves carry an electric impulse to the muscles we need to use. This impulse produces a temporary magnetic field. These fields are very weak and are about one-billionth of the earth’s magnetic field. Two main organs in the human body where the magnetic field produced is significant, are the heart and the brain. The magnetic field inside the body forms the basis of obtaining the images of different body parts. This is done using a technique called Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI). Analysis of these images helps in medical diagnosis. Magnetism has, thus, got important uses in medicine.', 'Electric currents in nerve cells produce very weak magnetic fields, approximately one-billionth the strength of Earth''s magnetic field. Significant magnetic fields are produced by the heart and brain, which are utilized in Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) to obtain images of body parts for medical diagnosis. Magnetism has important applications in medicine.', '["electric current", "magnetic field", "nerve cells", "heart", "brain", "Magnetic Resonance Imaging", "MRI", "medical diagnosis"]'::jsonb, NULL, 27, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["magnetic field produced by current"]'::jsonb, NULL, '46b229d33751f742ef0cb3fbfa2b5983') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (186, 197, NULL, 'concept', 'Figure 12.15 gives a schematic diagram of one of the common domestic circuits. In each separate circuit, different appli', 'A schematic diagram illustrates a common domestic electric circuit showing how different appliances are connected in separate circuits within a household.', '["domestic circuit", "schematic diagram", "appliances", "electric wiring"]'::jsonb, NULL, 28, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:27:45.537044+00:00', '["domestic electric circuits"]'::jsonb, NULL, '356cc7ce0465066a7b27dcdb5d10b02d') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (187, 198, NULL, 'concept', 'We have heard the word ‘environment’ often being used on the television, in newspapers and by people around us. Our elders tell us that the ‘environment’ is not what it used to be earlier; others say that we should work in a healthy ‘environment’; and global summits involving the developed and developing countries are regularly held to discuss ‘environmental’ issues. In this chapter, we shall be studying how various components in the environment interact with each other and how we impact the environment.', 'The term ''environment'' is commonly used in media and daily conversations, highlighting its significance. There is a recognition that the environment has changed over time and that maintaining a healthy environment is important. International discussions and summits focus on environmental issues, reflecting global concern. The chapter aims to explore the interactions among environmental components and human impacts on the environment.', '["environment", "environmental issues", "healthy environment", "global summits", "interaction", "impact"]'::jsonb, NULL, 0, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00', NULL, NULL, '4d35050eacbfa19d89fcd7a314f0119e') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (188, 199, NULL, 'concept', 'All organisms such as plants, animals, microorganisms and human beings as well as the physical surroundings interact with each other and maintain a balance in nature. All the interacting organisms in an area together with the non-living constituents of the environment form an ecosystem. Thus, an ecosystem consists of biotic components comprising living organisms and abiotic components comprising physical factors like temperature, rainfall, wind, soil and minerals. For example, if you visit a garden you will find different plants, such as grasses, trees; flower bearing plants like rose, jasmine, sunflower; and animals like frogs, insects and birds. All these living organisms interact with each other and their growth, reproduction and other activities are affected by the abiotic components of ecosystem. So a garden is an ecosystem. Other types of ecosystems are forests, ponds and lakes. These are natural ecosystems while gardens and crop-fields are human-made (artificial) ecosystems.', 'An ecosystem is formed by the interaction of living organisms (biotic components) and non-living physical factors (abiotic components) in an area. Examples include gardens, forests, ponds, and lakes. Natural ecosystems occur naturally, while human-made ecosystems include gardens and crop-fields.', '["ecosystem", "biotic components", "abiotic components", "natural ecosystems", "human-made ecosystems", "plants", "animals"]'::jsonb, NULL, 1, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00', NULL, NULL, '7eff63f6a5f5ba3f0d25b0bb28c16f1c') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (189, 201, NULL, 'concept', 'We have seen in earlier classes that organisms can be grouped as producers, consumers and decomposers according to the manner in which they obtain their sustenance from the environment. Let us recall what we have learnt through the self-sustaining ecosystem created by us above. Which organisms can make organic compounds like sugar and starch from inorganic substances using the radiant energy of the Sun in the presence of chlorophyll? All green plants and certain bacteria which can produce food by photosynthesis come under this category and are called the producers. Organisms depend on the producers either directly or indirectly for their sustenance. These organisms which consume the food produced, either directly from producers or indirectly by feeding on other consumers are the consumers. Consumers can be classed variously as herbivores, carnivores, omnivores and parasites. Can you give examples for each of these categories of consumers? Imagine the situation where you do not clean the aquarium and some fish and plants have died. Have you ever thought what happens when an organism dies? The microorganisms, comprising bacteria and fungi, break down the dead remains and waste products of organisms. These microorganisms are the decomposers as they break down the complex organic substances into simple inorganic substances that go into the soil and are used up once more by the plants. What will happen to the garbage, and dead animals and plants in their absence? Will the natural replenishment of the soil take place, even if decomposers are not there?', 'Organisms in an ecosystem are classified as producers, consumers, and decomposers based on how they obtain sustenance. Producers, such as green plants and some bacteria, make organic compounds by photosynthesis. Consumers feed on producers or other consumers and include herbivores, carnivores, omnivores, and parasites. Decomposers like bacteria and fungi break down dead organisms and waste into simple inorganic substances that replenish the soil.', '["producers", "consumers", "decomposers", "photosynthesis", "herbivores", "carnivores", "omnivores", "parasites"]'::jsonb, NULL, 3, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00', '["Components and Types of Ecosystems", "Design and Maintenance of a Self-Sustaining Aquarium Ecosystem"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'e6aeaea227fb0c23c04ae8cfafd7884c') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (190, 203, NULL, 'concept', 'In Activity 13.4 we have formed a series of organisms feeding on one another. This series or organisms taking part at various biotic levels form a food chain (Fig. 13.1). Each step or level of the food chain forms a trophic level. The autotrophs or the producers are at the first trophic level. They fix up the solar energy and make it available for heterotrophs or the consumers. The herbivores or the primary consumers come at the second, small carnivores or the secondary consumers at the third and larger carnivores or the tertiary consumers form the fourth trophic level (Fig. 13.2). We know that the food we eat acts as a fuel to provide us energy to do work. Thus the interactions among various components of the environment involves flow of energy from one component of the system to another. As we have studied, the autotrophs capture the energy present in sunlight and convert it into chemical energy. This energy supports all the activities of the living world. From autotrophs, the energy goes to the heterotrophs and decomposers. However, as we saw in the previous Chapter on ‘Sources of Energy’, when one form of energy is changed to another, some energy is lost to the environment in forms which cannot be used again. The flow of energy between various components of the environment has been extensively studied and it has been found that – The green plants in a terrestrial ecosystem capture about 1% of the energy of sunlight that falls on their leaves and convert it into food energy. When green plants are eaten by primary consumers, a great deal of energy is lost as heat to the environment, some amount goes into digestion and in doing work and the rest goes towards growth and reproduction. An average of 10% of the food eaten is turned into its own body and made available for the next level of consumers. Therefore, 10% can be taken as the average value for the amount of organic matter that is present at each step and reaches the next level of consumers. Since so little energy is available for the next level of consumers, food chains generally consist of only three or four steps. The loss of energy at each step is so great that very little usable energy remains after four trophic levels. There are generally a greater number of individuals at the lower trophic levels of an ecosystem, the greatest number is of the producers. The length and complexity of food chains vary greatly. Each organism is generally eaten by two or more other kinds of organisms which in turn are eaten by several other organisms. So instead of a straight line food chain, the relationship can be shown as a series of branching lines called a food web (Fig. 13.3).', 'A food chain is a series of organisms feeding on one another, with each step called a trophic level. Autotrophs or producers occupy the first trophic level by converting solar energy into chemical energy. Energy flows unidirectionally from producers to various levels of consumers, with significant energy loss at each step, averaging about 10% transfer efficiency. Due to energy loss, food chains usually have only three or four trophic levels. Food webs represent the complex feeding relationships among organisms, showing multiple feeding connections rather than a simple linear chain.', '["food chain", "trophic level", "autotroph", "heterotroph", "energy flow", "producers", "consumers", "food web"]'::jsonb, NULL, 5, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00', NULL, NULL, '1f880ac5e4e890ed1aa321a7d8c2f90e') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (191, 204, NULL, 'concept', 'From the energy flow diagram (Fig. 13.4), two things become clear. Firstly, the flow of energy is unidirectional. The energy that is captured by the autotrophs does not revert back to the solar input and the energy which passes to the herbivores does not come back to autotrophs. As it moves progressively through the various trophic levels it is no longer available to the previous level. Secondly, the energy available at each trophic level gets diminished progressively due to loss of energy at each level. Another interesting aspect of food chain is h', 'Energy flow in ecosystems is unidirectional, moving from autotrophs to herbivores and onward without returning to previous levels or the solar source. At each trophic level, energy availability decreases progressively due to losses, primarily as heat, making energy transfer inefficient. This limits the number of trophic levels in a food chain.', '["energy flow", "unidirectional", "autotrophs", "herbivores", "trophic levels", "energy loss"]'::jsonb, NULL, 6, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00', '["Structure and Energy Flow in Food Chains and Trophic Levels"]'::jsonb, NULL, '1b933982e2509ca40312596ca0924191') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (192, 205, NULL, 'concept', 'ENVIRONMENT?
We are an integral part of the environment. Changes in the environment affect us and our activities change the environment around us. We have already seen in Class IX how our activities pollute the environment. In this chapter, we shall be looking at two of the environmental problems in detail, that is, depletion of the ozone layer and waste disposal.', 'Humans are an integral part of the environment, and there is a reciprocal relationship where environmental changes affect human activities and vice versa. Previous studies have shown how human activities cause pollution. This chapter focuses on two specific environmental problems: depletion of the ozone layer and waste disposal.', '["environment", "pollution", "ozone layer depletion", "waste disposal", "human activities"]'::jsonb, NULL, 7, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00', NULL, NULL, '3f426961c7097f93ebd88d6472bc521c') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (193, 205, 25, 'concept', 'Ozone (O3) is a molecule formed by three atoms of oxygen. While O2, which we normally refer to as oxygen, is essential for all aerobic forms of life, ozone is a deadly poison. However, at the higher levels of the atmosphere, ozone performs an essential function. It shields the surface of the earth from ultraviolet (UV) radiation from the Sun. This radiation is highly damaging to organisms; for example, it is known to cause skin cancer in human beings. Ozone at the higher levels of the atmosphere is a product of UV radiation acting on oxygen (O2) molecules. The higher energy UV radiations split apart some molecular oxygen (O2) into free oxygen (O) atoms. These atoms then combine with the molecular oxygen to form ozone as shown—
O + O2 UV →  O3', 'Ozone is a triatomic oxygen molecule that is toxic at ground level but essential in the upper atmosphere where it protects life by absorbing harmful ultraviolet radiation from the Sun. Ultraviolet rays split oxygen molecules into atoms, which then combine with O2 to form ozone. This ozone layer prevents UV radiation from causing damage such as skin cancer.', '["Ozone", "O3", "Oxygen", "UV radiation", "Atmosphere", "Skin cancer", "Oxygen atoms"]'::jsonb, NULL, 8, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00', NULL, NULL, '0be88e4acf52a56ce423e3319bf14b80') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (194, 205, 25, 'concept', 'The amount of ozone in the atmosphere began to drop sharply in the 1980s. This decrease has been linked to synthetic chemicals like chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) which are used as refrigerants and in fire extinguishers. In 1987, the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) succeeded in forging an agreement to freeze CFC production at 1986 levels. It is now mandatory for all the manufacturing companies to make CFC-free refrigerators throughout the world.', 'Ozone levels in the atmosphere declined sharply starting in the 1980s due to the release of chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), chemicals used in refrigeration and fire extinguishers. In response, the UNEP established an international agreement in 1987 to freeze CFC production at 1986 levels, leading to mandatory production of CFC-free refrigerators worldwide.', '["Ozone depletion", "Chlorofluorocarbons", "CFCs", "Refrigerants", "UNEP", "1987 agreement", "CFC-free refrigerators"]'::jsonb, NULL, 9, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00', '["Role and Formation of Ozone in the Atmosphere"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'e13c06f096e0e597b5cafad1e50f2579') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (195, 205, 26, 'concept', 'In our daily activities, we generate a lot of material that are thrown away. What are some of these waste materials? What happens after we throw them away? Let us perform an activity to find answers to these questions.

We have seen in the chapter on ‘Life Processes’ that the food we eat is digested by various enzymes in our body. Have you ever wondered why the same enzyme does not break down everything we eat? Enzymes are specific in their action; specific enzymes are needed for the break-down of a particular substance. That is why we will not get any energy if we try to eat coal! Because of this, many human-made materials like plastics will not be broken down by the action of bacteria or other saprophytes. These materials will be acted upon by physical processes like heat and pressure, but under the ambient conditions found in our environment, these persist for a long time.

Substances that are broken down by biological processes are said to be biodegradable. How many of the substances you buried were biodegradable? Substances that are not broken down in this manner are said to be non-biodegradable. These substances may be inert and simply persist in the environment for a long time or may harm the various members of the eco-system.', 'Waste materials generated in daily life can be biodegradable or non-biodegradable. Enzymes in living organisms break down specific substances, which is why some materials like plastics do not decompose biologically. Biodegradable substances are broken down by biological processes, while non-biodegradable substances persist in the environment and may cause harm.', '["waste materials", "enzymes", "biodegradable", "non-biodegradable", "plastics", "biological processes", "eco-system"]'::jsonb, NULL, 13, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00', '["enzyme specificity", "biological digestion"]'::jsonb, NULL, '4adc274dda730527bbfa81f49aa988cc') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (196, 206, NULL, 'question', 'Use the library or internet to find out more about biodegradable and non-biodegradable substances. How long are various non-biodegradable substances expected to last in our environment? These days, new types of plastics which are said to be biodegradable are available. Find out more about such materials and whether they do or do not harm the environment.
1. Why are some substances biodegradable and some non-biodegradable?
2. Give any two ways in which biodegradable substances would affect the environment.
3. Give any two ways in which non-biodegradable substances would affect the environment.', 'Biodegradable substances can be broken down by natural processes, while non-biodegradable substances persist in the environment for long periods. New biodegradable plastics are being developed, but their environmental impact varies. Understanding the differences helps assess how these substances affect ecosystems.', '["biodegradable substances", "non-biodegradable substances", "plastics", "environmental impact", "decomposition"]'::jsonb, NULL, 14, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00', NULL, NULL, '6e2ee43833f3a223d4d474547be956cb') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (197, 209, NULL, 'concept', 'Disposable cups in trains
If you ask your parents, they will probably remember a time when tea in trains was served in plastic glasses which had to be returned to the vendor. The introduction of disposable cups was hailed as a step forward for reasons of hygiene. No one at that time perhaps thought about the impact caused by the disposal of millions of these cups on a daily basis. Some time back, kulhads, that is, disposable cups made of clay, were suggested as an alternative. But a little thought showed that making these kulhads on a large scale would result in the loss of the fertile top-soil. Now disposable paper-cups are being used. What do you think are the advantages of disposable paper-cups over disposable plastic cups? Think it over.', 'Disposable cups were introduced for hygiene but caused environmental problems due to disposal. Clay cups (kulhads) were proposed but large-scale use would deplete fertile topsoil. Disposable paper cups are now used as an alternative with potentially fewer environmental drawbacks.', '["disposable cups", "plastic cups", "clay kulhads", "paper cups", "topsoil loss", "environmental impact"]'::jsonb, NULL, 17, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00', NULL, NULL, 'ff138bb39503bfb51753437bfb3800e9') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (198, 210, NULL, 'question', 'Search the internet or library to find out what hazardous materials have to be dealt with while disposing of electronic items. How would these materials affect the environment? Find out how plastics are recycled. Does the recycling process have any impact on the environment?
1. What is ozone and how does it affect any ecosystem?
2. How can you help in reducing the problem of waste disposal? Give any two methods.', 'Electronic waste contains hazardous materials that can harm the environment if not properly managed. Plastic recycling processes may also have environmental impacts. Ozone plays a role in ecosystems, and waste reduction methods can help mitigate disposal problems.', '["electronic waste", "hazardous materials", "plastic recycling", "environmental impact", "ozone", "waste reduction"]'::jsonb, NULL, 18, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00', NULL, NULL, '69be00694c6e328f4d8d705b0ae9f8b9') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_blocks ("id", "topic_id", "subtopic_id", "block_type", "raw_text", "enriched_summary", "enriched_keywords", "page_num", "block_index", "prompt_version", "created_at", "enriched_prerequisites", "ocr_confidence", "content_hash") VALUES (199, 211, NULL, 'concept', 'The various components of an ecosystem are interdependent. The producers make the energy from sunlight available to the rest of the ecosystem. There is a loss of energy as we go from one trophic level to the next, this limits the number of', 'Ecosystem components depend on each other, with producers converting sunlight into energy for other organisms. Energy decreases at each trophic level, limiting the number of levels in an ecosystem.', '["ecosystem", "interdependence", "producers", "sunlight energy", "trophic levels", "energy loss"]'::jsonb, NULL, 19, 'json_to_db_v1', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00', NULL, NULL, '984e7e17e410bf6bad53ff9bd16bc26b') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

