The current through the heater becomes 8 A.', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO content_raw_archive ("id", "block_id", "raw_text", "repaired_text", "created_at") VALUES (142, 142, 'Resistance of a metal wire of length 1 m is 26 Ω at 20°C. If the
diameter of the wire is 0.3 mm, what will be the resistivity of the
metal at that temperature? Using Table 11.2, predict the material
of the wire.
We are given the resistance R  of the wire = 26 Ω, the diameter

d = 0.3 mm = 3 ×
 10-4 m, and the length l  of the wire = 1 m.

Therefore, from Eq. (11.10), the resistivity of the given metallic wire is

ρ=  (RA/l)  =  (R πd2/4l)

Substitution of values in this gives

ρ=  1.84 × 10–6 Ω m

The resistivity of the metal at 20°C is 1.84 × 10–6 Ω m. From
Table 11.2, we see that this is the resistivity of manganese.', 'Resistance of a metal wire of length 1 m is 26 Ω at 20°C. If the diameter of the wire is 0.3 mm, what will be the resistivity of the metal at that temperature? Using Table 11.2, predict the material of the wire.
