/square6There is a loss of energy as we go from one trophic level to the next, this limits the
number of trophic levels in a food-chain.
/square6Human activities have an impact on the environment.
/square6The use of chemicals like CFCs has endanger ed the ozone layer . Since the ozone
layer protects against the ultraviolet radiation from the Sun, this could damage
the environment.

/square6The waste we generate may be biodegradable or non-biodegradable.

/square6The disposal of the waste we generate is causing serious environmental problems.
EXERCISES
1.Which of the following groups contain only biodegradable items?
(a) Grass, flowers and leather
(b) Grass, wood and plastic
(c)Fruit-peels, cake and lime-juice
(d) Cake, wood and grass
2.Which of the following constitute a food-chain?
(a) Grass, wheat and mango
(b) Grass, goat and human

Our Environment 217(c)Goat, cow and elephant
(d) Grass, fish and goat
3.Which of the following are environment-friendly practices?
(a) Carrying cloth-bags to put purchases in while shopping
(b) Switching off unnecessary lights and fans
(c) Walking to school instead of getting your mother to dr op you on her scooter
(d) All of the above
4.What will happen if we kill all the organisms in one trophic level?
5.Will the impact of removing all the organisms in a trophic level be different for
different trophic levels? Can the organisms of any trophic level be removed without
causing any damage to the ecosystem?
6.What is biological magnification? Will the levels of this magnification be different at
different levels of the ecosystem?
7.What are the problems caused by the non-biodegradable wastes that we generate?
8.If all the waste we generate is biodegradable, will this have no impact on the
environment?
9.Why is damage to the ozone layer a cause for concern? What steps are being taken
to limit this damage?', 'Disposable cups in trains
If you ask your parents, they will probably remember a time when tea in trains was served in plastic glasses which had to be returned to the vendor. The introduction of disposable cups was hailed as a step forward for reasons of hygiene. No one at that time perhaps thought about the impact caused by the disposal of millions of these cups on a daily basis. Some time back, kulhads, that is, disposable cups made of clay, were suggested as an alternative. But a little thought showed that making these kulhads on a large scale would result in the loss of the fertile top-soil. Now disposable paper-cups are being used. What do you think are the advantages of disposable paper-cups over disposable plastic cups? Think it over.', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (1, 1, 'Activity 1.1', 'CAUTION: This Activity needs the teacher’s assistance. It would be better if students wear suitable eyeglasses.
- Clean a magnesium ribbon about 3-4 cm long by rubbing it with sandpaper.
- Hold it with a pair of tongs.
- Burn it using a spirit lamp or burner and collect the ash so formed in a watch-glass as shown in Fig. 1.1. Burn the magnesium ribbon keeping it away as far as possible from your eyes.
- What do you observe?

Figure 1.1
Burning of a magnesium ribbon in air and collection of magnesium oxide in a watch-glass', 'Burning a magnesium ribbon in air produces magnesium oxide, demonstrating a chemical reaction that changes the substance''s nature and produces a new compound.', '["magnesium ribbon", "burning", "magnesium oxide", "chemical reaction", "watch-glass", "tongs", "spirit lamp"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:18:52.433290+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (2, 1, 'Activity 1.2', 'Figure 1.2
Formation of hydrogen gas by the action of dilute sulphuric acid on zinc
- Take a few zinc granules in a conical flask or a test tube.
- Add dilute hydrochloric acid or sulphuric acid to this (Fig. 1.2).
CAUTION: Handle the acid with care.
- Do you observe anything happening around the zinc granules?
- Touch the conical flask or test tube. Is there any change in its temperature?', 'When zinc granules react with dilute sulphuric or hydrochloric acid, hydrogen gas is formed and temperature changes occur, indicating a chemical reaction.', '["zinc granules", "dilute sulphuric acid", "hydrogen gas", "chemical reaction", "temperature change", "acid"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:18:52.433290+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (3, 4, 'Activity 1.3', '- Take lead nitrate solution in a test tube.
- Add potassium iodide solution to this.
- What do you observe?', 'Mixing lead nitrate solution with potassium iodide solution results in an observable change, indicating a chemical reaction.', '["lead nitrate", "potassium iodide", "test tube", "chemical reaction"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:18:52.433290+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (4, 6, 'Formation of Slaked Lime by the Reaction of Calcium Oxide with Water', 'Take a small amount of calcium oxide or quick lime in a beaker. Slowly add water to this. Touch the beaker as shown in Fig. 1.3. Do you feel any change in temperature?

Calcium oxide reacts vigorously with water to produce slaked lime (calcium hydroxide) releasing a large amount of heat.

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(aq) + Heat (1.13)
(Quick lime) (Slaked lime)

In this reaction, calcium oxide and water combine to form a single product, calcium hydroxide. Such a reaction in which a single product is formed from two or more reactants is known as a combination reaction.', 'Calcium oxide reacts with water to form calcium hydroxide, releasing heat. This is an example of a combination reaction where two reactants form a single product.', '["calcium oxide", "quick lime", "water", "slaked lime", "calcium hydroxide", "combination reaction", "heat"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:18:52.433290+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (5, 11, 'Activity 1.5', 'Take about 2 g lead nitrate powder in a boiling tube. Hold the boiling tube with a pair of tongs and heat it over a flame, as shown in Fig. 1.5. What do you observe? Note down the change, if any. You will observe the emission of brown fumes. These fumes are of nitrogen dioxide (NO2). The reaction that takes place is –', 'Heating lead nitrate powder causes the emission of brown nitrogen dioxide fumes. This indicates a chemical change occurring during the heating process.', '["lead nitrate", "boiling tube", "heat", "brown fumes", "nitrogen dioxide", "NO2"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:18:52.433290+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (6, 11, 'Activity 1.6', 'Take about 2 g ferrous sulphate crystals in a dry boiling tube. Note the colour of the ferrous sulphate crystals. Heat the boiling tube over the flame of a burner or spirit lamp as shown in Fig. 1.4. Observe the colour of the crystals after heating. Have you noticed that the green colour of the ferrous sulphate crystals has changed? You can also smell the characteristic odour of burning sulphur.

2FeSO4(s) Heat →   Fe2O3(s)  +  SO2(g)  + SO3(g) (1.19)
(Ferrous sulphate)   (Ferric oxide)

In this reaction you can observe that a single reactant breaks down to give simpler products. This is a decomposition reaction. Ferrous sulphate crystals (FeSO4.7H2O) lose water when heated and the colour of the crystals changes. It then decomposes to ferric oxide (Fe2O3), sulphur dioxide (SO2) and sulphur trioxide (SO3). Ferric oxide is a solid, while SO2 and SO3 are gases.', 'Heating ferrous sulphate crystals causes them to lose water and decompose into ferric oxide, sulphur dioxide, and sulphur trioxide. This is an example of a decomposition reaction where a single reactant breaks down into simpler products.', '["ferrous sulphate", "decomposition", "ferric oxide", "sulphur dioxide", "sulphur trioxide", "heat", "crystals"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:18:52.433290+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (7, 14, 'Decomposition of Silver Chloride by Sunlight', 'Take about 2 g silver chloride in a china dish. What is its colour? Place this china dish in sunlight for some time (Fig. 1.7). Observe the colour of the silver chloride after some time. You will see that white silver chloride turns grey in sunlight. This is due to the decomposition of silver chloride into silver and chlorine by light.

2AgCl(s) Sunlight → 2Ag(s) + Cl2(g) (1.22)', 'Silver chloride is white in colour and turns grey when exposed to sunlight due to its decomposition into silver metal and chlorine gas. The reaction is represented by the equation 2AgCl(s) Sunlight → 2Ag(s) + Cl2(g).', '["silver chloride", "decomposition", "sunlight", "silver", "chlorine", "chemical reaction"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:18:52.433290+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (8, 14, 'Electrolysis of Water', 'Take a plastic mug. Drill two holes at its base and fit rubber stoppers in these holes. Insert carbon electrodes in these rubber stoppers as shown in Fig. 1.6. Connect these electrodes to a 6 volt battery. Fill the mug with water such that the electrodes are immersed. Add a few drops of dilute sulphuric acid to the water. Take two test tubes filled with water and invert them over the two carbon electrodes. Switch on the current and leave the apparatus undisturbed for some time. You will observe the formation of bubbles at both the electrodes. These bubbles displace water in the test tubes. Is the volume of the gas collected the same in both the test tubes? Once the test tubes are filled with the respective gases, remove them carefully. Test these gases one by one by bringing a burning candle close to the mouth of the test tubes.

CAUTION: This step must be performed carefully by the teacher.

What happens in each case? Which gas is present in each test tube?', 'Electrolysis of water using carbon electrodes and dilute sulphuric acid produces gases at the electrodes that displace water in inverted test tubes. The gases collected differ in volume and can be tested by bringing a burning candle near them to identify their nature.', '["electrolysis", "water", "carbon electrodes", "dilute sulphuric acid", "gas collection", "test tubes"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:18:52.433290+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (9, 19, 'Activity: Reaction of Iron Nails with Copper Sulphate Solution', 'Take three iron nails and clean them by rubbing with sand paper. Take two test tubes marked as (A) and (B). In each test tube, take about 10 mL copper sulphate solution. Tie two iron nails with a thread and immerse them carefully in the copper sulphate solution in test tube B for about 20 minutes [Fig. 1.8 (a)]. Keep one iron nail aside for comparison. After 20 minutes, take out the iron nails from the copper sulphate solution. Compare the intensity of the blue colour of copper sulphate solutions in test tubes (A) and (B) [Fig. 1.8 (b)]. Also, compare the colour of the iron nails dipped in the copper sulphate solution with the one kept aside [Fig. 1.8 (b)].', 'Iron nails immersed in copper sulphate solution cause the blue colour of the solution to fade and the nails to become brownish. This demonstrates a chemical change involving displacement of copper by iron.', '["iron nails", "copper sulphate solution", "displacement reaction", "blue colour", "chemical change"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:18:52.433290+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (10, 19, 'Activity: Reaction of Barium Hydroxide and Ammonium Chloride', 'Take about 2 g barium hydroxide in a test tube. Add 1 g of ammonium chloride and mix with the help of a glass rod. Touch the bottom of the test tube with your palm. What do you feel? Is this an exothermic or endothermic reaction?', 'Mixing barium hydroxide and ammonium chloride produces a reaction that absorbs heat, making the test tube feel cold, indicating an endothermic reaction.', '["barium hydroxide", "ammonium chloride", "endothermic reaction", "heat absorption"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:18:52.433290+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (11, 23, 'Formation of Barium Sulphate and Sodium Chloride', 'Take about 3 mL of sodium sulphate solution in a test tube. In another test tube, take about 3 mL of barium chloride solution. Mix the two solutions (Fig. 1.9). What do you observe? You will observe that a white substance, which is insoluble in water, is formed. This insoluble substance formed is known as a precipitate. Any reaction that produces a precipitate can be called a precipitation reaction.

Na2SO4(aq) + BaCl2(aq) → BaSO4(s) + 2NaCl(aq) (1.27)', 'Mixing sodium sulphate and barium chloride solutions produces a white precipitate of barium sulphate, which is insoluble in water. This type of reaction is called a precipitation reaction.', '["sodium sulphate", "barium chloride", "precipitate", "precipitation reaction", "barium sulphate", "sodium chloride"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:18:52.433290+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (12, 26, 'Recall Activity 1.2', 'Recall Activity 1.2, where you have mixed the solutions of lead(II) nitrate and potassium iodide.
(i) What was the colour of the precipitate formed? Can you name the compound precipitated?
(ii) Write the balanced chemical equation for this reaction.
(iii) Is this also a double displacement reaction?
What causes this? The white precipitate of BaSO4 is formed by the reaction of SO4 2– and Ba2+. The other product formed is sodium chloride which remains in the solution. Such reactions in which there is an exchange of ions between the reactants are called double displacement reactions.', 'Mixing lead(II) nitrate and potassium iodide solutions forms a precipitate whose color and compound can be identified. Double displacement reactions involve the exchange of ions between reactants, as exemplified by the formation of white BaSO4 precipitate from SO4 2– and Ba2+ ions.', '["lead(II) nitrate", "potassium iodide", "precipitate", "double displacement reaction", "BaSO4", "ions"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:18:52.433290+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (13, 28, 'Recall Activity 1.1', 'Recall Activity 1.1, where a magnesium ribbon burns with a dazzling flame in air (oxygen) and changes into a white substance, magnesium oxide. Is magnesium being oxidised or reduced in this reaction?', 'Magnesium burns in oxygen to form magnesium oxide, raising the question of whether magnesium is oxidised or reduced in this reaction.', '["magnesium", "burns", "oxygen", "magnesium oxide", "oxidised", "reduced"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:18:52.433290+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (14, 32, 'Use of Olfactory and Colour Indicators to Identify Acids and Bases', 'These indicators tell us whether a substance is acidic or basic by change in colour. There are some substances whose odour changes in acidic or basic media. These are called olfactory indicators. Let us try out some of these indicators.

You have been provided with three test tubes. One of them contains distilled water and the other two contain an acidic solution and a basic solution, respectively. If you are given only red litmus paper, how will you identify the contents of each test tube?

Collect the following solutions from the science laboratory – hydrochloric acid (HCl), sulphuric acid (H2SO4), nitric acid (HNO3), acetic acid (CH3COOH), sodium hydroxide (NaOH), calcium hydroxide [Ca(OH)2], potassium hydroxide (KOH), magnesium hydroxide [Mg(OH)2], and ammonium hydroxide (NH4OH).

Put a drop of each of the above solutions on a watch-glass one by one and test with a drop of the indicators shown in Table 2.1.

What change in colour did you observe with red litmus, blue litmus, phenolphthalein and methyl orange solutions for each of the solutions taken?

Tabulate your observations in Table 2.1.', 'Olfactory indicators change odour in acidic or basic media, while colour indicators change colour to indicate acidity or basicity. An activity is described where three test tubes containing distilled water, an acid, and a base are to be identified using only red litmus paper. Various acidic and basic solutions are collected and tested with indicators such as red litmus, blue litmus, phenolphthalein, and methyl orange. Observations of colour changes are to be recorded in a table.', '["olfactory indicators", "acidic solution", "basic solution", "red litmus paper", "blue litmus paper", "phenolphthalein", "methyl orange", "colour change"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (15, 34, 'Using Onion and Cloth Strips as Olfactory Indicators for Acids and Bases', 'Take some finely chopped onions in a plastic bag along with some strips of clean cloth. Tie up the bag tightly and leave overnight in the fridge. The cloth strips can now be used to test for acids and bases. Take two of these cloth strips and check their odour. Keep them on a clean surface and put a few drops of dilute HCl solution on one strip and a few drops of dilute NaOH solution on the other. Which of these – vanilla, onion and clove, can be used as olfactory indicators on the basis of your observations? Let us do some more activities to understand the chemical properties of acids and bases.', 'Finely chopped onions are stored with cloth strips in a sealed bag overnight in the fridge to prepare olfactory indicators. These cloth strips are then tested by applying dilute hydrochloric acid (HCl) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH) solutions to observe changes in odour. The activity compares the effectiveness of vanilla, onion, and clove as olfactory indicators for acids and bases.', '["onion", "cloth strips", "olfactory indicators", "dilute HCl", "dilute NaOH", "vanilla", "clove", "acid-base testing"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (16, 34, 'Effect of Acids and Bases on the Odour of Vanilla Essence and Clove Oil', 'Note your observations.
Now take some dilute vanilla essence and clove oil and check their odour.
Take some dilute HCl solution in one test tube and dilute NaOH solution in another. Add a few drops of dilute vanilla essence to both test tubes and shake well. Check the odour once again and record changes in odour, if any.
Similarly, test the change in the odour of clove oil with dilute HCl and dilute NaOH solutions and record your observations.', 'This activity involves observing the odour of dilute vanilla essence and clove oil before and after adding dilute hydrochloric acid (HCl) and dilute sodium hydroxide (NaOH) solutions. The changes in odour upon mixing with acidic and basic solutions are recorded to study the effect of acids and bases on the odour of these substances.', '["vanilla essence", "clove oil", "dilute HCl", "dilute NaOH", "odour", "acid-base reaction"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (17, 34, 'Investigation of Hydrogen Gas Evolution from Reaction of Metals with Acids', 'CAUTION: This activity needs the teacher’s assistance.
Set the apparatus as shown in Fig. 2.1.
Take about 5 mL of dilute sulphuric acid in a test tube and add a few pieces of zinc granules to it.
What do you observe on the surface of zinc granules?
Pass the gas being evolved through the soap solution.
Why are bubbles formed in the soap solution?
Take a burning candle near a gas filled bubble.
What do you observe?
Repeat this Activity with some more acids like HCl, HNO3 and CH3COOH.
Are the observations in all the cases the same or different?

Figure 2.1 Reaction of zinc granules with dilute sulphuric acid and testing hydrogen gas by burning

Note that the metal in the above reactions displaces hydrogen atoms from the acids as hydrogen gas and forms a compound called a salt. Thus, the reaction of a metal with an acid can be summarised as –
Acid + Metal 12 Salt + Hydrogen gas

Can you now write the equations for the reactions you have observed?', 'When zinc granules react with dilute sulphuric acid, hydrogen gas is evolved, which can be tested by passing it through soap solution to form bubbles and by burning the gas near a candle. Similar reactions occur with other acids such as HCl, HNO3, and CH3COOH. In these reactions, the metal displaces hydrogen from the acid, producing hydrogen gas and a salt. The general reaction is Acid + Metal → Salt + Hydrogen gas.', '["zinc granules", "dilute sulphuric acid", "hydrogen gas", "soap solution", "acid-metal reaction", "salt formation", "HCl", "HNO3", "CH3COOH"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (18, 38, 'Preparation of Sodium Carbonate Solution in Test Tubes', 'Take two test tubes, label them as A and B. Take about 0.5 g of sodium carbonate (Na2CO3) in test tube A and about', 'Two test tubes labeled A and B are prepared. Approximately 0.5 g of sodium carbonate (Na2CO3) is placed in test tube A for further experimentation.', '["test tubes", "sodium carbonate", "Na2CO3", "measurement", "labeling"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (19, 38, 'Observation of Carbon Dioxide Gas Passing Through Lime Water', '(NaHCO3) in test tube B.
5Add about 2 mL of dilute HCl to both the test tubes.
5What do you observe?
5Pass the gas produced in each case through lime water (calcium hydroxide solution) as shown in Fig. 2.2 and record your observations.

Passing carbon dioxide gas through calcium hydroxide solution', 'When dilute hydrochloric acid is added to sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO3) and another test tube, carbon dioxide gas is produced. This gas is passed through lime water (calcium hydroxide solution), and observations are recorded to study the reaction between carbon dioxide and lime water.', '["NaHCO3", "dilute HCl", "carbon dioxide", "lime water", "calcium hydroxide solution"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (20, 38, 'Effect of Acid and Base on Phenolphthalein Indicator', '5Take about 2 mL of dilute NaOH solution in a test tube and add two drops of phenolphthalein solution.
5What is the colour of the solution?
5Add dilute HCl solution to the above solution drop by drop.
5Is there any colour change for the reaction mixture?
5Why did the colour of phenolphthalein change after the addition of an acid?
5Now add a few drops of NaOH to the above mixture.
5Does the pink colour of phenolphthalein reappear?
5Why do you think this has happened?', 'Dilute sodium hydroxide solution turns phenolphthalein pink, indicating a basic medium. Adding dilute hydrochloric acid dropwise causes the pink colour to disappear due to neutralisation. Adding sodium hydroxide again restores the pink colour, demonstrating the reversible colour change of phenolphthalein with pH changes.', '["NaOH", "phenolphthalein", "dilute HCl", "colour change", "acid-base reaction", "neutralisation"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (21, 42, 'Reaction of Copper Oxide with Dilute Hydrochloric Acid', 'Take a small amount of copper oxide in a beaker and add dilute hydrochloric acid slowly while stirring. Note the colour of the solution. What has happened to the copper oxide? You will notice that the colour of the solution becomes blue-green and the copper oxide dissolves. The blue-green colour of the solution is due to the formation of copper(II) chloride in the reaction.', 'When copper oxide is added to dilute hydrochloric acid, it dissolves and the solution turns blue-green. This colour change occurs because copper(II) chloride is formed as a product of the reaction between copper oxide and the acid.', '["copper oxide", "dilute hydrochloric acid", "blue-green colour", "copper(II) chloride", "reaction"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (22, 45, 'Investigation of Acidity in Hydrogen-Containing Compounds', 'Hydrogen seems to be common to all acids. Let us perform an Activity to investigate whether all compounds containing hydrogen are acidic.', 'Hydrogen is a common element in all acids. This activity aims to investigate whether the presence of hydrogen in a compound necessarily makes it acidic.', '["hydrogen", "acids", "compounds", "acidic"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (29, 67, 'Investigation of Solubility and pH Characteristics of Various Salts', 'Collect the following salt samples – sodium chloride, potassium nitrate, aluminium chloride, zinc sulphate, copper sulphate, sodium acetate, sodium carbonate and sodium hydrogencarbonate (some other salts available can also be taken).
Check their solubility in water (use distilled water only).
Check the action of these solutions on litmus and find the pH using a pH paper.
Which of the salts are acidic, basic or neutral?
Identify the acid or base used to form the salt.
Report your observations in Table 2.4.
Salts of a strong acid and a strong base are neutral with pH value of 7. On the other hand, salts of a strong acid and weak base are acidic with pH value less than 7 and those of a strong base and weak acid are basic in nature, with pH value more than 7.', 'This activity involves collecting various salt samples and testing their solubility in distilled water. The solutions are then tested with litmus paper and pH paper to determine whether they are acidic, basic, or neutral. The activity also requires identifying the acid or base from which each salt is formed. It is noted that salts formed from a strong acid and strong base are neutral (pH 7), those from a strong acid and weak base are acidic (pH less than 7), and those from a strong base and weak acid are basic (pH greater than 7).', '["salt samples", "solubility", "litmus test", "pH paper", "acidic salts", "basic salts", "neutral salts", "strong acid", "weak base", "strong base", "weak acid"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (23, 45, 'Electrical Conductivity of Acidic and Non-Acidic Solutions', 'Take solutions of glucose, alcohol, hydrochloric acid, sulphuric acid, etc. Fix two nails on a cork, and place the cork in a 100 mL beaker. Connect the nails to the two terminals of a 6 volt battery through a bulb and a switch, as shown in Fig. 2.3. Now pour some dilute HCl in the beaker and switch on the current. Repeat with dilute sulphuric acid. What do you observe? Repeat the experiment separately with glucose and alcohol solutions. What do you observe now? Does the bulb glow in all cases? The bulb will start glowing in the case of acids, as shown in Fig. 2.3. But you will observe that glucose and alcohol solutions do not conduct electricity. Glowing of the bulb indicates that there is a flow of electric current through the solution. The electric current is carried through the acidic solution by ions. Acids contain H+ ion as cation and anion such as Cl– in HCl, NO3– in HNO3, SO4 2– in H2SO4, CH3COO– in CH3COOH. Since the cation present in acids is H+, this suggests that acids produce hydrogen ions, H+(aq), in solution, which are responsible for their acidic properties. Repeat the same Activity using alkalis such as sodium hydroxide, calcium hydroxide, etc. What can you conclude from the results of this Activity?', 'Acidic solutions such as dilute hydrochloric acid and sulphuric acid conduct electricity, indicated by the glowing of a bulb in an electrical circuit. Non-acidic solutions like glucose and alcohol do not conduct electricity. The conduction in acids is due to the presence of ions, specifically hydrogen ions (H+) as cations and various anions like Cl–, NO3–, SO4 2–, and CH3COO–. These hydrogen ions in aqueous solution are responsible for the acidic properties of acids. The activity suggests testing alkalis to compare their conductivity.', '["acidic solution", "electric current", "hydrogen ion", "ions", "conductivity", "HCl", "H2SO4", "alkalis"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (24, 45, 'Preparation and Properties of Hydrogen Chloride Gas', 'Take about 1g solid NaCl in a clean and dry test tube and set up the apparatus as shown in Fig. 2.4. Add some concentrated sulphuric acid to the test tube. What do you observe? Is there a gas coming out of the delivery tube? Test the gas evolved successively with dry and wet blue litmus paper. In which case does the litmus paper change colour? On the basis of the above Activity, what do you infer about the acidic character of: (i) dry HCl gas (ii) HCl solution?', 'Solid sodium chloride reacts with concentrated sulphuric acid to produce hydrogen chloride gas. The gas evolved can be tested with dry and wet blue litmus paper to observe its acidic properties. The activity helps distinguish the acidic character of dry HCl gas and HCl solution based on their interaction with litmus paper.', '["NaCl", "concentrated sulphuric acid", "HCl gas", "litmus paper", "acidic character"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (25, 51, 'Exothermic Nature of Dissolving Acids and Bases in Water', 'Take 10 mL water in a beaker. Add a few drops of concentrated H2SO4 to it and swirl the beaker slowly. Touch the base of the beaker. Is there a change in temperature? Is this an exothermic or endothermic process? Repeat the above Activity with sodium hydroxide pellets and record your observations.

Warning sign displayed on containers containing concentrated acids and bases.

The process of dissolving an acid or a base in water is a highly exothermic one. Care must be taken while mixing concentrated nitric acid or sulphuric acid with water. The acid must always be added slowly to water with constant stirring. If water is added to a concentrated acid, the heat generated may cause the mixture to splash out and cause burns. The glass container may also break due to excessive local heating. Look out for the warning sign (shown in Fig. 2.5) on the can of concentrated sulphuric acid and on the bottle of sodium hydroxide pellets.

Mixing an acid or base with water results in decrease in the concentration of ions (H3O+/OH–) per unit volume. Such a process is called dilution and the acid or the base is said to be diluted.', 'Dissolving concentrated acids or bases in water is a highly exothermic process that releases heat. To avoid splashing and burns, acids must be added slowly to water with stirring. Dilution occurs when acids or bases are mixed with water, decreasing the concentration of hydronium or hydroxide ions per unit volume.', '["exothermic", "concentrated H2SO4", "sodium hydroxide pellets", "dilution", "hydronium ions", "hydroxide ions", "acid", "base"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (26, 55, 'Testing pH Values of Common Substances Using Universal Indicator', 'Test the pH values of solutions given in Table 2.2. Record your observations. What is the nature of each substance on the basis of your observations?

pH of some common substances shown on a pH paper (colours are only a rough guide) (Fig. 2.7).', 'The activity involves testing the pH of various substances using universal indicator paper and recording observations to determine whether the substances are acidic, neutral, or basic based on their pH values.', '["pH values", "universal indicator", "common substances", "acidic", "neutral", "basic"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (27, 57, 'Determining Soil pH Using Universal Indicator', 'Put about 2 g soil in a test tube and add 5 mL water to it. Shake the contents of the test tube. Filter the contents and collect the filtrate in a test tube. Check the pH of this filtrate with the help of universal indicator paper. What can you conclude about the ideal soil pH for the growth of plants in your region?', 'This activity involves preparing a soil-water mixture, filtering it, and testing the pH of the filtrate using universal indicator paper. The results help determine the soil pH, which is important for understanding the ideal conditions for plant growth in a given region.', '["soil pH", "universal indicator paper", "filtrate", "test tube", "plant growth"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (28, 62, 'Chemical Formulas of Common Salts', 'Write the chemical formulae of the salts given below. Potassium sulphate, sodium sulphate, calcium sulphate, magnesium sulphate, copper sulphate, sodium chloride, sodium nitrate, sodium carbonate and ammonium chloride.', 'This activity asks for the chemical formulas of various common salts including potassium sulphate, sodium sulphate, calcium sulphate, magnesium sulphate, copper sulphate, sodium chloride, sodium nitrate, sodium carbonate, and ammonium chloride. It helps in understanding the composition of these salts.', '["chemical formulae", "potassium sulphate", "sodium sulphate", "calcium sulphate", "magnesium sulphate", "copper sulphate", "sodium chloride", "sodium nitrate", "sodium carbonate", "ammonium chloride"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (43, 106, 'Activity to Explore Properties of Ionic Compounds', 'To learn about the properties of ionic compounds, let us perform the following Activity:', 'This activity is designed to investigate the properties of ionic compounds through practical experimentation. It aims to provide hands-on experience to understand the characteristic features of ionic substances.', '["ionic compounds", "properties", "activity", "experiment"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (30, 76, 'Preparation and Use of Natural Indicators from Beetroot and Other Plants', 'Prepare your own indicator:
- Crush beetroot in a mortar.
- Add sufficient water to obtain the extract.
- Filter the extract by the procedure learnt by you in earlier classes.
- Collect the filtrate to test the substances you may have tasted earlier.
- Arrange four test tubes in a test tube stand and label them as A, B, C and D. Pour 2 mL each of lemon juice solution, soda-water, vinegar and baking soda solution in them respectively.
- Put 2-3 drops of the beetroot extract in each test tube and note the colour change if any. Write your observation in a table.
- You can prepare indicators by using other natural materials like extracts of red cabbage leaves, coloured petals of some flowers such as Petunia, Hydrangea and Geranium.', 'This activity describes the preparation of a natural pH indicator using beetroot extract. The extract is tested with various acidic and basic solutions to observe colour changes. It also suggests other natural sources for indicators, such as red cabbage leaves and flower petals, to demonstrate the concept of indicators in acid-base reactions.', '["beetroot extract", "natural indicator", "acid-base indicator", "colour change", "red cabbage", "Petunia", "Hydrangea", "Geranium"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (31, 76, 'Preparation and Working of a Soda-Acid Fire Extinguisher Using Acid and Sodium Hydrogencarbonate', 'The reaction of acids with metal hydrogencarbonates is used in fire extinguishers which produce carbon dioxide.
- Take 20 mL of sodium hydrogencarbonate (NaHCO3) solution in a wash-bottle.
- Suspend an ignition tube containing dilute sulphuric acid in the wash-bottle (Fig. 2.10).
- Close the mouth of the wash-bottle.
- Tilt the wash-bottle so that the acid from the ignition tube mixes with the sodium hydrogencarbonate solution below.
- You will notice discharge coming out of the nozzle.
- Direct this discharge on a burning candle. What happens?

Figure 2.10 (a) Ignition tube containing dilute sulphuric acid suspended in a wash-bottle containing sodium hydrogencarbonate, (b) Discharge coming out of the nozzle', 'This activity demonstrates the working of a soda-acid fire extinguisher, which operates by the reaction between dilute sulphuric acid and sodium hydrogencarbonate solution. The reaction produces carbon dioxide gas, which is discharged through a nozzle and can extinguish a burning candle flame. The setup involves suspending an acid-containing ignition tube inside a wash-bottle with sodium hydrogencarbonate solution.', '["sodium hydrogencarbonate", "dilute sulphuric acid", "fire extinguisher", "carbon dioxide", "chemical reaction", "discharge", "ignition tube"]'::jsonb, 'intermediate', '2026-09-23 12:20:22.298041+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (32, 79, 'Grouping Substances by Comparing Physical Properties of Metals', 'The easiest way to start grouping substances is by comparing their physical properties. Let us study this with the help of the following activities. For performing Activities 3.1 to 3.6, collect the samples of following metals – iron, copper, aluminium, magnesium, sodium, lead, zinc and any other metal that is easily available.', 'Substances can be grouped by comparing their physical properties. To study this, samples of various metals such as iron, copper, aluminium, magnesium, sodium, lead, and zinc are collected for a series of activities.', '["physical properties", "grouping substances", "metals", "iron", "copper", "aluminium", "magnesium", "sodium", "lead", "zinc"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (33, 79, 'Metallic Lustre and Surface Appearance of Metals', 'Take samples of iron, copper, aluminium and magnesium. Note the appearance of each sample. Clean the surface of each sample by rubbing them with sand paper and note their appearance again. Metals, in their pure state, have a shining surface. This property is called metallic lustre.', 'Samples of iron, copper, aluminium, and magnesium show distinct appearances. When their surfaces are cleaned by rubbing with sand paper, their shining surfaces become more evident. This shining surface characteristic of pure metals is known as metallic lustre.', '["iron", "copper", "aluminium", "magnesium", "metallic lustre", "surface appearance", "sand paper"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (34, 79, 'Observing the Malleability and Hardness of Various Metals', 'Take small pieces of iron, copper, aluminium, and magnesium. Try to cut these metals with a sharp knife and note your observations.
Hold a piece of sodium metal with a pair of tongs. CAUTION: Always handle sodium metal with care. Dry it by pressing between the folds of a filter paper.
Put it on a watch-glass and try to cut it with a knife.
What do you observe?
You will find that some metals can be beaten into thin sheets. This property is called malleability. Did you know that gold and silver are the most malleable metals?
Take pieces of iron, zinc, lead and copper.
Place any one metal on a block of iron and strike it four or five times with a hammer. What do you observe?
Repeat with other metals.
Record the change in the shape of these metals. You will find that metals are generally hard. The hardness varies from metal to metal.', 'This activity involves testing the malleability and hardness of various metals such as iron, copper, aluminium, magnesium, zinc, lead, and sodium. Malleability is demonstrated by the ability to beat metals into thin sheets, with gold and silver being the most malleable. Hardness is observed by striking metals with a hammer and noting changes in shape, showing that hardness varies among different metals.', '["malleability", "hardness", "iron", "copper", "aluminium", "magnesium", "sodium", "zinc", "lead"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (35, 83, 'Demonstration of Metals as Good Conductors of Heat', 'Take an aluminium or copper wire. Clamp this wire on a stand, as shown in Fig. 3.1. Fix a pin to the free end of the wire using wax. Heat the wire with a spirit lamp, candle or a burner near the place where it is clamped. What do you observe after some time? Note your observations. Does the metal wire melt? The above activity shows that metals are good conductors of heat and have high melting points. The best conductors of heat are silver and copper. Lead and mercury are comparatively poor conductors of heat. Do metals also conduct electricity? Let us find out.', 'Heating a metal wire such as aluminium or copper shows that metals conduct heat efficiently without melting easily, indicating their high melting points. Silver and copper are the best heat conductors, while lead and mercury conduct heat poorly. This activity introduces the concept of metals as good conductors of heat and raises the question of their electrical conductivity.', '["aluminium wire", "copper wire", "heat conduction", "melting point", "silver", "lead", "mercury", "metal wire"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (36, 85, 'Investigation of Electrical Conductivity in Metals and Non-Metals', 'Do non-metals also have physical properties similar to that of metals? Let us find out.

Activity 3.6

Set up an electric circuit as shown in Fig. 3.2.
Place the metal to be tested in the circuit between terminals A and B as shown.
Does the bulb glow? What does this indicate?

Metals are good conductors of electricity.', 'An activity to test the electrical conductivity of metals involves setting up a circuit with a metal placed between two terminals. If the bulb glows, it indicates that the metal conducts electricity well. This demonstrates that metals are good conductors of electricity.', '["electrical conductivity", "metal", "electric circuit", "conductors", "bulb", "terminals"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (37, 86, 'Investigation of Chemical Properties of Magnesium and Sulphur', 'Take a magnesium ribbon and some sulphur powder.
Burn the magnesium ribbon. Collect the ashes formed and dissolve them in water.
Test the resultant solution with both red and blue litmus paper.
Is the product formed on burning magnesium acidic or basic?
Now burn sulphur powder. Place a test tube over the burning sulphur to collect the fumes produced.
Add some water to the above test tube and shake.
Test this solution with blue and red litmus paper.
Is the product formed on burning sulphur acidic or basic?
Can you write equations for these reactions?', 'Magnesium ribbon is burned to form ashes which are dissolved in water and tested with red and blue litmus papers to determine if the product is acidic or basic. Sulphur powder is burned, and the fumes collected in a test tube are dissolved in water and similarly tested with litmus papers. The activity encourages writing chemical equations for these reactions.', '["magnesium ribbon", "sulphur powder", "burning", "litmus paper", "acidic", "basic", "chemical reactions"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (38, 89, 'Reaction of Metals with Air: Burning of Metals', 'Metals and Non-metals 413.2.1 What happens when Metals are burnt in Air? You have seen in Activity 3.8 that magnesium burns in air with a dazzling white flame. Do all metals react in the same manner? Let us check by performing the following Activity.', 'This section introduces the activity of burning metals in air to observe their reactions. It notes that magnesium burns with a dazzling white flame and questions whether all metals behave similarly when burnt in air.', '["burning", "metals", "magnesium", "reaction with air", "flame"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (39, 93, 'Activity to Observe Reactivity of Metals by Burning and Product Analysis', 'CAUTION: The following activity needs the teacher’s assistance. It would be better if students wear eye protection.
■ Hold any of the samples taken above with a pair of tongs and try burning over a flame. Repeat with the other metal samples.
■ Collect the product if formed.
■ Let the products and the metal surface cool down.
■ Which metals burn easily?
■ What flame colour did you observe when the metal burnt?
■ How does the metal surface appear after burning?
■ Arrange the metals in the decreasing order of their reactivity towards oxygen.
■ Are the products soluble in water?
After performing Activity 3.9, you must have observed that sodium is the most reactive of the samples of metals taken here. The reaction of magnesium is less vigorous implying that it is not as reactive as sodium. But burning in oxygen does not help us to decide about the reactivity of zinc, iron, copper or lead. Let us see some more reactions to arrive at a conclusion about the order of reactivity of these metals.', 'The activity involves burning various metal samples to observe their reactivity with oxygen, noting flame color, ease of burning, and appearance of the metal surface after burning. Students are asked to arrange metals by decreasing reactivity and check solubility of the products. Sodium is observed as the most reactive, magnesium less so, but burning alone is insufficient to determine reactivity order for zinc, iron, copper, or lead.', '["activity", "metal burning", "reactivity", "flame colour", "metal oxides", "solubility"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (40, 93, 'Activity to Investigate Reactivity of Metals with Water', 'CAUTION: This Activity needs the teacher’s assistance.
Collect the samples of the same metals as in Activity 3.9.
Put small pieces of the samples separately in beakers half-filled with cold water.
Which metals reacted with cold water? Arrange them in the increasing order of their reactivity with cold water.
Did any metal produce fire on water?
Does any metal start floating after some time?
Put the metals that did not react with cold water in beakers half-filled with hot water.
For the metals that did not react with hot water, arrange the apparatus as shown in Fig. 3.3 and observe their reaction with steam.
Which metals did not react even with steam?
Arrange the metals in the decreasing order of reactivity with water.', 'This activity guides the investigation of the reactivity of various metals with cold water, hot water, and steam. It involves observing which metals react, produce fire, or float due to hydrogen bubbles. The activity also includes arranging metals in order of their reactivity based on these observations.', '["metals", "cold water", "hot water", "steam", "reactivity", "hydrogen gas", "floating metals"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (41, 96, 'Reactivity of Metals with Dilute Hydrochloric Acid', 'Collect all the metal samples except sodium and potassium again. If the samples are tarnished, rub them clean with sand paper. CAUTION: Do not take sodium and potassium as they react vigorously even with cold water. Put the samples separately in test tubes containing dilute hydrochloric acid. Suspend thermometers in the test tubes, so that their bulbs are dipped in the acid. Observe the rate of formation of bubbles carefully. Which metals reacted vigorously with dilute hydrochloric acid? With which metal did you record the highest temperature? Arrange the metals in the decreasing order of reactivity with dilute acids. Write equations for the reactions of magnesium, aluminium, zinc and iron with dilute hydrochloric acid.', 'Metal samples, excluding sodium and potassium due to their vigorous reaction with water, are cleaned and placed in dilute hydrochloric acid to observe their reactivity. The rate of bubble formation and temperature changes indicate the reactivity order of the metals. Magnesium shows the fastest reaction and highest temperature increase, followed by aluminium, zinc, and iron. Copper does not react with dilute hydrochloric acid, as no bubbles or temperature change are observed.', '["metal samples", "dilute hydrochloric acid", "reactivity", "bubble formation", "temperature", "magnesium", "aluminium", "zinc", "iron", "copper"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (42, 100, 'Displacement Reactions Demonstrating Reactivity of Metals', 'Take a clean wire of copper and an iron nail. Put the copper wire in a solution of iron sulphate and the iron nail in a solution of copper sulphate taken in test tubes (Fig. 3.4). Record your observations after 20 minutes.
In which test tube did you find that a reaction has occurred? On what basis can you say that a reaction has actually taken place? Can you correlate your observations for the Activities 3.9, 3.10 and 3.11? Write a balanced chemical equation for the reaction that has taken place. Name the type of reaction.
Reactive metals can displace less reactive metals from their compounds in solution or molten form. We have seen in the previous sections that all metals are not equally reactive. We checked the reactivity of various metals with oxygen, water and acids. But all metals do not react with these reagents. So we were not able to put all the metal samples we had collected in decreasing order of their reactivity. Displacement reactions studied in Chapter 1 give better evidence about the reactivity of metals. It is simple and easy: if metal A displaces metal B from its solution, it is more reactive than B.
Metal A + Salt solution of B → Salt solution of A + Metal B
Which metal, copper or iron, is more reactive according to your observations in Activity 3.12?', 'This activity involves placing a copper wire in iron sulphate solution and an iron nail in copper sulphate solution to observe displacement reactions. A reaction occurs if a more reactive metal displaces a less reactive metal from its salt solution. The activity helps determine which metal, copper or iron, is more reactive based on the observed displacement. Displacement reactions provide evidence of relative reactivity among metals, as more reactive metals replace less reactive metals in their compounds.', '["copper wire", "iron nail", "iron sulphate", "copper sulphate", "displacement reaction", "reactivity of metals", "salt solution"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (44, 106, 'Investigation of Physical and Chemical Properties of Salt Samples', 'Take samples of sodium chloride, potassium iodide, barium chloride or any other salt from the science laboratory. What is the physical state of these salts? Take a small amount of a sample on a metal spatula and heat directly on the flame (Fig. 3.7). Repeat with other samples. What did you observe? Did the samples impart any colour to the flame? Do these compounds melt? Try to dissolve the samples in water, petrol and kerosene. Are they soluble? Make a circuit as shown in Fig. 3.8 and insert the electrodes into a solution of one salt. What did you observe? Test the other salt samples too in this manner. What is your inference about the nature of these compounds?', 'Samples of various salts such as sodium chloride, potassium iodide, and barium chloride are tested for their physical state, flame colour, melting behavior, solubility in different solvents, and electrical conductivity in solution. Observations include whether the salts melt, impart flame color, dissolve in water or organic solvents, and conduct electricity when dissolved.', '["sodium chloride", "potassium iodide", "barium chloride", "flame test", "solubility", "electrical conductivity", "salt samples"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (45, 111, 'Investigation of Conditions Required for Iron to Rust', 'Take three test tubes and place clean iron nails in each of them. Label these test tubes A, B and C. Pour some water in test tube A and cork it. Pour boiled distilled water in test tube B, add about 1 mL of oil and cork it. The oil will float on water and prevent the air from dissolving in the water. Put some anhydrous calcium chloride in test tube C and cork it. Anhydrous calcium chloride will absorb the moisture, if any, from the air. Leave these test tubes for a few days and then observe (Fig. 3.13).

Investigating the conditions under which iron rusts. In tube A, both air and water are present. In tube B, there is no air dissolved in the water. In tube C, the air is dry. You will observe that iron nails rust in test tube A, but they do not rust in test tubes B and C. In the test tube A, the nails are exposed to both air and water. In the test tube B, the nails are exposed to only water, and the nails in test tube C are exposed to dry air. What does this tell us about the conditions under which iron articles rust?', 'Three test tubes containing iron nails are set up under different conditions: tube A with water and air, tube B with boiled distilled water and oil to exclude air, and tube C with dry air using anhydrous calcium chloride. After a few days, rust forms only in tube A where both air and water are present. This demonstrates that both moisture and air are necessary for iron to rust.', '["iron nails", "rust", "test tubes", "boiled distilled water", "anhydrous calcium chloride", "air", "water", "oil"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:22:30.534548+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (46, 122, 'Procedure to Trace Light Path Through a Glass Prism', 'Fix a sheet of white paper on a drawing board using drawing pins. Place a glass prism on it in such a way that it rests on its triangular base. Trace the outline of the prism using a pencil. Draw a straight line PE inclined to one of the refracting surfaces, say AB, of the prism. Fix two pins, say at points P and Q, on the line PE as shown in Fig. 10.4. Look for the images of the pins, fixed at P and Q, through the other face AC. Fix two more pins, at points R and S, such that the pins at R and S and the images of the pins at P and Q lie on the same straight line. Remove the pins and the glass prism. The line PE meets the boundary of the prism at point E (see Fig. 10.4). Similarly, join and produce the points R and S. Let these lines meet the boundary of the prism at E and F, respectively. Join E and F. Draw perpendiculars to the refracting surfaces AB and AC of the prism at points E and F, respectively. Mark the angle of incidence (∠i), the angle of refraction (∠r) and the angle of emergence (∠e) as shown in Fig. 10.4.', 'This activity describes how to trace the path of a light ray through a glass prism using pins and a white paper. By placing pins along the incident ray and observing their images through the prism, the refracted and emergent rays can be located. The angles of incidence, refraction, and emergence are marked by drawing perpendiculars to the prism surfaces at the points where the light enters and exits.', '["glass prism", "incident ray", "refracting surfaces", "angle of incidence", "angle of refraction", "angle of emergence", "pins", "light path"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (47, 122, 'Observation of Dispersion of White Light Through a Prism', 'Take a thick sheet of cardboard and make a small hole or narrow slit in its middle. Allow sunlight to fall on the narrow slit. This gives a narrow beam of white light. Now, take a glass prism and allow the light from the slit to fall on one of its faces as shown in Fig. 10.5. Turn the prism slowly until the light that comes out of it appears on a nearby screen. What do you observe? You will find a beautiful band of colours. Why does this happen? Here PE is the incident ray, EF is the refracted ray and FS is the emergent ray. You may note that a ray of light is entering from air to glass at the first surface AB. The light ray on refraction has bent towards the normal. At the second surface AC, the light ray has entered from glass to air. Hence it has bent away from normal. Compare the angle of incidence and the angle of refraction at each refracting surface of the prism. Is this similar to the kind of bending that occurs in a glass slab? The peculiar shape of the prism makes the emergent ray bend at an angle to the direction of the incident ray. This angle is called the angle of deviation. In this case ∠D is the angle of deviation. Mark the angle of deviation in the above activity and measure it.', 'This activity demonstrates the dispersion of white light by passing a narrow beam of sunlight through a glass prism. The light refracts at the prism surfaces, bending towards the normal when entering glass and away from the normal when exiting to air. The prism''s shape causes the emergent ray to deviate from the incident ray by an angle called the angle of deviation. The dispersion produces a spectrum of colors visible on a screen.', '["dispersion", "white light", "glass prism", "angle of deviation", "refraction", "incident ray", "emergent ray", "narrow slit"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:24:19.999707+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (48, 138, 'Exploring the Relationship Between Potential Difference and Current', 'Is there a relationship between the potential difference across a conductor and the current through it? Let us explore with an Activity.', 'This activity introduces the investigation of the relationship between the potential difference across a conductor and the current flowing through it. It sets the stage for experimental exploration of this fundamental electrical concept.', '["potential difference", "current", "conductor", "relationship", "activity"]'::jsonb, 'foundational', '2026-09-23 12:25:35.584882+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (65, 205, 'Research on Chemicals Responsible for Ozone Layer Depletion and Effectiveness of Regulations', 'Find out from the library, internet or newspaper reports, which chemicals are responsible for the depletion of the ozone layer. Find out if the regulations put in place to control the emission of these chemicals have succeeded in reducing the damage to the ozone layer. Has the size of the hole in the ozone layer changed in recent years?', 'Students are tasked with researching the chemicals that cause ozone layer depletion and investigating whether regulations controlling these emissions have been effective. They are also asked to determine if the size of the ozone hole has changed in recent years.', '["Ozone layer depletion", "Chemicals", "Regulations", "Emission control", "Ozone hole", "Research"]'::jsonb, 'intermediate', '2026-09-23 12:28:50.705622+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO activities ("id", "topic_id", "title", "content", "summary", "keywords", "difficulty_level", "created_at") VALUES (49, 138, 'Experimental Verification of Ohm’s Law Using a Nichrome Wire', 'Set up a circuit as shown in Fig. 11.2, consisting of a nichrome wire XY of length, say 0.5 m, an ammeter, a voltmeter and four cells of 1.5 V each. (Nichrome is an alloy of nickel, chromium, manganese, and iron metals.) First use only one cell as the source in the circuit. Note the reading in the ammeter I, for the current and reading of the voltmeter V for the potential difference across the nichrome wire XY in the circuit. Tabulate them in the Table given. Next connect two cells in the circuit and note the respective readings of the ammeter and voltmeter for the values of current through the nichrome wire and potential difference across the nichrome wire. Repeat the above steps using three cells and then four cells in the circuit separately. Calculate the ratio of V to I for each pair of potential difference V and current I.

S. Number of cells Current through Potential difference V/I
No. used in the the nichrome across the (volt/ampere)
circuit wire, I nichrome
(ampere) wire,  V (volt)
1 1
2 2
